Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "articulation_use_dissociation", "Title": "Say vs. Use: A Causal Double Dissociation Between the Knowledge LLMs Articulate and the Knowledge They Act On", "Short Hypothesis": "The knowledge an LLM can verbally articulate and the knowledge it actually uses to make an implicit downstream decision are carried by partially distinct internal representations, producing a causal double dissociation: (A) facts the model states correctly but does not use ('inert articulation'), and (B) facts the model uses correctly in a downstream computation but cannot state ('tacit knowledge'). We hypothesize these two failure directions have distinct, separable internal loci, such that steering the 'articulation' locus changes what the model says without changing what it uses, and steering the 'use' locus does the reverse.", "Related Work": "Prior work establishes an acquisition-vs-utilization gap (Kandpal/Chen 2023; Physics of LMs 3.2, 2023), a 'know but don't tell' disconnect in long context (2024), missing-vs-unused knowledge in patents (2025), knowledge-reasoning dissociation in clinical NLI (2025), and comprehension-without-competence 'split-brain' framing (2025). Self-explanation-faithfulness work (NeuroFaith 2025) checks whether stated reasoning matches internal computation. All of these (i) treat the gap as unidirectional (model has knowledge but fails to apply/reason), (ii) are largely behavioral or measure a single direction, and (iii) do not test whether articulation and use are *causally separable representations*. Our contribution is distinct: we posit and test a *bidirectional* dissociation including the under-studied 'tacit knowledge' direction (used-but-unsayable), and we make it a *causal* claim — that separate internal loci carry the say-pathway and the use-pathway — validated by targeted, direction-specific interventions. This is orthogonal to my prior proposals, which all concern retrieval-to-output for direct factual queries; here the object is the mismatch between verbal articulation and implicit use of the same underlying fact.", "Abstract": "Large language models are known to 'possess' knowledge they fail to 'use', but the relationship between what a model can say and what it actually acts on remains unclear. We propose that articulation and use draw on partially distinct internal representations, and that this predicts a causal double dissociation. For a fixed fact f about a subject, we build two probes: an articulation query that asks the model to state f directly, and a use query that requires f only implicitly to succeed at a downstream task (e.g., choosing between two options where f is a hidden discriminator, or completing a task whose correct answer depends on f but never names it). We first document the behavioral double dissociation: inert-articulation facts (stated correctly, not used) and tacit-knowledge facts (used correctly, cannot be stated), quantifying their prevalence above matched baselines. We then test the mechanistic claim that a 'say' locus and a 'use' locus are separable: using linear probing we identify residual-stream directions predicting articulation success and use success, and with activation patching and steering we test direction-specificity — amplifying the say-locus improves articulation without changing use, and amplifying the use-locus improves task success without changing what the model states. We further test whether tacit-knowledge facts can be surfaced by patching from the use-pathway into the articulation-pathway. Experiments span GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B on PopQA, T-REx, and comparison/classification tasks derived from Physics-of-LMs manipulation setups, evaluating dissociation prevalence, probe separability, and direction-specific causal effects with strict specificity controls. Confirming the hypothesis reframes the utilization gap as a representational split between saying and doing, with implications for interpretability-based oversight (a model may use knowledge it never verbalizes).", "Experiments": ["1. Paired say/use task construction: For each fact f (subject-relation-object) from PopQA/T-REx, build (a) an articulation query ('What is X's {relation}?') and (b) a use query in which f is only an implicit discriminator — following Physics-of-LMs manipulation formats: a comparison ('Which of A or B is larger in {attribute}?' where knowing f resolves it) or a classification ('Is X's {attribute} even/odd/before-2000?') whose correct answer depends on f but never states it. Validate that the use query is unsolvable without f (ablate f via a matched query about an unknown subject; correct rate should drop to chance).", "2. Behavioral double-dissociation prevalence: For each model, cross-tabulate per-fact articulation success x use success into four cells. Quantify prevalence of the two off-diagonal cells — inert articulation (say-correct, use-wrong) and tacit knowledge (use-correct, say-wrong) — against a null that assumes a single shared knowledge signal with independent per-task noise (fit per-fact difficulty and per-task bias via logistic regression; test residual say x use interaction). Report per-relation breakdowns.", "3. Storage/leakage controls for tacit knowledge: For tacit-knowledge facts (used but not stated), confirm the model is genuinely using f (not a task shortcut) by (i) counterfactually flipping f in the prompt context and checking the use-answer flips, and (ii) verifying the use query fails when f is made indeterminate. For inert-articulation facts, confirm articulation is genuine (consistent across paraphrases) not lucky guessing.", "4. Probe separability: Train two linear probes on the last-token/subject-token residual stream at each layer: a say-probe (predicting articulation success / the stated answer) and a use-probe (predicting use-query success). Measure per-layer AUROC, the layers at which each becomes decodable, and — via residualization — whether each carries information about its own task not shared with the other. Label-shuffle controls.", "5. Direction-specific causal test via steering/patching: Derive a say-direction (mean activation difference between say-correct and say-wrong matched states) and a use-direction (mean difference between use-correct and use-wrong states). Test specificity: (a) amplify say-direction on inert-articulation cases' failing-use runs — hypothesis: articulation unchanged/strong, use largely unchanged; (b) amplify use-direction — hypothesis: use improves without changing what the model states; (c) cross-controls with the opposite direction and a random direction. Metrics: per-task effect sizes and a specificity index (on-target change minus off-target change).", "6. Surfacing tacit knowledge via cross-pathway patching: On tacit-knowledge facts, patch the use-pathway representation (from the use-query run where f is demonstrably used) into the articulation run at identified layers/positions, and test whether the model now states f. Compare against say-direction amplification and ROME editing. Metrics: articulation recovery rate, specificity (200 unrelated facts pre/post), fluency (held-out perplexity).", "7. Transfer and generality: Test whether say/use directions generalize across relations (train on a subset, apply to held-out relations) and repeat core analyses (2,4,5) on GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B for architecture-generality. Report whether larger models show a smaller dissociation.", "8. Task-format robustness: Vary the use-query format (comparison vs classification vs implicit completion) to confirm the dissociation is about say-vs-use rather than a specific task format, controlling for use-task difficulty."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["The dissociation could be an artifact of use-query difficulty (extra task demands) rather than a representational say/use split; the single-signal null baseline (Exp 2), format-robustness check (Exp 8), and counterfactual controls (Exp 3) are essential and a residual interaction after controls is the decisive behavioral evidence.", "Say and use representations may be entangled rather than cleanly separable; the direction-specific steering test (Exp 5), not probe onset alone, is decisive, and a graded/partial dissociation would weaken the strong claim but remain informative.", "Establishing that tacit-knowledge facts are genuinely 'used' (not solved by a shortcut) is hard; counterfactual flipping and indeterminacy controls mitigate but may not fully eliminate confounds, and coverage will be limited.", "Cross-pathway patching to surface tacit knowledge may induce confident confabulation on facts not actually held; strict specificity and fluency controls are required and utility may be capped.", "A single transferable say/use direction may not exist if the split is fact-specific, limiting generality though still scientifically informative.", "Multi-token answers complicate probe/logit-lens readout; primary analysis restricts to single-token-first answers with reported coverage.", "Findings on 6-8B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget."]}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'articulation_use_dissociation', 'Title': 'Say vs. Use: A Causal Double Dissociation Between the Knowledge LLMs Articulate and the Knowledge They Act On', 'Short Hypothesis': "The knowledge an LLM can verbally articulate and the knowledge it actually uses to make an implicit downstream decision are carried by partially distinct internal representations, producing a causal double dissociation: (A) facts the model states correctly but does not use ('inert articulation'), and (B) facts the model uses correctly in a downstream computation but cannot state ('tacit knowledge'). We hypothesize these two failure directions have distinct, separable internal loci, such that steering the 'articulation' locus changes what the model says without changing what it uses, and steering the 'use' locus does the reverse.", 'Related Work': "Prior work establishes an acquisition-vs-utilization gap (Kandpal/Chen 2023; Physics of LMs 3.2, 2023), a 'know but don't tell' disconnect in long context (2024), missing-vs-unused knowledge in patents (2025), knowledge-reasoning dissociation in clinical NLI (2025), and comprehension-without-competence 'split-brain' framing (2025). Self-explanation-faithfulness work (NeuroFaith 2025) checks whether stated reasoning matches internal computation. All of these (i) treat the gap as unidirectional (model has knowledge but fails to apply/reason), (ii) are largely behavioral or measure a single direction, and (iii) do not test whether articulation and use are *causally separable representations*. Our contribution is distinct: we posit and test a *bidirectional* dissociation including the under-studied 'tacit knowledge' direction (used-but-unsayable), and we make it a *causal* claim — that separate internal loci carry the say-pathway and the use-pathway — validated by targeted, direction-specific interventions. This is orthogonal to my prior proposals, which all concern retrieval-to-output for direct factual queries; here the object is the mismatch between verbal articulation and implicit use of the same underlying fact.", 'Abstract': "Large language models are known to 'possess' knowledge they fail to 'use', but the relationship between what a model can say and what it actually acts on remains unclear. We propose that articulation and use draw on partially distinct internal representations, and that this predicts a causal double dissociation. For a fixed fact f about a subject, we build two probes: an articulation query that asks the model to state f directly, and a use query that requires f only implicitly to succeed at a downstream task (e.g., choosing between two options where f is a hidden discriminator, or completing a task whose correct answer depends on f but never names it). We first document the behavioral double dissociation: inert-articulation facts (stated correctly, not used) and tacit-knowledge facts (used correctly, cannot be stated), quantifying their prevalence above matched baselines. We then test the mechanistic claim that a 'say' locus and a 'use' locus are separable: using linear probing we identify residual-stream directions predicting articulation success and use success, and with activation patching and steering we test direction-specificity — amplifying the say-locus improves articulation without changing use, and amplifying the use-locus improves task success without changing what the model states. We further test whether tacit-knowledge facts can be surfaced by patching from the use-pathway into the articulation-pathway. Experiments span GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B on PopQA, T-REx, and comparison/classification tasks derived from Physics-of-LMs manipulation setups, evaluating dissociation prevalence, probe separability, and direction-specific causal effects with strict specificity controls. Confirming the hypothesis reframes the utilization gap as a representational split between saying and doing, with implications for interpretability-based oversight (a model may use knowledge it never verbalizes).", 'Experiments': ["1. Paired say/use task construction: For each fact f (subject-relation-object) from PopQA/T-REx, build (a) an articulation query ('What is X's {relation}?') and (b) a use query in which f is only an implicit discriminator — following Physics-of-LMs manipulation formats: a comparison ('Which of A or B is larger in {attribute}?' where knowing f resolves it) or a classification ('Is X's {attribute} even/odd/before-2000?') whose correct answer depends on f but never states it. Validate that the use query is unsolvable without f (ablate f via a matched query about an unknown subject; correct rate should drop to chance).", '2. Behavioral double-dissociation prevalence: For each model, cross-tabulate per-fact articulation success x use success into four cells. Quantify prevalence of the two off-diagonal cells — inert articulation (say-correct, use-wrong) and tacit knowledge (use-correct, say-wrong) — against a null that assumes a single shared knowledge signal with independent per-task noise (fit per-fact difficulty and per-task bias via logistic regression; test residual say x use interaction). Report per-relation breakdowns.', '3. Storage/leakage controls for tacit knowledge: For tacit-knowledge facts (used but not stated), confirm the model is genuinely using f (not a task shortcut) by (i) counterfactually flipping f in the prompt context and checking the use-answer flips, and (ii) verifying the use query fails when f is made indeterminate. For inert-articulation facts, confirm articulation is genuine (consistent across paraphrases) not lucky guessing.', '4. Probe separability: Train two linear probes on the last-token/subject-token residual stream at each layer: a say-probe (predicting articulation success / the stated answer) and a use-probe (predicting use-query success). Measure per-layer AUROC, the layers at which each becomes decodable, and — via residualization — whether each carries information about its own task not shared with the other. Label-shuffle controls.', "5. Direction-specific causal test via steering/patching: Derive a say-direction (mean activation difference between say-correct and say-wrong matched states) and a use-direction (mean difference between use-correct and use-wrong states). Test specificity: (a) amplify say-direction on inert-articulation cases' failing-use runs — hypothesis: articulation unchanged/strong, use largely unchanged; (b) amplify use-direction — hypothesis: use improves without changing what the model states; (c) cross-controls with the opposite direction and a random direction. Metrics: per-task effect sizes and a specificity index (on-target change minus off-target change).", '6. Surfacing tacit knowledge via cross-pathway patching: On tacit-knowledge facts, patch the use-pathway representation (from the use-query run where f is demonstrably used) into the articulation run at identified layers/positions, and test whether the model now states f. Compare against say-direction amplification and ROME editing. Metrics: articulation recovery rate, specificity (200 unrelated facts pre/post), fluency (held-out perplexity).', '7. Transfer and generality: Test whether say/use directions generalize across relations (train on a subset, apply to held-out relations) and repeat core analyses (2,4,5) on GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B for architecture-generality. Report whether larger models show a smaller dissociation.', '8. Task-format robustness: Vary the use-query format (comparison vs classification vs implicit completion) to confirm the dissociation is about say-vs-use rather than a specific task format, controlling for use-task difficulty.'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['The dissociation could be an artifact of use-query difficulty (extra task demands) rather than a representational say/use split; the single-signal null baseline (Exp 2), format-robustness check (Exp 8), and counterfactual controls (Exp 3) are essential and a residual interaction after controls is the decisive behavioral evidence.', 'Say and use representations may be entangled rather than cleanly separable; the direction-specific steering test (Exp 5), not probe onset alone, is decisive, and a graded/partial dissociation would weaken the strong claim but remain informative.', "Establishing that tacit-knowledge facts are genuinely 'used' (not solved by a shortcut) is hard; counterfactual flipping and indeterminacy controls mitigate but may not fully eliminate confounds, and coverage will be limited.", 'Cross-pathway patching to surface tacit knowledge may induce confident confabulation on facts not actually held; strict specificity and fluency controls are required and utility may be capped.', 'A single transferable say/use direction may not exist if the split is fact-specific, limiting generality though still scientifically informative.', 'Multi-token answers complicate probe/logit-lens readout; primary analysis restricts to single-token-first answers with reported coverage.', 'Findings on 6-8B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget.']}

