Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "late_polarity_binding",
  "Title": "Content First, Polarity Last: Why LLMs Retrieve the Fact but Ignore Whether the Query Denied It",
  "Short Hypothesis": "When answering factual queries, LLMs retrieve the subject-attribute content association early and largely polarity-blind, then apply the query's polarity (affirmation vs. negation) in a separate, later, weaker step. Because content is bound before polarity, a stored true negation is frequently overridden by the positively-retrieved content, producing confident affirmation of a fact the model 'knows' is false. We predict content-recall and polarity-application are decodable at different layers, that negation-override errors concentrate where positive content is strongly primed, and that amplifying/binding the late polarity signal (not editing the fact) selectively fixes these errors while preserving affirmative recall.",
  "Related Work": "Behavioral work shows a 'belief conflict': LLMs answer polar negative questions correctly but generate positively-biased text about negative commonsense (Say What You Mean, 2023), attributing this to reporting bias and statistical shortcuts, and negated-LAMA studies (Understanding by Understanding Not, 2021; Kassner & Schutze) show models complete negated cloze the same as affirmative. Strong-hallucinations-from-negation (2024) treats negation theoretically as a latent operation and proposes a fix, but does not localize a mechanism. INLP work (2020) shows negation leaves a linear imprint. Entity-recognition (Do I Know This Entity, 2024) and answerability directions (2023) concern coverage/self-knowledge, not the polarity of a specific factual query. None of these localize a *content-before-polarity* two-stage retrieval mechanism, test polarity-application timing causally, or show that a targeted intervention on a late polarity signal recovers stored true-negation knowledge. This is orthogonal to all my prior proposals (gating, format copies, entity competition, route arbitration, familiarity, property collapse, lexicalization, overregularization, temporal anchors, consistency seams, inheritance, say-vs-use): here the object is the *polarity* of the query and its late binding to polarity-blind content.",
  "Abstract": "Large language models often affirm a fact even when the query negates it, and generate positively-biased statements about things they can correctly deny when asked yes/no questions\u2014the 'belief conflict.' Prior work documents this behaviorally and blames pretraining reporting bias, but offers no mechanistic account. We propose that factual retrieval is two-stage and polarity-blind at its core: the model retrieves the subject-attribute content association early, then applies the query's polarity (affirm vs. negate) in a separate, later, and comparatively weak step. Because content is bound before polarity is resolved, strongly-primed positive content overrides a stored true negation, yielding confident wrong-polarity answers. We test three claims. (1) Timing/dissociability: a content probe (predicting the retrieved subject-attribute association, polarity-invariant) and a polarity probe (predicting the query's affirmative/negative frame) are separable residual-stream directions decodable at different layers, with content emerging before polarity is applied to the answer. (2) Prevalence/geometry: negation-override errors\u2014correct polar-QA answer but wrong-polarity generation, or affirmation of a negated cloze\u2014concentrate where positive content is strongly primed (high subject-attribute association), above frequency-matched baselines, and the strength imbalance between content and polarity signals predicts override on held-out items. (3) Causal intervention: amplifying the late polarity direction (or patching it from a correct-polarity run) recovers correct-polarity answers with higher specificity and less collateral damage than fact editing, while preserving affirmative recall. Experiments span GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B on negated-LAMA, T-REx, and negative-commonsense sets (CSK-PN), evaluating probe separability, layer-onset ordering, override prevalence, causal recovery, and specificity.",
  "Experiments": [
    "1. Polarity-paired dataset construction: From LAMA/T-REx, PopQA, and negative-commonsense sets (CSK-PN / Say-What-You-Mean data), build minimally-contrasting affirmative/negative pairs for each fact: an affirmative cloze/question ('The capital of France is ___' / 'Is a lion an ocean animal?') and its negation ('The capital of France is not ___' / true-negation statements). Include a matched polar-QA form to establish the model's 'true' polarity knowledge. Restrict to single-token-first answers; report coverage.",
    "2. Content-vs-polarity probe construction and layer-onset test: Train two linear probes on the last-token/subject-token residual stream at every layer\u2014a content probe (target: the polarity-invariant subject-attribute association, i.e., predict the fact's object regardless of query polarity, trained across both affirmative and negative forms) and a polarity probe (target: whether the query is affirmative or negative). Metrics: per-layer AUROC/accuracy for each; onset-layer difference (does content become decodable, and does the answer reflect content, before the polarity signal is applied?) with bootstrap CIs; label-shuffle controls; residualization to confirm non-redundant information.",
    "3. Negation-override error taxonomy and prevalence: Classify responses on negated/negative-fact queries into (a) negation-override (model produces the affirmative/positive-content answer while the correct answer is the negation, and the model's polar-QA form shows it 'knows' the true polarity), (b) genuine ignorance (polar-QA also wrong), (c) correct. Test whether override concentrates where positive content is strongly primed\u2014measured by affirmative-form answer confidence / subject-attribute association strength\u2014versus a null controlling for answer frequency and lexical similarity (logistic regression; chi-square). Test whether the content-vs-polarity signal-strength imbalance (from Exp 2) predicts override on held-out items (AUROC).",
    "4. Causal timing/decomposition via patching: (a) Patch the polarity representation from a correct-polarity run into an override-failure run at the identified polarity-onset layers and test whether the answer flips to correct polarity while content stays fixed. (b) Ablate the polarity signal in a correct affirmative run and test whether it induces spurious negation errors (reverse control). (c) Patch content alone and confirm it does not fix polarity. These dissociate whether the failure is late/weak polarity application rather than missing content, and establish the two-stage timing causally.",
    "5. Polarity-amplification recovery intervention: Learn a low-rank polarity steering vector (mean activation difference between correct-polarity and override states) and apply it on held-out negation-override failures. Metrics: recovery rate; specificity\u2014accuracy on (i) 200 unrelated affirmative facts and (ii) a held-out set of correctly-answered affirmative queries (which must remain correct, a critical control since we don't want to make the model spuriously negate) pre/post; fluency (held-out perplexity); recovered-per-unit-collateral-damage. Compare against content-direction manipulation, ROME/MEMIT editing of the fact, the Strong-Hallucinations \u039b method, and a random-direction control.",
    "6. Transfer and generality: Test whether a single polarity steering vector generalizes across relations and across both factual and commonsense negation (train on a subset, apply to held-out relations/domains); repeat core analyses (2\u20135) on GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B for architecture-generality. Report whether larger models show earlier/stronger polarity binding (smaller override rate).",
    "7. Source analysis: Regress override rate on subject-attribute association strength, affirmative-vs-negative pretraining-frequency asymmetry (using entity popularity and, where feasible, co-occurrence on an open-data model such as Pythia), and syntactic distance of the negation cue to the answer, to quantify how much late polarity binding is driven by positive-content dominance and negation reporting bias (the core mechanistic claim)."
  ],
  "Risk Factors and Limitations": [
    "The content and polarity signals may be entangled rather than cleanly separable; the causal patching test (Exp 4), not probe onset alone, is decisive, and a graded/partial dissociation would weaken the strong two-stage claim while remaining informative.",
    "Negation-override may be conflated with genuine ignorance; the polar-QA control (establishing the model 'knows' the true polarity) is essential, and strong-prompt leakage could overestimate knowledge\u2014we use conservative thresholds and report coverage.",
    "Polarity amplification could over-negate\u2014making the model deny true affirmative facts; the correctly-answered-affirmative specificity control (Exp 5) is central and utility may be capped even if the analysis holds.",
    "Timing (content before polarity) could be a probe-difficulty artifact; tuned-lens, matched-capacity probes, and causal patching mitigate this.",
    "A single transferable polarity vector may not exist if polarity handling is relation-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.",
    "Cloze-form negation ('is not ___') is under-determined (many valid completions); we prioritize verification/polar and single-answer negation formats and report format-specific results.",
    "Multi-token answers and multi-cue negation complicate readout; primary analysis restricts to single-token-first answers and single negation cues, with reported coverage.",
    "Findings on 6\u20138B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget."
  ]
}}
