tition, route arbitration, familiarity, property collapse, lexicalization, overregularization, temporal anchors, consistency seams, inheritance, say-vs-use): here the object is the *polarity* of the query and its late binding to polarity-blind content.", 'Abstract': "Large language models often affirm a fact even when the query negates it, and generate positively-biased statements about things they can correctly deny when asked yes/no questions—the 'belief conflict.' Prior work documents this behaviorally and blames pretraining reporting bias, but offers no mechanistic account. We propose that factual retrieval is two-stage and polarity-blind at its core: the model retrieves the subject-attribute content association early, then applies the query's polarity (affirm vs. negate) in a separate, later, and comparatively weak step. Because content is bound before polarity is resolved, strongly-primed positive content overrides a stored true negation, yielding confident wrong-polarity answers. We test three claims. (1) Timing/dissociability: a content probe (predicting the retrieved subject-attribute association, polarity-invariant) and a polarity probe (predicting the query's affirmative/negative frame) are separable residual-stream directions decodable at different layers, with content emerging before polarity is applied to the answer. (2) Prevalence/geometry: negation-override errors—correct polar-QA answer but wrong-polarity generation, or affirmation of a negated cloze—concentrate where positive content is strongly primed (high subject-attribute association), above frequency-matched baselines, and the strength imbalance between content and polarity signals predicts override on held-out items. (3) Causal intervention: amplifying the late polarity direction (or patching it from a correct-polarity run) recovers correct-polarity answers with higher specificity and less collateral damage than fact editing, while preserving affirmative recall. Experiments span GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B on negated-LAMA, T-REx, and negative-commonsense sets (CSK-PN), evaluating probe separability, layer-onset ordering, override prevalence, causal recovery, and specificity.", 'Experiments': ["1. Polarity-paired dataset construction: From LAMA/T-REx, PopQA, and negative-commonsense sets (CSK-PN / Say-What-You-Mean data), build minimally-contrasting affirmative/negative pairs for each fact: an affirmative cloze/question ('The capital of France is ___' / 'Is a lion an ocean animal?') and its negation ('The capital of France is not ___' / true-negation statements). Include a matched polar-QA form to establish the model's 'true' polarity knowledge. Restrict to single-token-first answers; report coverage.", "2. Content-vs-polarity probe construction and layer-onset test: Train two linear probes on the last-token/subject-token residual stream at every layer—a content probe (target: the polarity-invariant subject-attribute association, i.e., predict the fact's object regardless of query polarity, trained across both affirmative and negative forms) and a polarity probe (target: whether the query is affirmative or negative). Metrics: per-layer AUROC/accuracy for each; onset-layer difference (does content become decodable, and does the answer reflect content, before the polarity signal is applied?) with bootstrap CIs; label-shuffle controls; residualization to confirm non-redundant information.", "3. Negation-override error taxonomy and prevalence: Classify responses on negated/negative-fact queries into (a) negation-override (model produces the affirmative/positive-content answer while the correct answer is the negation, and the model's polar-QA form shows it 'knows' the true polarity), (b) genuine ignorance (polar-QA also wrong), (c) correct. Test whether override concentrates where positive content is strongly primed—measured by affirmative-form answer confidence / subject-attribute association strength—versus a null controlling for answer frequency and lexical similarity (logistic regression; chi-square). Test whether the content-vs-polarity signal-strength imbalance (from Exp 2) predicts override on held-out items (AUROC).", '4. Causal timing/decomposition via patching: (a) Patch the polarity representation from a correct-polarity run into an override-failure run at the identified polarity-onset layers and test whether the answer flips to correct polarity while content stays fixed. (b) Ablate the polarity signal in a correct affirmative run and test whether it induces spurious negation errors (reverse control). (c) Patch content alone and confirm it does not fix polarity. These dissociate whether the failure is late/weak polarity application rather than missing content, and establish the two-stage timing causally.', "5. Polarity-amplification recovery intervention: Learn a low-rank polarity steering vector (mean activation difference between correct-polarity and override states) and apply it on held-out negation-override failures. Metrics: recovery rate; specificity—accuracy on (i) 200 unrelated affirmative facts and (ii) a held-out set of correctly-answered affirmative queries (which must remain correct, a critical control since we don't want to make the model spuriously negate) pre/post; fluency (held-out perplexity); recovered-per-unit-collateral-damage. Compare against content-direction manipulation, ROME/MEMIT editing of the fact, the Strong-Hallucinations Λ method, and a random-direction control.", '6. Transfer and generality: Test whether a single polarity steering vector generalizes across relations and across both factual and commonsense negation (train on a subset, apply to held-out relations/domains); repeat core analyses (2–5) on GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B for architecture-generality. Report whether larger models show earlier/stronger polarity binding (smaller override rate).', '7. Source analysis: Regress override rate on subject-attribute association strength, affirmative-vs-negative pr