Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "recite_vs_execute", "Title": "It Can State the Rule but Runs a Different One: The Recitation-Execution Gap in LLM Procedural Knowledge", "Short Hypothesis": "LLMs store a procedure's declarative description (which they can recite) and the executable pathway that actually runs during forward-pass computation as partially distinct representations, so a model frequently executes a simpler heuristic that contradicts the very rule it just stated correctly. We hypothesize this recitation-execution gap is a distinct, gradable failure mode, that the recited-rule representation is only weakly causally coupled to the execution circuit, and that steering the execution pathway toward the stated rule's representation makes the model actually follow it without changing what it recites.", "Related Work": "Prior work shows arithmetic is a 'bag of heuristics' rather than an algorithm (Arithmetic Without Algorithms, 2024) and undergoes 'algorithmic phase transitions' across subtasks (2024); procedural pretraining installs modular reusable circuits, mostly in attention (Procedural Pretraining, 2026; Modular Structures, 2025); Programming by Backprop (2025) finds execution-from-instruction is far less reliable than in-context instructions; procedures generalize poorly across code/graph/NL representations (Generalize Procedures Across Representations, 2026). Circuit-reuse work (2023) shows shared algorithmic building blocks. All of these characterize the enacted procedure or its reusability, but none isolate the *dissociation between the rule a model can recite and the rule it actually executes* as a behavioral phenomenon, nor test whether the recited-rule representation is causally connected to the execution circuit, nor show that binding recitation to execution fixes execution. This also differs from my prior say-vs-use proposal, which concerns implicit *use of a fact* rather than *execution of a procedure that contradicts a correctly-stated rule*.", "Abstract": "Large language models can often correctly state how to perform a procedure\u2014the rule for pluralization, a sorting comparator, a modular-arithmetic step, a game rule\u2014yet, when they actually carry it out, they execute a different, usually simpler procedure that contradicts the rule they just recited. We call this the recitation-execution gap and propose that it arises because a procedure's declarative description and the executable pathway that runs in the forward pass are carried by partially distinct internal representations that are only weakly causally coupled. We first document the phenomenon behaviorally across procedure families (string/morphology rules, list operations, modular and base-conversion arithmetic, rule-following games): for each item we elicit the model's stated rule, verify it is correct, and separately measure whether its execution obeys that rule, quantifying the gap and its dependence on procedure complexity and input difficulty. We then test the mechanistic claim with Location + Causal Intervention: linear probes identify a residual-stream representation of the recited rule and the components of the execution circuit (following bag-of-heuristics and circuit-reuse methods); activation patching tests whether the recited-rule representation is present-but-uncoupled during execution (patching a correct-execution rule representation into a gap case should not fix execution if the pathways are decoupled), and a steering intervention that injects the recited-rule representation into the execution circuit tests whether we can bind recitation to execution\u2014making the model follow its own stated rule\u2014without changing what it recites. Experiments span GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B, evaluating gap prevalence, probe separability, causal coupling, execution recovery, and specificity.", "Experiments": ["1. Procedure dataset construction: Build controlled item sets across four procedure families where a rule can be both stated and executed: (a) string/morphology transformations (reverse, remove-vowels, irregular vs regular inflection), (b) list operations (sort by a specified key, filter, dedup), (c) arithmetic procedures with an explicit algorithm (modular addition, base conversion, carrying), (d) simple rule-following games (apply a stated move rule). For each item define the correct rule-consistent output and a plausible heuristic-consistent output (the shortcut the model might run instead).", "2. Recitation elicitation and verification: For each procedure, elicit the model's stated rule in natural language / pseudocode and verify it is correct (via string match to a canonical rule and GPT-4o judging with human spot-checks). Only items where the recited rule is correct enter the analysis set; report coverage.", "3. Recitation-execution gap prevalence: Separately measure execution accuracy (does the model's produced output obey its own stated rule?). Classify each item as (a) recite-execute gap (recites correct rule but output matches the contradicting heuristic), (b) both correct, (c) recites wrong (excluded), (d) both wrong (genuine incapacity). Quantify gap prevalence per family and test its dependence on procedure complexity (rule length / number of steps) and input difficulty via regression, against a null assuming recitation and execution share one competence signal.", "4. Probe separability: Train linear probes on the residual stream to decode (i) the recited rule identity (rule-representation probe, trained where the model states the rule) and (ii) the execution-relevant intermediate state (which heuristic/branch is being applied), following bag-of-heuristics neuron categorization. Measure per-layer AUROC and, via residualization, whether the recited-rule direction carries information not present in the execution-state signal during the execution forward pass.", "5. Causal coupling test via patching: On gap items, patch the recited-rule representation from a both-correct run (same procedure) into the failing execution run at the identified layers/positions and test whether execution flips to rule-consistent output. Weak/no recovery supports the 'present-but-uncoupled' claim; strong recovery supports 'weak but usable coupling.' Reverse control: ablate the recited-rule representation in both-correct runs and test whether execution degrades toward the heuristic. Also patch execution-circuit components to confirm they carry the enacted procedure.", "6. Recitation-to-execution binding intervention: Learn a low-rank steering vector that injects the recited-rule representation into the execution circuit (mean activation difference between rule-consistent-execution and heuristic-execution matched states). Apply on held-out gap items. Metrics: execution-recovery rate (output now follows the stated rule); specificity\u2014the model's recited rule and accuracy on unrelated procedures must be unchanged, and both-correct items must remain correct (pre/post); fluency (held-out perplexity). Compare against amplifying the execution-state direction alone, fine-tuning on rule-consistent execution examples, and a random-direction control.", "7. Transfer and generality: Test whether a single binding vector generalizes across items within a family and across families (train on one family, apply to held-out families), and repeat core analyses (3\u20136) on GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B for architecture-generality. Report whether larger models show a smaller gap.", "8. Source analysis: Regress gap prevalence on procedure complexity, heuristic-shortcut availability (how strongly a simpler contradicting procedure fits the surface pattern), and input-distribution familiarity, to quantify how much the gap is driven by execution defaulting to a well-primed heuristic (the core mechanistic claim)."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["The gap could reflect that the model does not truly 'know' the rule despite reciting it (recitation as surface pattern, not internalized procedure); the recitation-verification step and the causal patching test (Exp 5) mitigate this, and a graded result remains informative.", "Recitation and execution representations may be entangled rather than separable; the steering/patching tests, not probe onset alone, are decisive, and partial dissociation would weaken the strong claim while still being informative.", "Defining a single 'execution-state' representation is hard when procedures are multi-step and distributed; primary analysis restricts to procedures with a localizable branch/decision point and reports coverage.", "The binding intervention could break correctly-executed procedures or degrade fluency if the injected direction overlaps useful features; the both-correct and unrelated-procedure specificity controls are central and utility may be capped.", "A single transferable binding vector may not exist if coupling is procedure-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.", "Multi-token outputs and long procedures complicate readout; primary analysis restricts to short outputs / single decision points with reported coverage.", "Findings on 6\u20138B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget."]}}
