Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "context_priming_contamination", "Title": "Primed to Err: How Irrelevant Contextual Mentions Contaminate an LLM's Factual Recall", "Short Hypothesis": "Factual recall in LLMs is not a pure function of the query but is biased by entities/attributes recently activated in the context: merely mentioning a related but irrelevant entity earlier in the prompt causes the model to bleed that entity's attributes into the answer for an unrelated target. We hypothesize this context-priming contamination (1) is a gradable behavioral phenomenon that scales with the semantic similarity and recency of the primed distractor, (2) is carried by a residual-stream 'primed-entity' signal that attention over the distractor mention injects into the target's answer readout, and (3) can be causally suppressed by ablating the distractor-token attention or subtracting the primed-entity direction, restoring correct recall without editing the fact.", "Related Work": "Prior work on retrieval competition studies distractors co-activated by the query itself (my retrieval_competition proposal) or by logically-linked context (in-context knowledge conflict; Fakepedia 2023; DynamicQA 2024), where the injected context explicitly contradicts or grounds the fact. Distraction/irrelevant-context work (Shi et al. 2023, 'LLMs Easily Distracted by Irrelevant Context') shows accuracy drops on math with irrelevant sentences, but does not isolate a *factual-attribute-bleed* mechanism, does not require the distractor to be a semantically related entity, and offers no mechanistic locus or targeted fix. Induction-head / copy-mechanism work explains verbatim copying from context but not attribute contamination of parametric recall. My other proposals concern query-internal gating, format copies, arbitration, polarity, and procedures. None characterize *priming contamination*: that an incidental, non-contradictory mention of a neighbor entity systematically corrupts recall of an unrelated target, nor localize it to distractor-token attention, nor show a non-destructive suppression fix. We transfer the classic human semantic-priming / source-monitoring error into parametric factual recall and give it a causal account.", "Abstract": "Human memory retrieval is famously contaminated by recently activated but irrelevant material\u2014semantic priming and source-monitoring errors. We ask whether large language models exhibit an analogous phenomenon in parametric factual recall: does merely mentioning a related but irrelevant entity earlier in the prompt corrupt the model's answer about an unrelated target? We call this context-priming contamination. We first document the behavior: for a target fact (e.g., the capital of country A), we prepend a neutral, non-contradictory sentence mentioning a semantically related distractor entity (e.g., neighboring country B) and measure how often the model answers with the distractor's attribute (B's capital) instead of A's. We predict contamination is gradable\u2014rising with the semantic similarity between distractor and target and with the recency/salience of the mention\u2014and concentrated on low-frequency targets, above frequency-matched neutral-prime baselines. We then test the mechanism with a Location + Causal Intervention strategy: a linear probe should recover a 'primed-entity' signal in the residual stream at the target's answer position that is absent under neutral primes; activation patching and attention-head analysis should show that attention over the distractor-mention tokens injects this signal; and ablating that attention or subtracting the primed-entity direction should restore correct recall with high specificity (unrelated facts and un-primed recall unchanged), whereas simply editing the fact does not address the cause. Experiments span GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B on PopQA, T-REx, and EntityQuestions, evaluating contamination rate, similarity/recency dose-response, probe recovery, causal recovery, and specificity.", "Experiments": ["1. Priming dataset construction: From PopQA/T-REx/EntityQuestions, take target facts (subject A, relation r, answer a). For each, select a distractor entity B of the same type with a *different* attribute value under r (B's answer b != a) and known semantic similarity to A (e.g., cosine of entity embeddings, taxonomic distance, geographic adjacency). Build prompts that prepend a neutral, non-contradictory sentence mentioning B (e.g., 'I was reading about {B} yesterday. What is the {relation} of {A}?'). Construct matched neutral-prime controls (a same-length sentence mentioning an unrelated, dissimilar entity) and no-prime controls.", "2. Contamination-rate measurement: For each model, measure the rate at which the primed answer is the distractor's attribute b (contamination), the correct answer a, or other, comparing related-prime vs neutral-prime vs no-prime. Metric: contamination-rate lift over neutral baseline, tested against a null controlling for answer frequency and lexical overlap (logistic regression; chi-square).", "3. Dose-response on similarity and recency: Vary (a) distractor-target semantic similarity across bins and (b) recency/salience of the mention (distance in tokens from the query, number of mentions, syntactic prominence). Hypothesis: contamination rises monotonically with similarity and recency. Report regression coefficients and per-bin curves. Also test whether low-frequency targets are more contaminated (interaction with target popularity).", "4. Primed-entity signal localization: Train a linear probe on the last-token/answer-position residual stream to decode the presence/identity of the primed distractor B, comparing related-prime, neutral-prime, and no-prime runs. Metric: per-layer AUROC for detecting the primed signal and whether the primed-entity direction aligns with b's unembedding; label-shuffle controls. This locates a candidate contamination signal.", "5. Causal test via attention analysis and patching: (a) Identify attention heads at the query/answer position that attend to the distractor-mention tokens; ablate their attention to those tokens and measure contamination reduction. (b) Activation-patch the target answer-position residual stream from a no-prime run into a contaminated run and measure recovery of a. (c) Reverse control: inject the primed-entity signal into a no-prime run and test whether it induces contamination on demand. These establish that distractor-token attention causally injects the contaminating signal.", "6. Suppression intervention: Learn a low-rank 'primed-entity' steering vector (mean activation difference between contaminated and neutral-prime matched states) and subtract it at the identified layers on held-out contaminated queries. Metrics: recovery rate; specificity (accuracy on 200 unrelated un-primed facts and on correctly-answered primed facts pre/post); fluency (held-out perplexity); recovered-per-unit-collateral-damage. Compare against distractor-attention ablation (Exp 5a), ROME editing of the target fact (expected not to help), and a random-direction control.", "7. Transfer and generality: Test whether a single suppression vector generalizes across relations and distractor types (train on a subset, apply to held-out relations); repeat core analyses (2\u20136) on GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B for architecture-generality. Report whether larger models are more robust (lower contamination).", "8. Safety-domain transfer: Replicate Exp 2\u20133 in a medical/clinical factual-recall setting (e.g., drug-indication or symptom-disease facts, using a curated safe subset) where a benign mention of a related drug/disease could contaminate the answer, quantifying whether context-priming contamination produces higher-stakes errors."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["The neutral prime may not be truly neutral (any prepended sentence adds tokens/attention load); matched-length neutral-prime and unrelated-entity controls are essential to attribute the effect to semantic priming rather than generic distraction.", "Contamination could be driven by verbatim copying (induction) rather than parametric-recall corruption; we require the distractor's attribute b to NOT appear in the prompt, so any b in the answer must come from parametric recall, and the attention analysis distinguishes copying from attribute injection.", "The primed-entity signal and target recall may be entangled; the causal patching/ablation tests (Exp 5), not probe AUROC alone, are decisive, and graded results remain informative.", "Suppression could over-correct (harm cases where contextual mention is legitimately relevant) or degrade fluency; the correctly-answered-primed and unrelated-fact specificity controls are central and utility may be capped.", "A single transferable suppression vector may not exist if contamination is relation- or distractor-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.", "Multi-token answers complicate readout; primary analysis restricts to single-token-first answers with reported coverage.", "Findings on 6\u20138B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget."]}}
