specificity.", 'Experiments': ["1. Priming dataset construction: From PopQA/T-REx/EntityQuestions, take target facts (subject A, relation r, answer a). For each, select a distractor entity B of the same type with a *different* attribute value under r (B's answer b != a) and known semantic similarity to A (e.g., cosine of entity embeddings, taxonomic distance, geographic adjacency). Build prompts that prepend a neutral, non-contradictory sentence mentioning B (e.g., 'I was reading about {B} yesterday. What is the {relation} of {A}?'). Construct matched neutral-prime controls (a same-length sentence mentioning an unrelated, dissimilar entity) and no-prime controls.", "2. Contamination-rate measurement: For each model, measure the rate at which the primed answer is the distractor's attribute b (contamination), the correct answer a, or other, comparing related-prime vs neutral-prime vs no-prime. Metric: contamination-rate lift over neutral baseline, tested against a null controlling for answer frequency and lexical overlap (logistic regression; chi-square).", '3. Dose-response on similarity and recency: Vary (a) distractor-target semantic similarity across bins and (b) recency/salience of the mention (distance in tokens from the query, number of mentions, syntactic prominence). Hypothesis: contamination rises monotonically with similarity and recency. Report regression coefficients and per-bin curves. Also test whether low-frequency targets are more contaminated (interaction with target popularity).', "4. Primed-entity signal localization: Train a linear probe on the last-token/answer-position residual stream to decode the presence/identity of the primed distractor B, comparing related-prime, neutral-prime, and no-prime runs. Metric: per-layer AUROC for detecting the primed signal and whether the primed-entity direction aligns with b's unembedding; label-shuffle controls. This locates a candidate contamination signal.", '5. Causal test via attention analysis and patching: (a) Identify attention heads at the query/answer position that attend to the distractor-mention tokens; ablate their attention to those tokens and measure contamination reduction. (b) Activation-patch the target answer-position residual stream from a no-prime run into a contaminated run and measure recovery of a. (c) Reverse control: inject the primed-entity signal into a no-prime run and test whether it induces contamination on demand. These establish that distractor-token attention causally injects the contaminating signal.', "6. Suppression intervention: Learn a low-rank 'primed-entity' steering vector (mean activation difference between contaminated and neutral-prime matched states) and subtract it at the identified layers on held-out contaminated queries. Metrics: recovery rate; specificity (accuracy on 200 unrelated un-primed facts and on correctly-answered primed facts pre/post); fluency (held-out perplexity); recovered-per-unit-collateral-damage. Compare against distractor-attention ablation (Exp 5a), ROME editing of the target fact (expected not to help), and a random-direction control.", '7. Transfer and generality: Test whether a single suppression vector generalizes across relations and distractor types (train on a subset, apply to held-out relations); repeat core analyses (2–6) on GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B for architecture-generality. Report whether larger models are more robust (lower contamination).', '8. Safety-domain transfer: Replicate Exp 2–3 in a medical/clinical factual-recall setting (e.g., drug-indication or symptom-disease facts, using a curated safe subset) where a benign mention of a related drug/disease could contaminate the answer, quantifying whether context-priming contamination produces higher-stakes errors.'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['The neutral prime may not be truly neutral (any prepended sentence adds tokens/attention load); matched-length neutral-prime and unrelated-entity controls are essential to attribute the effect to semantic priming rather than generic distraction.', "Contamination could be driven by verbatim copying (induction) rather than parametric-recall corruption; we require the distractor's attribute b to NOT appear in the prompt, so any b in the answer must come from parametric recall, and the attention analysis distinguishes copying from attribute injection.", 'The primed-entity signal and target recall may be entangled; the causal patching/ablation tests (Exp 5), not probe AUROC alone, are decisive, and graded results remain informative.', 'Suppression could over-correct (harm cases where contextual mention is legitimately relevant) or degrade fluency; the correctly-answered-primed and unrelated-fact specificity controls are central and utility may be capped.', 'A single transferable suppression vector may not exist if contamination is relation- or distractor-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.', 'Multi-token answers complicate readout; primary analysis restricts to single-token-first answers with reported coverage.', 'Findings on 6–8B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget.']}

