Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "presupposition_writeback", "Title": "Presupposition Writeback: How a False Aside About an Entity Silently Corrupts an Unrelated Fact the Model Knows", "Short Hypothesis": "When a query embeds a false presupposition about one attribute of a subject as an incidental aside (not the thing being asked), LLMs 'write back' that counterfactual attribute into the subject's early representation, and this corruption then bleeds into recall of a *different, unrelated* attribute the model otherwise answers correctly. We hypothesize this presupposition-writeback (1) is a gradable behavioral phenomenon whose error rate scales with the plausibility of the false presupposition and its semantic relatedness to the queried attribute, (2) is carried by a subject-position residual-stream 'writeback' signal injected early via attention over the presupposed-attribute tokens, and (3) can be causally suppressed by ablating that attention or subtracting the writeback direction, restoring correct recall of the unrelated attribute without editing any fact.", "Related Work": "False-premise work studies models answering the false-premise question itself: FAITH (2024) localizes 'false premise heads' that disturb knowledge extraction for the presupposed object; answerability-probe work (2023) and Ambiguity-Encoding Neurons (2025) study abstention; DecoPrompt (2024) reduces hallucination via prompt decoding; FalseQA/CREPE (2022-2023) benchmark rebuttal ability; 'LLMs Struggle to Reject False Presuppositions when Stakes are High' (2025) is behavioral-only. All of these concern whether the model detects/rejects the false premise or hallucinates an answer *to the premise's own attribute*. None study the *cross-attribute spillover*: that a false aside about attribute A silently corrupts recall of an *unrelated* attribute B of the same subject that the model would otherwise get right. This also differs from my context_priming_contamination proposal, which primes a *different distractor entity* (attribute bleed across entities); here the false content is about the *same* subject but a *different* attribute, and the query never asks about the false content. The writeback-into-subject mechanism and the cross-attribute suppression fix are new.", "Abstract": "Large language models are known to be misled when a question's premise is false and the question asks about that very premise. We study a subtler and more insidious phenomenon: a false presupposition slipped in as an incidental aside about one attribute of a subject silently corrupts the model's recall of a *different, unrelated* attribute it otherwise knows. For example, asking 'Given that Marie Curie was born in Germany, what field did she win her Nobel Prize in?' can degrade the correct 'Physics/Chemistry' answer even though nationality is irrelevant to the prize field. We call this presupposition writeback: the false attribute is written into the subject's early representation and then bleeds into orthogonal attribute recall. We first document the behavior across relation pairs, measuring how error on the queried (unrelated) attribute rises when a plausible false presupposition about a second attribute is present, versus a true-presupposition control and a no-presupposition control. We predict the effect is gradable, scaling with the presupposition's plausibility and its semantic relatedness to the queried attribute, and concentrating on low-frequency subjects. We then test the mechanism with a Location + Causal Intervention strategy: a linear probe should recover a 'writeback' signal at the subject position that carries the false attribute even though it was never queried; activation patching and attention analysis should show early attention over the presupposed-attribute tokens injects it into the subject representation; and ablating that attention or subtracting the writeback direction should restore correct recall of the unrelated attribute with high specificity. Experiments span GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B on PopQA/T-REx, evaluating writeback error rate, plausibility/relatedness dose-response, probe recovery, causal recovery, and specificity.", "Experiments": ["1. Cross-attribute presupposition dataset: From PopQA/T-REx select subjects with at least two known attributes under distinct relations (e.g., person: nationality and Nobel-field; city: country and founding-year). For each subject pick a queried attribute B (relation r_B, correct answer b) and a presupposed attribute A (relation r_A, true value a_true). Build three prompt conditions: (i) no-presupposition ('What is the {r_B} of {S}?'), (ii) true-presupposition ('Given that {S}'s {r_A} is {a_true}, what is the {r_B} of {S}?'), (iii) false-presupposition (same but with a plausible false value a_false != a_true). Crucially the query only ever asks about B; A is an irrelevant aside. Restrict to single-token-first B answers; report coverage.", "2. Writeback error prevalence: For each model measure accuracy on B across the three conditions. Define writeback error as: B correct under no-presupposition and true-presupposition but wrong under false-presupposition. Metric: writeback error-rate lift of false- over true-presupposition, tested against a null controlling for prompt length and B-answer frequency (logistic regression; McNemar/chi-square on paired items). This isolates cross-attribute corruption from generic distraction.", "3. Dose-response on plausibility and relatedness: Vary (a) plausibility of a_false (rate via a_false's own prior likelihood / entity-type match, from implausible to highly plausible) and (b) semantic relatedness between the presupposed relation r_A and queried relation r_B (e.g., same-domain vs cross-domain relations, measured by relation-embedding similarity). Hypothesis: writeback error rises monotonically with plausibility and with r_A-r_B relatedness, and is larger for low-frequency subjects (interaction with subject popularity). Report regression coefficients and per-bin curves.", "4. Writeback signal localization: Train a linear probe on the subject-token residual stream (across layers) to decode the value of the *presupposed* attribute A (i.e., can we read a_false out of the subject representation even though A was never queried?). Compare false- vs true- vs no-presupposition runs. Metric: per-layer AUROC/accuracy for detecting the injected a_false signal and whether the writeback direction aligns with a_false's unembedding; label-shuffle controls. Also probe whether the correct b signal is weakened at the query position under false-presupposition.", "5. Causal test via attention analysis and patching: (a) Identify early attention heads at the subject position that attend to the presupposed-attribute (a_false) tokens; ablate their attention to those tokens and measure writeback-error reduction. (b) Activation-patch the subject-token residual stream from a no-presupposition run into a false-presupposition run and measure recovery of correct b. (c) Reverse control: inject the writeback signal (a_false direction) into a no-presupposition run and test whether it induces errors on B on demand. These establish that early subject-writeback of the false attribute causally drives the unrelated-attribute error.", "6. Suppression intervention: Learn a low-rank 'writeback' steering vector (mean subject-token activation difference between false-presupposition writeback-error states and matched true-presupposition states) and subtract it at the identified layers on held-out false-presupposition queries. Metrics: recovery rate on B; specificity (accuracy on 200 unrelated no-presupposition facts and on true-presupposition items, which must stay correct, pre/post); fluency (held-out perplexity); recovered-per-unit-collateral-damage. Compare against attention ablation (Exp 5a), ROME editing (expected not to help since no fact is wrong), the FAITH false-premise-head constraint, and a random-direction control.", "7. Transfer and generality: Test whether a single writeback-suppression vector generalizes across relation pairs and subject types (train on a subset, apply to held-out relation pairs); repeat core analyses (2-6) on GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B for architecture-generality. Report whether larger models are more robust (lower writeback error), consistent with 'Too Big to Fool' (2024).", "8. Safety-domain transfer: Replicate Exp 2-3 in a clinical/medical factual-recall setting (e.g., a false aside about a drug's route-of-administration while asking its correct indication, using a curated safe subset), quantifying whether presupposition writeback produces higher-stakes cross-attribute errors than in general-knowledge prompts."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["The true-presupposition control may not fully match the false one (a_false could be longer/rarer than a_true); we match token length and control for a_false frequency, and use the true-presupposition condition (not just no-presupposition) as the key baseline so any extra error must come from the falseness, not from adding a clause.", "The effect could be generic distraction rather than attribute-specific writeback; the dose-response on r_A-r_B relatedness (Exp 3) and the subject-position decoding of a_false (Exp 4) distinguish targeted writeback from nonspecific interference.", "The writeback signal and correct-b recall may be entangled; the causal patching/ablation tests (Exp 5), not probe AUROC alone, are decisive, and graded results remain informative.", "Suppression could over-correct on cases where the presupposition is legitimately relevant, or degrade fluency; the true-presupposition and unrelated-fact specificity controls are central and utility may be capped.", "Writeback errors may be rare relative to genuine ignorance; the paired within-subject design (same subject across conditions) maximizes power and a modest but significant lift is still informative.", "A single transferable suppression vector may not exist if writeback is relation-pair-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.", "Multi-token B answers complicate readout; primary analysis restricts to single-token-first answers with reported coverage.", "Findings on 6-8B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget."]}}
