Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "enumeration_closure_gate",
  "Title": "Knowing More Than It Lists: A Closure Signal That Terminates LLM Enumeration Before Its Knowledge Is Exhausted",
  "Short Hypothesis": "When an LLM enumerates the members of a set (e.g., 'list the countries bordering France'), it stops not because it has exhausted its stored members but because a distinct, query-independent 'closure' signal in the residual stream crosses a threshold. We hypothesize (1) at the moment the model emits a stop, additional valid members remain internally recoverable (a completeness gap), (2) a linear 'closure' direction predicts termination and is largely driven by count/list-length priors rather than by actual member-set exhaustion, and (3) suppressing this closure direction extends enumeration to recover more true members with high specificity, whereas it is not fixed by simply prompting 'list more'.",
  "Related Work": "Prior work explains the promote-then-suppress mechanism for generating *distinct* one-to-many answers (Promote-Suppress-Iterate 2025) and how counting is computed layerwise (CountScope 2025; When Can Transformers Count to n 2024). Benchmarks show LLMs cannot enumerate completely (Can LMs Store/Retrieve 1-to-N 2023; Do LLMs Know How Much They Know 2024; Olympic Rankings 2024), are brittle on set membership (Brittleness of Set Membership 2025), and cannot detect omissions (AbsenceBench 2025). Aggregation/completeness is studied at the systems level (AGGBench 2026; GraphRAG). Crucially, all of these describe how distinct answers are produced, how counting works, or that enumeration is incomplete behaviorally; none isolate the *termination decision* as a mechanism, none test whether stored members remain internally recoverable at the stop point, and none show a closure signal that gates completeness independent of exhaustion. This is orthogonal to my prior proposals (all single-answer recall/gating): here the object is the decision to *stop enumerating* and the completeness gap it causes.",
  "Abstract": "Large language models routinely stop listing set members before exhausting what they know: asked to list authors of an author, films of a director, or countries bordering a nation, they produce a plausible-length answer and stop, omitting members they can produce when prompted individually. Prior work explains how models generate distinct successive answers and how they count, but not why they decide to stop. We propose that enumeration termination is governed by a distinct, query-independent closure signal in the residual stream that crosses a threshold and halts listing—often before the model's stored member set is exhausted, producing a completeness gap. We test three claims on one-to-many factual queries. (1) Completeness gap: at the position where the model emits its stop token, additional valid members are still internally recoverable (via linear probing / logit-lens over member-typed candidates and via single-item verification prompts), showing the stop is premature relative to stored knowledge. (2) Closure locus and drivers: a linear closure direction, decodable at intermediate layers before the stop token, predicts termination, and regression shows it tracks generic list-length/count priors (position in list, typical set-size for the relation) more than actual set exhaustion. (3) Causal intervention: suppressing the closure direction (or ablating the attention that reads it) extends enumeration and recovers additional true members, with specificity controls ensuring the model does not fabricate members or degrade on sets it correctly completes, and outperforming a 'keep listing' re-prompt baseline. Experiments span GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B on curated one-to-many relations, evaluating completeness-gap prevalence, closure-prediction AUROC, recovered-member yield, precision, and fluency.",
  "Experiments": [
    "1. One-to-many dataset construction: Curate factual set-valued relations with known complete ground-truth membership from Wikidata (countries bordering X; films directed by X; children of X; member states of an organization; elements in a chemical group), spanning small (3-8) and large (15-40) true set sizes. For each query record the model's free enumeration (list until stop), and the ground-truth member set. Restrict to single-token-first members where needed; report coverage.",
    "2. Completeness-gap measurement: At the position where the model emits its list-ending token (EOS / 'and that's all' / stop), test whether additional true members are internally recoverable via (a) logit-lens/tuned-lens over member-typed candidate tokens (restricted to the relation's answer type and excluding already-listed members), and (b) single-item verification prompts ('Is Y a member of {set}?') confirming the model stores Y. Metric: fraction of stops where >=1 unlisted true member is internally recoverable (completeness-gap rate), vs a null where stopping coincides with exhaustion.",
    "3. Closure-signal localization: Train a linear probe on the last-token residual stream at each pre-stop step to predict whether the model will emit a stop at the next step. Metric: per-layer AUROC of stop prediction; earliest layer where closure is decodable; label-shuffle control. Compare against a 'remaining-members' probe (does the residual stream still encode unlisted members?) to test that closure and exhaustion are separable directions (residualization for non-redundancy).",
    "4. Driver regression: Regress the closure probe output on (a) generic list-length priors (number of items already emitted, typical set-size for the relation estimated from other subjects), (b) actual exhaustion signals (number of true members remaining, recoverability from Exp 2), and (c) surface cues (repetition/self-attention to prior answers). Hypothesis: closure is dominated by generic list-length/count priors over true exhaustion.",
    "5. Causal termination test via patching/ablation: (a) Suppress the closure direction (subtract the learned low-rank closure vector) at the identified layers when the model is about to stop, and measure whether valid enumeration continues. (b) Identify and ablate attention heads reading the closure signal (following promote-then-suppress-style knockout). (c) Reverse control: inject the closure direction early to force premature stopping on demand. Metrics: additional true members recovered per query; precision of newly emitted members (must not be fabrications or repeats).",
    "6. Closure-suppression recovery intervention: Apply the closure-suppression steering on held-out queries with dose control. Metrics: recovered-true-member yield; precision (new members that are ground-truth correct); specificity (on small sets the model already completes correctly, it must not over-generate or fabricate; measure false-member rate pre/post); fluency (held-out perplexity). Baselines: a 'continue listing / are there more?' re-prompt, temperature increase, and beam widening.",
    "7. Transfer and generality: Test whether a single closure-suppression vector generalizes across relations and set sizes (train on a subset, apply to held-out relations); repeat core analyses (2-6) on GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B for architecture-generality. Report whether larger models have smaller completeness gaps.",
    "8. Source analysis: Regress completeness-gap rate on true set size, member popularity distribution, and relation frequency, to quantify whether premature closure is driven by generic length priors and tail-member obscurity rather than storage."
  ],
  "Risk Factors and Limitations": [
    "The completeness gap could reflect that unlisted members are not truly stored (probe/logit-lens false positives); the single-item verification control (Exp 2b) with conservative thresholds is essential and coverage is reported.",
    "Closure and exhaustion signals may be entangled rather than separable directions; the causal suppression/injection tests (Exp 5), not probe AUROC alone, are decisive, and graded results remain informative.",
    "Closure suppression could induce fabrication or repetition (the model keeps listing false members); precision and false-member specificity controls (Exp 6) are central and utility may be capped if suppression trades completeness for hallucination.",
    "Ground-truth set completeness is hard for open-ended relations; we restrict to relations with authoritative complete membership (Wikidata-verifiable) and report coverage limits.",
    "A single transferable closure vector may not exist if termination is relation-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.",
    "Multi-token members and stop-token detection vary across tokenizers; primary analysis restricts to single-token-first members and canonical stop conditions with reported coverage.",
    "Findings on 6-8B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget."
  ]
}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'enumeration_closure_gate', 'Title': 'Knowing More Than It Lists: A Closure Signal That Terminates LLM Enumeration Before Its Knowledge Is Exhausted', 'Short Hypothesis': "When an LLM enumerates the members of a set (e.g., 'list the countries bordering France'), it stops not because it has exhausted its stored members but because a distinct, query-independent 'closure' signal in the residual stream crosses a threshold. We hypothesize (1) at the moment the model emits a stop, additional valid members remain internally recoverable (a completeness gap), (2) a linear 'closure' direction predicts termination and is largely driven by count/list-length priors rather than by actual member-set exhaustion, and (3) suppressing this closure direction extends enumeration to recover more true members with high specificity, whereas it is not fixed by simply prompting 'list more'.", 'Related Work': 'Prior work explains the promote-then-suppress mechanism for generating *distinct* one-to-many answers (Promote-Suppress-Iterate 2025) and how counting is computed layerwise (CountScope 2025; When Can Transformers Count to n 2024). Benchmarks show LLMs cannot enumerate completely (Can LMs Store/Retrieve 1-to-N 2023; Do LLMs Know How Much They Know 2024; Olympic Rankings 2024), are brittle on set membership (Brittleness of Set Membership 2025), and cannot detect omissions (AbsenceBench 2025). Aggregation/completeness is studied at the systems level (AGGBench 2026; GraphRAG). Crucially, all of these describe how distinct answers are produced, how counting works, or that enumeration is incomplete behaviorally; none isolate the *termination decision* as a mechanism, none test whether stored members remain internally recoverable at the stop point, and none show a closure signal that gates completeness independent of exhaustion. This is orthogonal to my prior proposals (all single-answer recall/gating): here the object is the decision to *stop enumerating* and the completeness gap it causes.', 'Abstract': "Large language models routinely stop listing set members before exhausting what they know: asked to list authors of an author, films of a director, or countries bordering a nation, they produce a plausible-length answer and stop, omitting members they can produce when prompted individually. Prior work explains how models generate distinct successive answers and how they count, but not why they decide to stop. We propose that enumeration termination is governed by a distinct, query-independent closure signal in the residual stream that crosses a threshold and halts listing—often before the model's stored member set is exhausted, producing a completeness gap. We test three claims on one-to-many factual queries. (1) Completeness gap: at the position where the model emits its stop token, additional valid members are still internally recoverable (via linear probing / logit-lens over member-typed candidates and via single-item verification prompts), showing the stop is premature relative to stored knowledge. (2) Closure locus and drivers: a linear closure direction, decodable at intermediate layers before the stop token, predicts termination, and regression shows it tracks generic list-length/count priors (position in list, typical set-size for the relation) more than actual set exhaustion. (3) Causal intervention: suppressing the closure direction (or ablating the attention that reads it) extends enumeration and recovers additional true members, with specificity controls ensuring the model does not fabricate members or degrade on sets it correctly completes, and outperforming a 'keep listing' re-prompt baseline. Experiments span GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B on curated one-to-many relations, evaluating completeness-gap prevalence, closure-prediction AUROC, recovered-member yield, precision, and fluency.", 'Experiments': ["1. One-to-many dataset construction: Curate factual set-valued relations with known complete ground-truth membership from Wikidata (countries bordering X; films directed by X; children of X; member states of an organization; elements in a chemical group), spanning small (3-8) and large (15-40) true set sizes. For each query record the model's free enumeration (list until stop), and the ground-truth member set. Restrict to single-token-first members where needed; report coverage.", "2. Completeness-gap measurement: At the position where the model emits its list-ending token (EOS / 'and that's all' / stop), test whether additional true members are internally recoverable via (a) logit-lens/tuned-lens over member-typed candidate tokens (restricted to the relation's answer type and excluding already-listed members), and (b) single-item verification prompts ('Is Y a member of {set}?') confirming the model stores Y. Metric: fraction of stops where >=1 unlisted true member is internally recoverable (completeness-gap rate), vs a null where stopping coincides with exhaustion.", "3. Closure-signal localization: Train a linear probe on the last-token residual stream at each pre-stop step to predict whether the model will emit a stop at the next step. Metric: per-layer AUROC of stop prediction; earliest layer where closure is decodable; label-shuffle control. Compare against a 'remaining-members' probe (does the residual stream still encode unlisted members?) to test that closure and exhaustion are separable directions (residualization for non-redundancy).", '4. Driver regression: Regress the closure probe output on (a) generic list-length priors (number of items already emitted, typical set-size for the relation estimated from other subjects), (b) actual exhaustion signals (number of true members remaining, recoverability from Exp 2), and (c) surface cues (repetition/self-attention to prior answers). Hypothesis: closure is dominated by generic list-length/count priors over true exhaustion.', '5. Causal termination test via patching/ablation: (a) Suppress the closure direction (subtract the learned low-rank closure vector) at the identified layers when the model is about to stop, and measure whether valid enumeration continues. (b) Identify and ablate attention heads reading the closure signal (following promote-then-suppress-style knockout). (c) Reverse control: inject the closure direction early to force premature stopping on demand. Metrics: additional true members recovered per query; precision of newly emitted members (must not be fabrications or repeats).', "6. Closure-suppression recovery intervention: Apply the closure-suppression steering on held-out queries with dose control. Metrics: recovered-true-member yield; precision (new members that are ground-truth correct); specificity (on small sets the model already completes correctly, it must not over-generate or fabricate; measure false-member rate pre/post); fluency (held-out perplexity). Baselines: a 'continue listing / are there more?' re-prompt, temperature increase, and beam widening.", '7. Transfer and generality: Test whether a single closure-suppression vector generalizes across relations and set sizes (train on a subset, apply to held-out relations); repeat core analyses (2-6) on GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B for architecture-generality. Report whether larger models have smaller completeness gaps.', '8. Source analysis: Regress completeness-gap rate on true set size, member popularity distribution, and relation frequency, to quantify whether premature closure is driven by generic length priors and tail-member obscurity rather than storage.'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['The completeness gap could reflect that unlisted members are not truly stored (probe/logit-lens false positives); the single-item verification control (Exp 2b) with conservative thresholds is essential and coverage is reported.', 'Closure and exhaustion signals may be entangled rather than separable directions; the causal suppression/injection tests (Exp 5), not probe AUROC alone, are decisive, and graded results remain informative.', 'Closure suppression could induce fabrication or repetition (the model keeps listing false members); precision and false-member specificity controls (Exp 6) are central and utility may be capped if suppression trades completeness for hallucination.', 'Ground-truth set completeness is hard for open-ended relations; we restrict to relations with authoritative complete membership (Wikidata-verifiable) and report coverage limits.', 'A single transferable closure vector may not exist if termination is relation-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.', 'Multi-token members and stop-token detection vary across tokenizers; primary analysis restricts to single-token-first members and canonical stop conditions with reported coverage.', 'Findings on 6-8B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget.']}

