Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "granularity_overcommitment", "Title": "Too Precise to Be Right: A Query-Independent Precision Prior That Makes LLMs Overcommit to a Granularity and Hide Coarser Correct Knowledge", "Short Hypothesis": "When answering a factual query that admits answers at multiple granularities (e.g., 'August 4, 1961' vs '1961' vs 'the 1960s' for a birth date), LLMs select the *specificity level* of the answer via a distinct, largely query-independent 'precision prior' computed before the specific content is fully resolved. Overcommitment to a too-fine granularity is a common failure mode: the model emits a confident, wrong specific value even though it reliably stores the coarser, correct value. We hypothesize (1) the chosen granularity is linearly decodable from intermediate activations before the answer, and is driven by generic format/precision priors more than by how confidently the fine-grained value is stored; (2) overcommitment errors concentrate on long-tail entities where the fine value is uncertain but a coarse value is stored; and (3) suppressing the precision-prior direction (not editing any fact) shifts the model to the correct coarser answer with high specificity, unlike a 'be less specific' re-prompt.", "Related Work": "GRANOLA QA (Yona et al. 2024) behaviorally shows LLMs default to overly specific answers that are often wrong, and that evaluating against multi-granularity answers reveals ~20 points of hidden knowledge (larger for rare entities); their fix, DRAG, is a decoding heuristic tied to output uncertainty, with no mechanistic account. Inside-Out (2025) and 'Empty Shelves or Lost Keys?' (2026) show a large recall/access gap between encoded and expressed knowledge but do not treat *answer granularity* as the axis of failure. Concept Depth (2024) and layer-wise knowledge-encoding studies characterize where concepts of varying complexity emerge but not a granularity-selection decision. Knowledge-editing granularity work (DiKE 2025, FiNE 2025) concerns editing precision at the fact level, not the model's own choice of answer specificity. Crucially, no prior work (i) isolates granularity/precision selection as a distinct internal decision separable from content retrieval, (ii) tests that it is driven by a query-independent precision prior rather than fine-value confidence, or (iii) causally recovers coarser correct knowledge by intervening on that decision. This is orthogonal to my prior proposals, which all concern single-granularity retrieval, competition, arbitration, or enumeration closure; here the object is the *specificity level* of the answer.", "Abstract": "Large language models often answer factual questions with a confidently wrong, overly specific value even when they reliably know a coarser, correct one\u2014stating an exact but wrong date when they know the correct decade. Prior work documents this behaviorally and patches it at decoding time, but why models commit to a granularity is unknown. We propose that answer specificity is set by a distinct, largely query-independent precision prior computed in intermediate layers before the specific content is resolved, and that granularity overcommitment\u2014emitting a too-fine, wrong value while the coarser correct value is internally available\u2014is a systematic, mechanistically localizable failure. We test three claims on multi-granularity factual queries (dates, locations, quantities, taxonomic categories). (1) Dissociability and locus: a linear 'granularity' probe predicts the specificity level of the forthcoming answer before it is written, is separable (via residualization) from a content probe that predicts the specific value, and\u2014by regression\u2014tracks generic format/precision cues (relation type, prompt phrasing, entity popularity) more than the internal confidence in the fine-grained value. (2) Overcommitment geometry: overcommitment errors concentrate on long-tail entities where the fine value is uncertain but a coarser value is verifiably stored (via coarse-level probing and direct coarse-question verification), above frequency-matched baselines. (3) Causal recovery: suppressing the precision-prior direction, or patching it from a coarse-answer run, shifts the model to the correct coarser answer with high specificity\u2014preserving cases where the fine answer is correct\u2014and outperforms a 'be less specific' re-prompt and fact editing. Experiments span GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B on GRANOLA-EQ and multi-granularity T-REx/PopQA, evaluating probe separability, driver regression, overcommitment prevalence, causal recovery, and specificity.", "Experiments": ["1. Multi-granularity dataset: Use GRANOLA-EQ (multi-granularity EntityQuestions) and build additional multi-granularity items from T-REx/PopQA for four answer types\u2014dates (day/month/year/decade), locations (city/region/country), quantities (exact/rounded/order-of-magnitude), and taxonomic categories (species/genus/family). For each item record the ordered set of correct answers at increasing coarseness, plus the model's free-form answer and its granularity level. Restrict primary analysis to single-token-first coarse answers where needed; report coverage.", "2. Storage verification for coarse answers: For each item where the model's fine answer is wrong, verify the coarser correct value is stored via (a) a direct coarse-question prompt ('In what decade was X born?') and (b) a linear content probe decoding the coarse value from the residual stream. Only items with verified coarse-value storage enter the 'overcommitment-candidate' set; report coverage. This separates overcommitment from genuine ignorance.", "3. Granularity vs content probes and layer onset: Train two linear probes on the last-token/subject-token residual stream at every layer: a granularity probe (target: the specificity level of the forthcoming answer\u2014ordinal: fine/mid/coarse) and a content probe (target: the specific fine value). Metrics: per-layer accuracy/AUROC; whether granularity is decodable before the fine value is resolved (onset-layer difference, bootstrap CIs); residualization to confirm the granularity direction carries information not in the content signal; label-shuffle controls.", "4. Driver regression (precision prior): Regress the granularity-probe output on (a) query-independent/format cues\u2014relation type, prompt phrasing (open vs 'be brief' vs 'be precise'), and a fixed per-relation default granularity\u2014and (b) fine-value confidence signals\u2014content-probe strength, logit margin of the top fine candidate. Hypothesis: chosen granularity is dominated by generic precision priors over fine-value confidence, i.e., the model commits to a specificity level largely regardless of whether it actually pins the fine value.", "5. Overcommitment prevalence and geometry: Define overcommitment error as: fine answer wrong, but coarser correct value verifiably stored (Exp 2). Test whether these errors concentrate on long-tail (low-popularity) entities and on relations with high default precision, versus a null controlling for answer frequency and lexical similarity (logistic regression; chi-square). Report per-relation and per-answer-type breakdowns.", "6. Causal test via patching/steering: (a) Suppress the granularity/precision-prior direction (subtract a learned low-rank vector, computed as the mean activation difference between coarse-answer and fine-answer matched states) at the identified layers and test whether the model shifts to the correct coarser answer. (b) Patch the granularity representation from a coarse-answer run (elicited by a 'be approximate' prompt) into an overcommitment run and measure recovery of the correct coarse value. (c) Reverse control: inject the fine-precision direction to force overcommitment on demand. (d) Content control: patch only the content direction and confirm it does not change granularity. Metrics: recovery rate; that the correct value emerges at the coarse level rather than a new wrong fine value.", "7. Recovery intervention with specificity: Apply granularity-suppression steering on held-out overcommitment failures with dose control. Metrics: coarse-correct recovery rate; specificity\u2014accuracy on (i) 200 items the model answers correctly at fine granularity (must not be coarsened; a critical control) and (ii) 200 unrelated facts, pre/post; fluency (held-out perplexity); recovered-per-unit-collateral-damage. Baselines: a 'be less specific / give the decade' re-prompt, DRAG-style uncertainty decoding, ROME editing (expected not to help since no fact is wrong), and a random-direction control.", "8. Transfer, generality, and source: Test whether a single granularity-suppression vector generalizes across relations and answer types (train on a subset, apply to held-out); repeat core analyses (2\u20137) on GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B for architecture-generality, reporting whether larger models overcommit less. Regress overcommitment rate on entity popularity, per-relation default granularity, and fine-value uncertainty to quantify the precision-prior mechanism."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["Granularity and content signals may be entangled rather than cleanly separable; the causal patching/steering tests (Exp 6), not probe onset alone, are decisive, and a graded result would weaken the strong dissociation while remaining informative.", "Verifying the coarse value is 'stored' risks overestimation from prompt leakage; conservative content-probe thresholds and direct coarse-question verification with reported coverage mitigate this.", "Granularity is naturally ordinal/graded, not discrete; we model it ordinally and report continuous granularity scores rather than forcing clean levels.", "Granularity suppression could over-coarsen\u2014making the model vague even when it knows the precise answer; the correctly-answered-fine specificity control (Exp 7) is central and utility may be capped even if the analysis holds.", "The precision prior may be partly query-dependent (some phrasings legitimately demand specificity); the driver regression separates format cues from fine-value confidence rather than claiming full query-independence.", "A single transferable suppression vector may not exist if granularity control is relation-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.", "Multi-token coarse answers (e.g., month names, region names) complicate readout; primary analysis restricts to single-token-first answers with reported coverage.", "Findings on 6\u20138B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget."]}}
