stored (via coarse-level probing and direct coarse-question verification), above frequency-matched baselines. (3) Causal recovery: suppressing the precision-prior direction, or patching it from a coarse-answer run, shifts the model to the correct coarser answer with high specificity—preserving cases where the fine answer is correct—and outperforms a 'be less specific' re-prompt and fact editing. Experiments span GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B on GRANOLA-EQ and multi-granularity T-REx/PopQA, evaluating probe separability, driver regression, overcommitment prevalence, causal recovery, and specificity.", 'Experiments': ["1. Multi-granularity dataset: Use GRANOLA-EQ (multi-granularity EntityQuestions) and build additional multi-granularity items from T-REx/PopQA for four answer types—dates (day/month/year/decade), locations (city/region/country), quantities (exact/rounded/order-of-magnitude), and taxonomic categories (species/genus/family). For each item record the ordered set of correct answers at increasing coarseness, plus the model's free-form answer and its granularity level. Restrict primary analysis to single-token-first coarse answers where needed; report coverage.", "2. Storage verification for coarse answers: For each item where the model's fine answer is wrong, verify the coarser correct value is stored via (a) a direct coarse-question prompt ('In what decade was X born?') and (b) a linear content probe decoding the coarse value from the residual stream. Only items with verified coarse-value storage enter the 'overcommitment-candidate' set; report coverage. This separates overcommitment from genuine ignorance.", '3. Granularity vs content probes and layer onset: Train two linear probes on the last-token/subject-token residual stream at every layer: a granularity probe (target: the specificity level of the forthcoming answer—ordinal: fine/mid/coarse) and a content probe (target: the specific fine value). Metrics: per-layer accuracy/AUROC; whether granularity is decodable before the fine value is resolved (onset-layer difference, bootstrap CIs); residualization to confirm the granularity direction carries information not in the content signal; label-shuffle controls.', "4. Driver regression (precision prior): Regress the granularity-probe output on (a) query-independent/format cues—relation type, prompt phrasing (open vs 'be brief' vs 'be precise'), and a fixed per-relation default granularity—and (b) fine-value confidence signals—content-probe strength, logit margin of the top fine candidate. Hypothesis: chosen granularity is dominated by generic precision priors over fine-value confidence, i.e., the model commits to a specificity level largely regardless of whether it actually pins the fine value.", '5. Overcommitment prevalence and geometry: Define overcommitment error as: fine answer wrong, but coarser correct value verifiably stored (Exp 2). Test whether these errors concentrate on long-tail (low-popularity) entities and on relations with high default precision, versus a null controlling for answer frequency and lexical similarity (logistic regression; chi-square). Report per-relation and per-answer-type breakdowns.', "6. Causal test via patching/steering: (a) Suppress the granularity/precision-prior direction (subtract a learned low-rank vector, computed as the mean activation difference between coarse-answer and fine-answer matched states) at the identified layers and test whether the model shifts to the correct coarser answer. (b) Patch the granularity representation from a coarse-answer run (elicited by a 'be approximate' prompt) into an overcommitment run and measure recovery of the correct coarse value. (c) Reverse control: inject the fine-precision direction to force overcommitment on demand. (d) Content control: patch only the content direction and confirm it does not change granularity. Metrics: recovery rate; that the correct value emerges at the coarse level rather than a new wrong fine value.", "7. Recovery intervention with specificity: Apply granularity-suppression steering on held-out overcommitment failures with dose control. Metrics: coarse-correct recovery rate; specificity—accuracy on (i) 200 items the model answers correctly at fine granularity (must not be coarsened; a critical control) and (ii) 200 unrelated facts, pre/post; fluency (held-out perplexity); recovered-per-unit-collateral-damage. Baselines: a 'be less specific / give the decade' re-prompt, DRAG-style uncertainty decoding, ROME editing (expected not to help since no fact is wrong), and a random-direction control.", '8. Transfer, generality, and source: Test whether a single granularity-suppression vector generalizes across relations and answer types (train on a subset, apply to held-out); repeat core analyses (2–7) on GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B for architecture-generality, reporting whether larger models overcommit less. Regress overcommitment rate on entity popularity, per-relation default granularity, and fine-value uncertainty to quantify the precision-prior mechanism.'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['Granularity and content signals may be entangled rather than cleanly separable; the causal patching/steering tests (Exp 6), not probe onset alone, are decisive, and a graded result would weaken the strong dissociation while remaining informative.', "Verifying the coarse value is 'stored' risks overestimation from prompt leakage; conservative content-probe thresholds and direct coarse-question verification with reported coverage mitigate this.", 'Granularity is naturally ordinal/graded, not discrete; we model it ordinally and report continuous granularity scores rather than forcing clean levels.', 'Granularity suppression could over-coarsen—making the model vague even when it knows the precise answer; the correctly-answered-fine specificity control (Exp 7) is central and utility may be capped even if the analysis holds.', 'The precision prior may be partly query-dependent (some phrasings legitimately demand specificity); the driver regression separates format cues from fine-value confidence rather than claiming full query-independence.', 'A single transferable suppression vector may not exist if granularity control is relation-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.', 'Multi-token coarse answers (e.g., month names, region names) complicate readout; primary analysis restricts to single-token-first answers with reported coverage.', 'Findings on 6–8B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget.']}

