Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "spontaneous_vs_provoked_confabulation", "Title": "Two Types of Confabulation: Why LLMs Refuse When Asked but Fabricate When They Speak Freely", "Short Hypothesis": "Borrowing the classic clinical double dissociation between provoked and spontaneous confabulation, we hypothesize LLMs have two distinct confabulation regimes with different internal mechanisms: (1) provoked confabulation, when directly queried about an unknown entity, is gated by the model's entity-recognition/knowledge-awareness signal (so it can refuse); (2) spontaneous confabulation, when the model freely generates extended text and fabricated facts appear unbidden, bypasses that same self-knowledge gate because generation-continuation pressure causally overrides it. The prediction: the model's own 'I don't know this' signal is present but causally disconnected during free generation, so an entity it would refuse to answer about when asked is confidently fabricated about when it produces the mention itself.", "Related Work": "The clinical literature (Kopelman 1987; Schnider et al. 1996) establishes a double dissociation between provoked confabulation (a normal gap-filling response to a faulty memory when questioned) and spontaneous confabulation (unbidden fabrication reflecting reality/temporal-monitoring failure). In LLMs, 'Do I Know This Entity?' (2024) finds causal entity-recognition directions that gate knowledge refusal under direct queries; 'LLMs Do NOT Really Know What They Don't Know' (2025) shows internal states track recall process not truth; answerability directions (2023) and factual self-awareness features (2025) are all measured under direct-query conditions. Crucially, every prior work studies the knowledge-awareness/refusal signal in the *provoked* setting (a question is posed). None test whether the same signal is *engaged versus bypassed* when the model spontaneously introduces an entity during free generation, nor establish a provoked-vs-spontaneous behavioral dissociation, nor localize why the self-knowledge gate fails to fire during generation momentum. This differs from all my prior proposals, which concern direct-query retrieval/expression; here the object is the generation *mode* (queried vs free) and its effect on whether self-knowledge causally gates output.", "Abstract": "Human neuropsychology distinguishes two confabulations: provoked (fabrication in response to a question, a normal reaction to faulty memory) and spontaneous (unbidden fabrication reflecting a failure to monitor reality). We ask whether large language models exhibit an analogous dissociation and whether the two arise from different mechanisms. LLMs are known to carry an internal entity-recognition signal that gates refusal when they are asked about an unknown entity. We hypothesize this gate is engaged when the model is provoked by a direct query but bypassed when the model spontaneously introduces an unknown entity mid-generation under coherence/continuation pressure, so a model that correctly refuses 'Who is <obscure name>?' will nonetheless fabricate a detailed biography for the same name when free-generating a passage that happens to mention it. We first document the behavioral dissociation: for matched unknown entities, compare fabrication rate and confidence across a provoked condition (direct query) and a spontaneous condition (free continuation where the entity is planted or self-introduced). We predict spontaneous fabrication far exceeds provoked fabrication for the same entities, above matched baselines. We then test the mechanism with a Location + Causal Intervention strategy: a linear/SAE entity-recognition probe should still fire ('unknown') during spontaneous fabrication, showing the self-knowledge signal is present but causally decoupled; activation patching and attention analysis should show that in the spontaneous regime the recognition direction fails to disrupt downstream attribute-extraction heads (unlike the provoked regime); and amplifying the recognition signal (or the attention path it acts through) at the entity position during free generation should re-engage the gate and convert spontaneous fabrication into appropriate hedging/refusal, with specificity controls preserving generation about known entities. Experiments span Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, and Gemma-2-9B, evaluating dissociation prevalence, probe firing under each regime, causal re-gating, and specificity.", "Experiments": ["1. Matched unknown-entity dataset: Build a set of clearly-unknown entities (nonexistent/fictional names, ultra-long-tail real entities the model demonstrably cannot recall) and a matched set of known entities. Validate unknown-status via direct probing and the entity-recognition probe. Restrict to entities the model refuses/hedges on under a direct query.", "2. Provoked vs spontaneous conditions: For each entity create (a) a provoked prompt (direct question: 'Who is X? / What did X do?') and (b) a spontaneous prompt where the entity is embedded in a free-generation task that gives continuation pressure (e.g., 'Write a short encyclopedia paragraph:' with the entity as the topic sentence, or a narrative where X is introduced and the model must continue). Measure per-entity fabrication rate (does the model assert specific false attributes vs hedge/refuse) and fabrication confidence (asserted-attribute token probabilities), scored by GPT-4o judging plus attribute-level ground truth for fictional entities (any specific attribute is a fabrication).", "3. Dissociation prevalence: Test whether, for the same unknown entities, spontaneous fabrication rate significantly exceeds provoked fabrication rate, against a null that treats fabrication as a single entity-difficulty signal independent of mode (paired McNemar test; logistic regression with mode x entity). Report per-entity-type breakdowns and a matched-known-entity control (should show high, appropriate assertion in both modes).", "4. Recognition-signal firing under both regimes: Using the entity-recognition direction (recovered via SAE features or a linear probe on the entity-token residual stream, following Do-I-Know-This-Entity methodology), measure whether the 'unknown' signal fires equally in the provoked and spontaneous regimes at the entity position. Hypothesis: the signal fires in both (self-knowledge is present), establishing that spontaneous fabrication is a gate-bypass, not a recognition failure. Metric: per-layer probe AUROC/activation for unknown-vs-known, compared across modes; label-shuffle control.", "5. Causal decoupling test via attention analysis and patching: In the provoked regime the recognition direction is known to disrupt downstream attribute-extraction heads (suppressing hallucination/enabling refusal). Test whether in the spontaneous regime this disruption is weakened: (a) measure attention of attribute-extraction heads to the entity token in each mode; (b) activation-patch the entity-token recognition representation from a provoked (refusing) run into a spontaneous (fabricating) run and test whether fabrication is suppressed; (c) reverse control: patch from spontaneous into provoked. This localizes whether the gate is disconnected in free generation.", "6. Re-gating intervention: Amplify the entity-recognition 'unknown' direction (or restore its attention effect on attribute-extraction heads) at the entity position during free generation on held-out unknown entities. Metrics: conversion rate of spontaneous fabrication into hedging/refusal; specificity (generation about known entities must remain fluent and assertive; measure over-refusal rate and accuracy on 200 known entities pre/post); fluency (held-out perplexity). Compare against a generic 'only state what you are sure of' system-prompt baseline, refusal fine-tuning, and a random-direction control.", "7. Continuation-pressure dose-response: Vary the strength of generation-continuation pressure (position of the entity in the passage, how committed the prior text is to elaborating, sampling temperature) and test whether spontaneous fabrication scales with continuation pressure while the recognition signal stays constant, supporting the momentum-overrides-gate account.", "8. Transfer and generality: Test whether a single re-gating vector generalizes across entity types; repeat core analyses (3-6) on Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, and Gemma-2-9B; report whether larger models close the provoked-vs-spontaneous gap (better reality monitoring)."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["The provoked and spontaneous conditions differ in prompt format, so the dissociation could be a format artifact rather than a genuine mode effect; matched-known-entity controls, the single-signal null (Exp 3), and the constant-recognition-signal finding (Exp 4) are essential to attribute the effect to generation mode.", "The recognition signal might in fact fire less in the spontaneous regime (a recognition failure, not a gate bypass); Exp 4 directly tests this and either outcome is informative, but it could weaken the specific bypass claim.", "The recognition direction and its attention effect may be entangled/distributed; the causal patching and attention tests (Exp 5), not probe firing alone, are decisive, and graded results remain informative.", "Re-gating could cause over-refusal on known entities or degrade fluency; the known-entity specificity control and over-refusal metric are central and utility may be capped.", "Scoring 'fabrication' during free generation is noisy for real long-tail entities (some asserted facts may be true); using fictional/nonexistent entities where any specific attribute is a fabrication mitigates this, with reported coverage.", "A single transferable re-gating vector may not exist if the bypass is entity-type-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.", "Findings on 7-9B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget."]}}
