Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "scalar_incoherence", "Title": "Ordered but Incoherent: When LLMs Answer Comparisons Pairwise Instead of From a Shared Scalar, Breaking Transitivity", "Short Hypothesis": "LLMs can store a coherent scalar for an entity attribute in a linear subspace, yet many comparative-recall queries are answered not by reading that scalar but by an ad-hoc pairwise 'who-is-bigger' shortcut. Transitivity violations (A>B, B>C, yet C>=A) concentrate precisely on triples the model resolves via the pairwise shortcut rather than the scalar subspace, because pairwise decisions need not cohere into a global order. We hypothesize (1) whether a comparison is decided by the scalar subspace vs. the pairwise shortcut is decodable and gradable, (2) transitivity violations concentrate on shortcut-decided comparisons with small true scalar margins, and (3) forcing the answer to be read from the shared scalar subspace (steering/patching) restores transitivity without editing any stored attribute value.", "Related Work": "The Geometry of Numerical Reasoning (2024) shows LLMs extract numeric attributes into linear subspaces and that causal intervention in those subspaces alters comparison answers; Interpreting Multi-Attribute Confounding (2025) shows attributes share overlapping subspaces and are perturbed by irrelevant context. BERT IS-A transitivity (2022) and NLI/veridicality transitivity work show behavioral transitivity failure but offer no mechanism. Relational-reasoning-and-inductive-bias (2025) studies transitive inference via in-weights vs in-context circuits on synthetic ordinals, not stored real-world attributes. Rationality/transitivity-axiom benchmarks (2025) are purely behavioral. Crucially, all prior mechanistic work assumes comparisons are read from a single scalar subspace and does NOT ask whether the model instead uses a competing pairwise shortcut, does not test that transitivity violations are the signature of that shortcut, and does not show that forcing scalar-based reading restores global coherence. This reframes comparative-recall failure as a scalar-vs-pairwise arbitration problem, orthogonal to all my prior single-fact and set-level proposals.", "Abstract": "Large language models can answer 'Was Einstein born before Curie?' and encode entity attributes like birth year in linear subspaces, yet they violate transitivity: told nothing false, they will affirm A>B and B>C but deny A>C. Prior mechanistic work assumes comparisons are read from a single shared scalar subspace, which would guarantee transitivity, so it cannot explain why violations occur. We propose that comparative recall is not always scalar-based: for many pairs the model uses an ad-hoc pairwise 'who-is-bigger' shortcut driven by local lexical/frequency cues, and because pairwise decisions need not cohere, this shortcut is the source of transitivity violations. We test three claims on comparative queries over ordered attributes (birth/death year, latitude, elevation, population, physical size). (1) Dissociability: a probe over the attribute's scalar subspace predicts the correct comparison, while a separable 'pairwise-shortcut' signal predicts the model's answer independently of the scalar; whether a given comparison is scalar- or shortcut-decided is gradable via the alignment between the answer and the scalar-subspace projection. (2) Violation geometry: transitivity-violating triples concentrate on shortcut-decided comparisons with small true scalar margins, above frequency- and margin-matched baselines, and the scalar-margin/shortcut-strength imbalance predicts violations on held-out triples. (3) Causal repair: patching the answer to be read from the shared scalar subspace, or suppressing the pairwise-shortcut direction, restores transitivity and improves comparison accuracy with high specificity, whereas editing individual attribute values does not. Experiments span Llama-3-8B, Mistral-7B, and Qwen2.5-7B, evaluating probe separability, violation prevalence, causal recovery, and specificity.", "Experiments": ["1. Ordered-attribute dataset: Build comparison queries over entities with ground-truth scalar attributes from Wikidata across five attribute types (birth year, death year, latitude, elevation, city population, and commonsense physical size). For each attribute assemble many entity triples (X,Y,Z) with known true ordering, spanning small and large true scalar margins. Generate pairwise comparison queries ('Was X born before Y?' / 'Is X taller than Y?') in a fixed template plus paraphrases. Restrict to yes/no-answerable comparisons; report coverage.", "2. Transitivity-violation prevalence: For each triple, query all three pairwise comparisons and label a transitivity violation when the three answers form a cycle or contradict the true order despite each within-pair answer being individually plausible. Quantify violation rate per attribute and test whether it exceeds a null in which each pairwise answer is drawn independently from a single latent scalar with per-pair noise (fit per-entity difficulty; test residual cyclic structure). Report per-attribute and margin-binned breakdowns.", "3. Scalar-subspace vs shortcut probes: Following the numerical-geometry method, fit the attribute's scalar subspace via PLS/linear probing on entity representations, and define the scalar-based comparison prediction as the sign of the difference of scalar projections. Separately train a 'pairwise-answer' probe on the last-token residual stream at the comparison query to predict the model's actual answer. Metric: per-layer AUROC of each; via residualization, test whether the pairwise-answer signal carries information about the model's answer NOT explained by the scalar projection (evidence of a competing shortcut). Label-shuffle controls.", "4. Scalar-vs-shortcut arbitration measure: For each comparison, compute (a) the scalar margin (difference of scalar projections) and (b) alignment between the model's answer and the scalar-based prediction. Define shortcut-decided comparisons as those where the answer diverges from the scalar prediction or the scalar margin is near zero while the answer is confident. Test (regression/AUROC) whether transitivity violations concentrate on shortcut-decided, small-scalar-margin comparisons, controlling for entity frequency and lexical similarity.", "5. Causal test via patching/steering: (a) Force scalar-based reading: patch the comparison-answer position to be driven by the scalar-subspace difference (inject the scalar projections of both entities computed in isolation) and test whether the answer flips to the scalar-correct value and whether transitivity is restored across the triple. (b) Suppress the pairwise-shortcut direction (learned low-rank difference between shortcut-decided and scalar-decided matched states) and re-measure. (c) Reverse control: amplify the shortcut direction to induce violations on demand. Metrics: per-triple transitivity-restoration rate; comparison accuracy on shortcut-decided pairs.", "6. Repair intervention with specificity: Apply the scalar-reading steering/shortcut-suppression on held-out triples with dose control. Metrics: transitivity-violation reduction; comparison-accuracy gain on shortcut-decided pairs; specificity (accuracy on scalar-decided pairs the model already gets right must not drop; accuracy on 200 unrelated non-comparison facts pre/post); fluency (held-out perplexity). Baselines: a 'reason step by step / recall each value first' re-prompt, ROME editing of individual attribute values (expected not to help), and a random-direction control.", "7. Transfer and generality: Test whether a single scalar-reading/shortcut-suppression recipe generalizes across attributes (train on birth-year comparisons, apply to elevation/population); repeat core analyses (2-6) on Llama-3-8B, Mistral-7B, Qwen2.5-7B; report whether larger models rely more on the scalar subspace (fewer violations).", "8. Source analysis: Regress shortcut-reliance and violation rate on scalar margin, entity-pair co-occurrence/frequency (lexical 'who-is-bigger' cue availability), and attribute type, to quantify how much the shortcut is driven by local lexical/frequency cues rather than genuine scalar retrieval (the core mechanistic claim)."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["The scalar subspace and the model's answer may be so tightly coupled that a separable 'shortcut' signal is not identifiable; the residualization (Exp 3) and the causal induction of violations (Exp 5c) are the decisive tests, and a graded result remains informative.", "Transitivity violations could reflect genuine scalar-storage noise rather than a distinct shortcut; the margin-matched null (Exp 2) and shortcut-vs-scalar arbitration measure (Exp 4) are designed to separate these, but the distinction may be partly a continuum.", "Forcing scalar-based reading could degrade cases where the pairwise answer was actually correct, or hurt fluency; the scalar-decided specificity control (Exp 6) is central and utility may be capped.", "Fitting a reliable scalar subspace requires enough entities per attribute and is noisiest for physical-size/commonsense attributes; we report which attributes admit reliable subspaces and restrict primary analysis accordingly.", "A single transferable repair recipe may not exist if the shortcut is attribute-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.", "Comparison answers depend on template phrasing (larger vs smaller framing); we use paraphrases and report robustness, since framing itself may modulate shortcut reliance.", "Findings on 7-8B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget."]}}
