Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "acquisition_language_anchor", "Title": "Anchored to Where It Was Learned: A Per-Fact Acquisition-Language Anchor That Hides Cross-Lingual Knowledge", "Short Hypothesis": "Each fact carries a per-fact 'acquisition-language anchor' \u2014 the language in which the model predominantly encountered it \u2014 and cross-lingual recall is routed through that anchor's representation regardless of the query language. Many cross-lingual failures are anchor-override failures: when the query language differs from the fact's anchor, the model fails even though the fact is stored, and the failure direction is predicted by the anchor rather than by a fixed English hub. We hypothesize (1) the anchor is decodable from intermediate activations, (2) which query directions fail is predicted by anchor-vs-query mismatch above a fixed-English-hub baseline, and (3) suppressing anchor-language routing (or steering toward the query language earlier) recovers the fact without editing it.", "Related Work": "Prior mechanistic work shows multilingual LLMs recall facts through an English-centric pipeline then translate to the target language (Paths Not Taken 2025; Lost in Multilinguality 2025), and that cross-lingual inconsistency has a middle/final-layer bottleneck (Are Knowledge and Reference Cross-Lingually Consistent 2025). Beyond the Rosetta Stone (2025) shows unified-vs-separate representations form early in training and drive transfer. Language Models' Factuality Depends on the Language of Inquiry (2025) and ECLeKTic (2025) benchmark the phenomenon that a fact known in one language fails in another. Crucially, all mechanistic accounts privilege a fixed English hub and treat failure as insufficient engagement of the English mechanism or bad translation. None posit a per-fact, data-driven acquisition-language anchor that can be non-English, none show that the *direction* of failure (which query language fails) is predicted by anchor-vs-query mismatch better than a fixed-English-hub model, and none demonstrate recovery by suppressing anchor-language routing. This is orthogonal to all my prior (monolingual) proposals; it transfers my temporal_anchor_override logic from time to language: a query-independent default (the acquisition language) overrides the query-specified frame.", "Abstract": "Multilingual LLMs often 'know' a fact in one language yet fail to produce it in another. The prevailing mechanistic account routes all recall through an English-centric hub and blames failure on weak English engagement or faulty back-translation. We propose a different, per-fact account: each fact has an acquisition-language anchor \u2014 the language in which the model predominantly encountered it during training \u2014 and cross-lingual recall is routed through that anchor's representation regardless of query language. Under this view, whether a given query language succeeds is governed by anchor-vs-query mismatch, and failures are anchor-override failures: the fact is stored (recoverable in the anchor language) but the query language cannot access it. We test three claims across a controlled multilingual fact set (country-anchored entities, culturally-anchored facts, and Wikipedia-article-presence-derived items following ECLeKTic) with anchors that are frequently non-English. (1) Localization: a linear probe recovers a per-fact anchor-language signal from intermediate activations, distinct from the query-language signal. (2) Predictiveness: anchor-vs-query mismatch predicts which query directions fail, with higher AUROC than a fixed-English-hub baseline, and non-English-anchored facts show reverse-direction failures (fail in English, succeed in the anchor). (3) Causal recovery: suppressing anchor-language routing, or steering the language-transition earlier toward the query language, recovers stored facts with higher specificity and less collateral damage than fact editing or generic English-pivot prompting. Experiments span Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, and Gemma-2-9B, evaluating probe separability, mismatch-predicted failure geometry, causal recovery, and specificity.", "Experiments": ["1. Anchor-labeled dataset construction: Build a multilingual fact set where each fact has an estimable acquisition-language anchor. Sources: (a) country/culture-anchored entities (people, places, dishes) whose Wikipedia coverage is dominant in a specific non-English language, using per-language Wikipedia article length/presence as an anchor proxy following ECLeKTic; (b) Language Specific Knowledge items. For each fact create semantically equivalent queries in 5-8 languages (spanning scripts/families) plus verified translations. Restrict to single-token-first answers where needed; report coverage. Estimate a per-fact anchor as the language with dominant training-corpus coverage (Wikipedia-presence proxy; validated where possible on an open-data model).", "2. Reverse-direction prevalence: For each model measure per-language recall accuracy per fact. Test the core behavioral claim: non-English-anchored facts show reverse-direction failures (recalled in the anchor language, failed in English), against a fixed-English-hub null which predicts English should always be strongest. Metric: fraction of facts whose best-recall language is non-English and where English fails; compare to null.", "3. Anchor-vs-query mismatch predicts failure: For each (fact, query-language) pair, test whether the anchor-vs-query language mismatch predicts recall failure, with AUROC compared against (i) a fixed-English-hub predictor (mismatch = query != English) and (ii) frequency-only baselines (per-language fact frequency), controlling for answer frequency and script similarity via regression. Key result target: per-fact anchor mismatch beats the fixed-English-hub model.", "4. Anchor-signal localization: Train a linear probe on the last-token/subject-token residual stream at each layer to decode (a) the per-fact anchor language and (b) the query language. Metrics: per-layer AUROC for each; whether the anchor signal is decodable independently of query language (residualization for non-redundancy); the layer at which anchor-vs-query resolution occurs (relate to the known language-transition layers). Label-shuffle controls.", "5. Storage verification for override cases: For failing (fact, query-language) pairs, confirm the fact is stored via anchor-language recall and a linear content probe decoding the correct answer during the failing run. Only verified-stored cases count as anchor-override (hidden, not absent); report coverage.", "6. Causal test via patching/steering: On anchor-override failures, (a) patch the anchor-language routing representation from a successful anchor-language run into the failing query-language run at the identified transition layers and test recovery; (b) suppress the anchor-language direction (learned low-rank difference between anchor-dominant and query-dominant matched states) and re-measure; (c) steer the language-transition earlier toward the query language; (d) reverse control: inject the anchor-language direction to induce override on demand. Metrics: per-pair recovery rate; that the correct answer appears in the query language.", "7. Recovery intervention with specificity: Apply the best intervention on held-out override failures. Metrics: recovery rate; specificity (accuracy on facts already recalled correctly in the query language must not drop; accuracy on 200 unrelated facts pre/post); fluency (held-out perplexity per language); recovered-per-unit-collateral-damage. Baselines: the Paths-Not-Taken translation/recall vectors, English-pivot prompting ('answer in English then translate'), ROME editing (expected not to help since the fact is stored), and a random-direction control.", "8. Transfer, generality, and source: Test whether a single anchor-suppression/early-transition vector generalizes across relations and language pairs (train on a subset, apply to held-out); repeat core analyses (2-7) on Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B; report whether larger models have weaker anchoring. Regress override rate on anchor-vs-query Wikipedia-coverage ratio and script/family similarity to quantify the acquisition-language-anchor mechanism, validating the anchor proxy on an open-data model (OLMo) where training-corpus language distribution is inspectable."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["The acquisition-language anchor is estimated via a Wikipedia-coverage proxy, which may be inaccurate for closed models; we validate the proxy on an open-data model (OLMo) and report proxy limitations, treating the anchor as approximate.", "The anchor account may collapse to the fixed-English-hub account if most facts are in fact English-anchored; the deliberate inclusion of strongly non-English-anchored facts (Exp 1-2) and the AUROC comparison (Exp 3) are the decisive tests, and a partial improvement over the English-hub baseline is still informative.", "Anchor-language and query-language signals may be entangled; the causal patching/steering tests (Exp 6), not probe AUROC alone, are decisive, and graded results remain informative.", "Verifying a fact is 'stored' via anchor-language recall risks overestimation from surface entity-token leakage (shared names); we exclude facts with shared answer tokens across languages and use conservative content-probe thresholds, reporting coverage.", "Interventions could degrade fluency in the query language or over-recover (force English-derived answers awkwardly); per-language specificity and perplexity controls are central and utility may be capped.", "A single transferable intervention vector may not exist if anchoring is language-pair-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.", "Multi-token answers and cross-script tokenization complicate readout; primary analysis restricts to single-token-first answers with reported coverage.", "Findings on 7-9B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget."]}}
