Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "magnitude_prior_regression", "Title": "Regressing Toward the Typical: How a Compressed Magnitude Prior Distorts LLMs' Recall of Numeric Facts", "Short Hypothesis": "LLMs store numeric factual quantities (populations, distances, dates, masses, prices) but read them out through a query-independent compressed magnitude prior that pulls the answer toward the scale-typical value for that quantity type. Many numeric-recall errors are not missing facts but prior-regression distortions: the correct magnitude is internally decodable, yet the emitted number is systematically shrunk toward the category's typical order of magnitude. We hypothesize (1) this compression/regression is a gradable behavioral phenomenon whose distortion increases with the fact's distance from the category-typical magnitude, (2) the internally decodable magnitude is more accurate than the verbalized answer, and (3) suppressing the compression prior (or steering toward the decoded internal magnitude) recovers accurate values without editing any fact.", "Related Work": "Numeracy-probing work shows LLMs linearly encode log-magnitudes yet verbalize poorly ('LLMs Know More About Numbers than They Can Say', 2026), that number representations are sublinearly/logarithmically compressed like the human mental number line (Number Representations as Human Perception 2025; ALBERT numeracy 2024; Representational Geometry of Number 2026), and that digit-frequency (Benford) biases cause numerical hallucination (Benford's Curse 2025). Cognitive science documents systematic magnitude underestimation and log-to-linear shifts (Adults Underestimate Decimals 2022; Straightening Up 2011). Crucially, all prior work studies magnitude encoding/verbalization of *presented* numbers or digit-generation bias; none isolate a *prior-regression distortion of recalled factual quantities* \u2014 that the model shrinks a stored fact's magnitude toward its category-typical value \u2014 nor test that the correct value is internally hidden and recoverable by suppressing the compression prior. This is orthogonal to my prior proposals (all about entity/attribute fact retrieval, gating, competition), transferring the human production-estimation regression bias into LLM numeric factual recall with a causal mechanism and non-destructive fix.", "Abstract": "Large language models often produce a confidently wrong number when recalling a factual quantity \u2014 stating a city's population, a mountain's height, or a country's GDP with a magnitude that is off in a systematic direction. Prior interpretability work shows LLMs encode numeric magnitude on a compressed, logarithmic-like internal scale and know more about numbers than they verbalize, but does not connect this to errors in recalling real numeric facts. We propose magnitude-prior regression: when recalling a numeric fact, the model reads the stored magnitude out through a query-independent compressed prior that pulls the answer toward the typical order of magnitude for that quantity type, so atypically large or small facts are systematically distorted even when the correct value is internally available. We test three claims. (1) Behavioral signature: recalled-number error is directional and grows with the fact's log-distance from the category-typical magnitude \u2014 large values are underestimated, small values overestimated \u2014 mirroring human production-estimation regression, above frequency- and popularity-matched baselines. (2) Internal-vs-verbalized gap: a linear magnitude probe on intermediate activations recovers the fact's true magnitude more accurately than the model's verbalized answer, and the verbalized answer aligns with a shrunk-toward-prior version of the internal estimate. (3) Causal recovery: suppressing the compression-prior direction, or steering the readout toward the decoded internal magnitude, reduces regression distortion and recovers accurate values with high specificity, unlike a 'be precise' re-prompt or fact editing. Experiments span Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, and Mistral-7B on curated numeric-fact sets across quantity types, evaluating directional-distortion dose-response, probe-vs-verbalized accuracy, causal recovery, and specificity.", "Experiments": ["1. Numeric-fact dataset: From Wikidata build factual numeric-recall queries across quantity types with authoritative ground truth and wide within-category magnitude ranges: city/country population, mountain elevation, river length, country GDP/area, celestial-body distance/mass, historical event years, product prices. For each category compute the category-typical magnitude (median log-value) and each fact's log-distance from it. Generate direct queries ('What is the population of {city}?') plus paraphrases. Record the model's verbalized number.", "2. Directional-distortion dose-response: For each model measure signed relative error (log emitted minus log true) as a function of the fact's log-distance from the category-typical magnitude. Hypothesis: error correlates negatively with log-distance (large-magnitude facts underestimated, small overestimated \u2014 regression toward the category mean). Fit per-category regressions controlling for entity popularity, answer digit-length, and query length; test the slope against a null of unbiased noise. Report per-category dose-response curves and compare shape to the human production-estimation regression bias.", "3. Internal magnitude probe: Train a linear (log-magnitude) probe on the last-token/subject-token residual stream at each layer to decode the fact's true magnitude, following numeracy-probing methodology. Compare probe-decoded magnitude accuracy to the model's verbalized-answer accuracy on held-out facts. Hypothesis: the internal probe is systematically more accurate (less regressed) than the verbalized answer, and the verbalized answer equals a shrunk-toward-prior transform of the internal estimate (fit a shrinkage coefficient; test it is <1). Label-shuffle controls; restrict to facts where the probe generalizes to held-out entities.", "4. Compression-prior localization: Identify the direction(s) carrying the category-typical prior by contrasting activations for atypical vs typical facts, and test (via residualization) whether a 'prior' signal is separable from the fact-specific magnitude signal. Locate the layers where verbalized readout diverges from the internal estimate (logit-lens/tuned-lens over number tokens showing progressive shrinkage toward the prior across layers).", "5. Causal test via patching/steering: (a) Suppress the compression-prior direction (learned low-rank difference between prior-dominant and fact-magnitude-dominant matched states) at the identified layers and test whether the emitted number moves toward the true value. (b) Steer the readout toward the probe-decoded internal magnitude (inject the decoded log-magnitude direction). (c) Reverse control: amplify the prior direction to induce stronger regression on demand. (d) Content control: confirm suppression does not change non-numeric answers. Metrics: reduction in directional distortion; per-fact accuracy improvement on atypical facts.", "6. Recovery intervention with specificity: Apply the best intervention on held-out atypical facts with dose control. Metrics: relative-error reduction; specificity (accuracy on typical facts, which are already near-correct, must not degrade; accuracy on 200 unrelated non-numeric facts pre/post); fluency (held-out perplexity); recovered-per-unit-collateral-damage. Baselines: a 'give the precise value / think carefully' re-prompt, auxiliary-probe finetuning (per the numeracy paper), ROME editing of the fact (expected not to help since regression is systematic not fact-specific), and a random-direction control.", "7. Transfer and generality: Test whether a single prior-suppression/magnitude-steering recipe generalizes across quantity types (train on population facts, apply to elevation/GDP); repeat core analyses (2-6) on Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Mistral-7B; report whether larger models show weaker regression (less compression).", "8. Source analysis: Regress distortion magnitude on log-distance-from-typical, entity popularity, and (where feasible on an open-data model such as OLMo) the frequency distribution of the fact's magnitude in comparable text, to quantify how much regression is driven by the compressed prior versus tail-value obscurity (the core mechanistic claim)."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["Numeric-recall errors could reflect genuinely missing facts (tail obscurity) rather than prior regression; the internal-probe recovery (Exp 3) and log-distance dose-response controlling for popularity (Exp 2) are the decisive separators, and coverage is reported for facts where the probe generalizes.", "The compression-prior and fact-magnitude signals may be entangled; the causal suppression/steering tests (Exp 5), not probe accuracy alone, are decisive, and graded results remain informative.", "Prior suppression could over-correct \u2014 introducing wild large values or degrading typical-fact accuracy; the typical-fact specificity control (Exp 6) is central and utility may be capped even if the analysis holds.", "Multi-token number readout (large numbers span many tokens) complicates logit-lens and steering; primary analysis uses log-magnitude probes and order-of-magnitude accuracy metrics rather than exact-digit recovery, with reported coverage.", "Directional distortion may be small relative to noise for some categories; per-category dose-response and the paired within-category design maximize power, and a modest but significant regression slope is still informative.", "A single transferable intervention recipe may not exist if the prior is category-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.", "Frequency proxies for magnitude distribution are only inspectable on open-data models; we validate the source claim on OLMo and treat closed-model proxies as approximate.", "Findings on 7-8B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget."]}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'magnitude_prior_regression', 'Title': "Regressing Toward the Typical: How a Compressed Magnitude Prior Distorts LLMs' Recall of Numeric Facts", 'Short Hypothesis': "LLMs store numeric factual quantities (populations, distances, dates, masses, prices) but read them out through a query-independent compressed magnitude prior that pulls the answer toward the scale-typical value for that quantity type. Many numeric-recall errors are not missing facts but prior-regression distortions: the correct magnitude is internally decodable, yet the emitted number is systematically shrunk toward the category's typical order of magnitude. We hypothesize (1) this compression/regression is a gradable behavioral phenomenon whose distortion increases with the fact's distance from the category-typical magnitude, (2) the internally decodable magnitude is more accurate than the verbalized answer, and (3) suppressing the compression prior (or steering toward the decoded internal magnitude) recovers accurate values without editing any fact.", 'Related Work': "Numeracy-probing work shows LLMs linearly encode log-magnitudes yet verbalize poorly ('LLMs Know More About Numbers than They Can Say', 2026), that number representations are sublinearly/logarithmically compressed like the human mental number line (Number Representations as Human Perception 2025; ALBERT numeracy 2024; Representational Geometry of Number 2026), and that digit-frequency (Benford) biases cause numerical hallucination (Benford's Curse 2025). Cognitive science documents systematic magnitude underestimation and log-to-linear shifts (Adults Underestimate Decimals 2022; Straightening Up 2011). Crucially, all prior work studies magnitude encoding/verbalization of *presented* numbers or digit-generation bias; none isolate a *prior-regression distortion of recalled factual quantities* — that the model shrinks a stored fact's magnitude toward its category-typical value — nor test that the correct value is internally hidden and recoverable by suppressing the compression prior. This is orthogonal to my prior proposals (all about entity/attribute fact retrieval, gating, competition), transferring the human production-estimation regression bias into LLM numeric factual recall with a causal mechanism and non-destructive fix.", 'Abstract': "Large language models often produce a confidently wrong number when recalling a factual quantity — stating a city's population, a mountain's height, or a country's GDP with a magnitude that is off in a systematic direction. Prior interpretability work shows LLMs encode numeric magnitude on a compressed, logarithmic-like internal scale and know more about numbers than they verbalize, but does not connect this to errors in recalling real numeric facts. We propose magnitude-prior regression: when recalling a numeric fact, the model reads the stored magnitude out through a query-independent compressed prior that pulls the answer toward the typical order of magnitude for that quantity type, so atypically large or small facts are systematically distorted even when the correct value is internally available. We test three claims. (1) Behavioral signature: recalled-number error is directional and grows with the fact's log-distance from the category-typical magnitude — large values are underestimated, small values overestimated — mirroring human production-estimation regression, above frequency- and popularity-matched baselines. (2) Internal-vs-verbalized gap: a linear magnitude probe on intermediate activations recovers the fact's true magnitude more accurately than the model's verbalized answer, and the verbalized answer aligns with a shrunk-toward-prior version of the internal estimate. (3) Causal recovery: suppressing the compression-prior direction, or steering the readout toward the decoded internal magnitude, reduces regression distortion and recovers accurate values with high specificity, unlike a 'be precise' re-prompt or fact editing. Experiments span Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, and Mistral-7B on curated numeric-fact sets across quantity types, evaluating directional-distortion dose-response, probe-vs-verbalized accuracy, causal recovery, and specificity.", 'Experiments': ["1. Numeric-fact dataset: From Wikidata build factual numeric-recall queries across quantity types with authoritative ground truth and wide within-category magnitude ranges: city/country population, mountain elevation, river length, country GDP/area, celestial-body distance/mass, historical event years, product prices. For each category compute the category-typical magnitude (median log-value) and each fact's log-distance from it. Generate direct queries ('What is the population of {city}?') plus paraphrases. Record the model's verbalized number.", "2. Directional-distortion dose-response: For each model measure signed relative error (log emitted minus log true) as a function of the fact's log-distance from the category-typical magnitude. Hypothesis: error correlates negatively with log-distance (large-magnitude facts underestimated, small overestimated — regression toward the category mean). Fit per-category regressions controlling for entity popularity, answer digit-length, and query length; test the slope against a null of unbiased noise. Report per-category dose-response curves and compare shape to the human production-estimation regression bias.", "3. Internal magnitude probe: Train a linear (log-magnitude) probe on the last-token/subject-token residual stream at each layer to decode the fact's true magnitude, following numeracy-probing methodology. Compare probe-decoded magnitude accuracy to the model's verbalized-answer accuracy on held-out facts. Hypothesis: the internal probe is systematically more accurate (less regressed) than the verbalized answer, and the verbalized answer equals a shrunk-toward-prior transform of the internal estimate (fit a shrinkage coefficient; test it is <1). Label-shuffle controls; restrict to facts where the probe generalizes to held-out entities.", "4. Compression-prior localization: Identify the direction(s) carrying the category-typical prior by contrasting activations for atypical vs typical facts, and test (via residualization) whether a 'prior' signal is separable from the fact-specific magnitude signal. Locate the layers where verbalized readout diverges from the internal estimate (logit-lens/tuned-lens over number tokens showing progressive shrinkage toward the prior across layers).", '5. Causal test via patching/steering: (a) Suppress the compression-prior direction (learned low-rank difference between prior-dominant and fact-magnitude-dominant matched states) at the identified layers and test whether the emitted number moves toward the true value. (b) Steer the readout toward the probe-decoded internal magnitude (inject the decoded log-magnitude direction). (c) Reverse control: amplify the prior direction to induce stronger regression on demand. (d) Content control: confirm suppression does not change non-numeric answers. Metrics: reduction in directional distortion; per-fact accuracy improvement on atypical facts.', "6. Recovery intervention with specificity: Apply the best intervention on held-out atypical facts with dose control. Metrics: relative-error reduction; specificity (accuracy on typical facts, which are already near-correct, must not degrade; accuracy on 200 unrelated non-numeric facts pre/post); fluency (held-out perplexity); recovered-per-unit-collateral-damage. Baselines: a 'give the precise value / think carefully' re-prompt, auxiliary-probe finetuning (per the numeracy paper), ROME editing of the fact (expected not to help since regression is systematic not fact-specific), and a random-direction control.", '7. Transfer and generality: Test whether a single prior-suppression/magnitude-steering recipe generalizes across quantity types (train on population facts, apply to elevation/GDP); repeat core analyses (2-6) on Llama-3-8B, 