Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "persona_knowledge_suppression", "Title": "In Character, Out of Knowledge: How a Low-Competence Persona Suppresses Facts the Model Still Stores", "Short Hypothesis": "When an LLM adopts a low-competence or non-expert persona (e.g., 'answer as a young child', 'as a medieval peasant', 'as someone who failed science'), it fails factual queries it answers correctly in the default assistant voice, not because the fact is lost but because a persona-competence signal computed early in the network causally gates expression of the stored fact. We hypothesize (1) the correct answer remains linearly decodable from intermediate activations during persona failures (hidden, not erased), (2) a persona-competence direction predicts and causally drives suppression, and (3) suppressing this direction (not editing the fact) reopens the hidden knowledge while leaving the persona's stylistic behavior intact.", "Related Work": "Prior behavioral work shows low-knowledge personas (child, toddler, layperson) reduce factual accuracy (Prompting Science Report 4, 2025; LLMs downplay cognitive abilities, 2024), that persona assignment triggers abstentions and stereotype-driven errors (Bias Runs Deep, 2023), and that inquiry personas alter QA accuracy and cause hallucinated limitations/refusals (Who's Asking?, 2025). Mechanistic work localizes persona information to early MLP layers and identity-token positions (Dissecting Persona-Driven Reasoning, 2025; Localizing Persona Representations, 2025) and builds role/steering vectors that improve reasoning (Role Vectors, 2025; SRPS, 2025). Split Personality Training (2026) uses a second persona to read latent knowledge for auditing. Entity-cell work (Friends and Grandmothers in Silico, 2026) localizes and causally toggles stored facts. None of these test the specific claim that a competence-signaling persona causally *gates expression of a fact that is still stored*, that the correct answer stays decodable during persona failures, or that a targeted competence-direction suppression non-destructively reopens hidden knowledge while preserving persona style. This is orthogonal to all my prior single-turn retrieval/gating proposals: here the gating variable is the persona's implied competence, and the object is knowledge suppressed by role-play.", "Abstract": "Large language models will happily role-play as a young child, a medieval peasant, or someone who failed high-school science\u2014and when asked a factual question in such a low-competence persona, they often get it wrong even though the same model answers correctly as the default assistant. Prior work documents that low-knowledge personas degrade accuracy but treats this as a capability change. We propose instead that it is a knowledge-suppression failure: the fact remains stored and internally recoverable, but a persona-competence signal computed early in the network causally gates its expression. We test three claims on factual queries wrapped in personas of varying implied competence (expert, default, layperson, child, incompetent-character). (1) Hidden knowledge: on persona-induced failures, a linear probe on intermediate activations recovers the correct answer at rates far above the model's verbalized accuracy, showing the fact is present but unexpressed, above matched baselines. (2) Localization and drivers: a persona-competence direction, decodable early and separable (via residualization) from the fact-content signal, predicts suppression, and its strength scales with the persona's implied incompetence rather than with genuine fact difficulty. (3) Causal, style-preserving recovery: suppressing the persona-competence direction (or patching it from a default-voice run) reopens hidden facts with high specificity\u2014the persona's stylistic behavior is retained\u2014outperforming a 'be accurate' reminder and fact editing. Experiments span Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, and Gemma-2-9B on PopQA, T-REx, and a medical-fact subset, evaluating hidden-knowledge recovery, competence-direction prediction, causal recovery, style preservation, and specificity.", "Experiments": ["1. Persona-graded dataset construction: From PopQA/T-REx/EntityQuestions select facts the model answers correctly in the default assistant voice. Wrap each in a fixed template under five persona conditions spanning implied competence: (i) matched expert, (ii) default assistant (baseline), (iii) layperson, (iv) young child, (v) an in-character incompetent persona (e.g., 'a medieval peasant', 'someone who failed science'). Record per-condition verbalized accuracy. Restrict to single-token-first answers; report coverage.", "2. Suppression prevalence: Measure accuracy drop from default to each low-competence persona, and define persona-suppression cases as facts correct under default but wrong under a low-competence persona. Test that suppression scales monotonically with implied incompetence (ordinal regression), against a null in which persona only adds output-format noise. Include a control that varies persona *style* without competence (e.g., 'a cheerful assistant', 'a formal assistant') to show suppression tracks competence, not mere stylistic shift.", "3. Hidden-knowledge test: On suppression cases, train a linear probe on the last-token/subject-token residual stream to decode the correct answer, using held-out facts and label-shuffle controls. Metric: probe recovery accuracy vs verbalized accuracy under the low-competence persona; hypothesis: the correct answer is decodable at rates far above verbalized accuracy, and comparable to the default-voice probe, showing the fact is hidden not erased.", "4. Persona-competence direction localization: Contrast activations across persona-competence conditions to identify an early persona-competence direction; via residualization confirm it is separable from the fact-content signal (it should predict the persona's implied competence but not the specific fact). Regress the direction's activation on implied competence vs true fact difficulty (baseline default-voice confidence) to show it tracks the persona, not fact difficulty. Locate the layers/positions (expected: early MLP / identity-token position, per prior persona-localization work).", "5. Causal test via patching/steering: (a) Suppress the persona-competence direction (learned low-rank difference between low-competence and default states) at the identified layers on suppression cases and test whether the correct answer is expressed. (b) Patch the persona-competence representation from a default-voice run into a persona run. (c) Reverse control: inject the low-competence direction into a default-voice run to induce suppression on demand. (d) Content control: confirm suppression does not change the fact-content signal. Metrics: per-fact recovery rate; that recovery comes from the stored fact (matches the default-voice answer).", "6. Style-preserving recovery intervention with specificity: Apply the best intervention on held-out suppression cases with dose control. Metrics: hidden-fact recovery rate; STYLE PRESERVATION\u2014the response should still read as the persona (measured by a persona-classifier / GPT-4o judging that the output remains in character), distinguishing this from simply deleting the persona; specificity (accuracy on 200 unrelated facts and on facts the persona already answers correctly, pre/post); fluency (held-out perplexity). Baselines: a 'be accurate regardless of persona' reminder prompt, dropping the persona entirely, ROME editing (expected not to help), and a random-direction control.", "7. Transfer and generality: Test whether a single competence-suppression vector generalizes across personas and relations (train on a subset, apply to held-out); repeat core analyses (2\u20136) on Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B; report whether larger models suppress less.", "8. Safety-domain transfer: Replicate Exp 2\u20135 on a curated medical/clinical factual-recall subset (e.g., drug indications, symptom-disease facts) where users commonly self-describe with low-competence inquiry personas ('I'm just a worried parent with no medical background'), quantifying whether persona-induced suppression produces higher-stakes hidden-knowledge failures than in general-knowledge prompts."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["Persona-induced errors could reflect genuine strategy change (the model deliberately 'dumbs down') rather than knowledge suppression; the hidden-knowledge probe (Exp 3) and the causal, style-preserving recovery (Exp 6) are the decisive separators\u2014if recovering the fact necessarily breaks the persona, the gating claim is weakened but still informative.", "The persona-competence and fact-content signals may be entangled; the causal patching/steering tests (Exp 5), not probe accuracy alone, are decisive, and graded results remain informative.", "Style preservation and knowledge recovery may trade off (suppressing the competence direction could also erase persona style); the persona-classifier metric (Exp 6) quantifies this and utility may be capped if the two cannot be dissociated.", "Suppression prevalence may be modest for some persona/relation combinations; the ordinal competence gradient and paired within-fact design maximize power, and a significant monotonic effect is still informative.", "A single transferable competence-suppression vector may not exist if suppression is persona-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.", "The style-vs-competence control persona may imperfectly isolate competence from other persona attributes; multiple control personas and regression on implied competence mitigate this.", "Multi-token answers complicate readout; primary analysis restricts to single-token-first answers with reported coverage.", "Findings on 7\u20139B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget."]}}
