Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "prefetch_slot_override", "Title": "Reserved but Wrong: How Early Plan-Slot Prefetching Overrides Fresh Retrieval of Facts Stated Later in an Answer", "Short Hypothesis": "When an LLM plans a multi-fact answer, it reserves 'slots' for facts it will state later and prefetches a coarse placeholder for each slot before the generation cursor reaches it. We hypothesize a distinct failure mode\u2014prefetch-slot override\u2014in which a prematurely committed placeholder (a plausible but wrong entity, often the more typical/frequent one) overrides fresh, correct retrieval when the model actually reaches the slot, so the model states a fact it does not hold for the subject even though it would retrieve the correct value if that fact were queried first or in isolation. We predict (1) the reserved-slot placeholder is decodable in the residual stream before the slot is generated, (2) override errors concentrate on later slots where the placeholder was mis-prefetched while the correct value remains internally recoverable, and (3) suppressing the stale placeholder (not editing the fact) restores fresh retrieval of the correct value with high specificity.", "Related Work": "A growing body shows LLMs encode future-output attributes in advance: Emergent Response Planning (2025) probes future structure/content/behavior attributes; ParaScopes (2025) decodes paragraph-scale plans from the residual stream; Future Lens (2023) recovers subsequent tokens from a single hidden state; look-ahead work in chess (Jenner et al. 2024) shows future targets are represented and moved 'backward in time.' All of these establish that planning/lookahead information is *encoded and decodable*, and treat it as a capability. None ask whether an *early reserved slot for a specific upcoming fact can be filled with the wrong value that then overrides correct retrieval at generation time*, none identify this as a concrete factual-recall failure mode distinct from single-query retrieval failure, and none show a targeted intervention that suppresses the stale placeholder to recover the fact. This is also orthogonal to my prior proposals, which all concern single-query recall/gating/competition; here the object is a *within-answer, position-dependent* corruption caused by premature slot commitment during multi-fact generation, connecting the planning literature to knowledge-retrieval failures.", "Abstract": "Large language models increasingly produce answers packed with multiple facts about a subject, and recent work shows they encode plans for their future output in advance. We ask whether this planning has a dark side for factual accuracy. We propose prefetch-slot override: when generating a multi-fact answer, the model reserves slots for facts it will state later and prefetches a coarse placeholder for each slot before the generation cursor arrives; when the placeholder is a plausible-but-wrong value (often the category-typical or more frequent entity), it overrides fresh, correct retrieval at the slot, so the model asserts a fact it does not actually hold for the subject\u2014even though it would retrieve the correct value if that same fact were asked first or in isolation. We test three claims on templated multi-fact generation about entities. (1) Prefetch decodability: the reserved slot's placeholder value is linearly decodable from the residual stream several tokens before the slot is generated, at a position/layer distinct from where the earlier facts are being produced. (2) Override geometry: within-answer errors concentrate on later slots whose placeholder was mis-prefetched, while the correct value remains internally recoverable (via probing and via querying the fact first), above position- and frequency-matched baselines; the same fact is answered correctly in a first-slot or isolated position. (3) Causal repair: suppressing the stale placeholder direction, or patching the slot representation from an isolated-query run, restores the correct value with higher specificity and less collateral damage than fact editing or a 'double-check each fact' re-prompt. Experiments span Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, and Gemma-2-9B, evaluating prefetch-decoding AUROC, override prevalence and position dependence, causal recovery, and specificity.", "Experiments": ["1. Multi-fact generation dataset: From Wikidata build subjects that have several single-token-first attributes under distinct relations (e.g., person: birth city, nationality, occupation, notable-award; city: country, founding decade, mayor). For each subject construct a templated multi-fact prompt that elicits K facts in a fixed order ('Give a brief profile of {S}: birthplace, nationality, and field.'), plus (a) isolated single-fact queries for each attribute and (b) permuted-order versions that place each attribute in early vs late slots. Record per-slot ground truth and model outputs. Report coverage.", "2. Position-dependent override prevalence: For each attribute, compare accuracy when it appears in an early slot vs a late slot of the multi-fact answer and vs isolated query. Define prefetch-override errors as: attribute correct in isolation and/or early-slot but wrong in a later slot, where the wrong value is a plausible-but-incorrect entity (e.g., category-typical/frequent). Test that override rate increases with slot position, against a null in which each fact is retrieved independently regardless of position (mixed-effects logistic regression with slot-position x attribute; McNemar on paired early-vs-late items). Control for cumulative generation length and answer format.", "3. Storage verification: For override cases, confirm the correct value is stored via (i) isolated/first-slot recall and (ii) a linear content probe decoding the correct value from the residual stream during the failing multi-fact run at the pre-slot position. Only verified-stored cases count as override (hidden, not absent); report coverage.", "4. Prefetch-placeholder localization: Train a linear probe on the residual stream to decode the value the model will emit at slot k, evaluated at positions several tokens before slot k is generated (while earlier facts are still being produced). Metrics: per-layer/per-offset AUROC for decoding the (correct vs placeholder) slot value ahead of time; compare override cases (placeholder decodable early and differs from the isolated-query answer) vs correct cases. Establish that a stale placeholder is reserved in advance. Label-shuffle and offset controls.", "5. Causal test via patching/steering: (a) Suppress the stale-placeholder direction (learned low-rank difference between override and matched correct states) at the pre-slot positions/layers and test whether the emitted slot value flips to the correct one. (b) Patch the slot representation from an isolated-query run (where the fact is retrieved correctly) into the multi-fact run at the slot position and measure recovery. (c) Reverse control: inject the placeholder for an early slot to induce override on demand. (d) Content control: confirm suppression does not change earlier, already-correct slots. Metrics: per-slot recovery rate; that recovery matches the isolated-query answer.", "6. Repair intervention with specificity: Apply the best intervention on held-out override cases with dose control. Metrics: recovery rate; specificity\u2014accuracy on (i) earlier correct slots and (ii) 200 unrelated facts must not drop, pre/post; fluency (held-out perplexity); recovered-per-unit-collateral-damage. Baselines: a 'double-check each fact independently' re-prompt, re-querying each fact in isolation (upper bound), ROME editing (expected not to help since the fact is stored), and a random-direction control.", "7. Transfer, generality, and source: Test whether a single suppression/patch recipe generalizes across relations and slot positions (train on a subset, apply to held-out); repeat core analyses (2-6) on Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B; report whether larger models override less. Regress override rate on slot position, placeholder-vs-correct frequency gap (is the placeholder the more typical/frequent value?), and inter-slot semantic relatedness, to quantify whether override is driven by premature commitment to the category-typical value (the core mechanistic claim)."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["Position-dependent errors could arise from generic long-generation degradation or attention dilution rather than a specific stale-placeholder mechanism; the isolated-query control, permuted-order design (same fact early vs late), and the ahead-of-time placeholder decoding (Exp 4) are the decisive separators, and length controls are included.", "The prefetched placeholder and the correct-value signal may be entangled; the causal patching/suppression tests (Exp 5), not probe AUROC alone, are decisive, and graded results remain informative.", "Verifying a fact is 'stored' via isolated recall risks overestimation from format effects; conservative content-probe thresholds and first-slot recall as an additional check with reported coverage mitigate this.", "Suppression could corrupt earlier correct slots or degrade fluency; the earlier-slot and unrelated-fact specificity controls (Exp 6) are central and utility may be capped even if the analysis holds.", "Override may be rare relative to genuine ignorance for some attributes; the paired within-subject early-vs-late design maximizes power and a modest but significant position effect is still informative.", "A single transferable intervention recipe may not exist if prefetching is relation-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.", "Ahead-of-time decoding is noisiest when slots are close together; primary analysis uses prompts with clear inter-slot separation and single-token-first answers, with reported coverage.", "Findings on 7-9B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget."]}}
