ith higher specificity and less collateral damage than fact editing or a 'double-check each fact' re-prompt. Experiments span Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, and Gemma-2-9B, evaluating prefetch-decoding AUROC, override prevalence and position dependence, causal recovery, and specificity.", 'Experiments': ["1. Multi-fact generation dataset: From Wikidata build subjects that have several single-token-first attributes under distinct relations (e.g., person: birth city, nationality, occupation, notable-award; city: country, founding decade, mayor). For each subject construct a templated multi-fact prompt that elicits K facts in a fixed order ('Give a brief profile of {S}: birthplace, nationality, and field.'), plus (a) isolated single-fact queries for each attribute and (b) permuted-order versions that place each attribute in early vs late slots. Record per-slot ground truth and model outputs. Report coverage.", '2. Position-dependent override prevalence: For each attribute, compare accuracy when it appears in an early slot vs a late slot of the multi-fact answer and vs isolated query. Define prefetch-override errors as: attribute correct in isolation and/or early-slot but wrong in a later slot, where the wrong value is a plausible-but-incorrect entity (e.g., category-typical/frequent). Test that override rate increases with slot position, against a null in which each fact is retrieved independently regardless of position (mixed-effects logistic regression with slot-position x attribute; McNemar on paired early-vs-late items). Control for cumulative generation length and answer format.', '3. Storage verification: For override cases, confirm the correct value is stored via (i) isolated/first-slot recall and (ii) a linear content probe decoding the correct value from the residual stream during the failing multi-fact run at the pre-slot position. Only verified-stored cases count as override (hidden, not absent); report coverage.', '4. Prefetch-placeholder localization: Train a lin