Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "conditional_suppression_effect", "Title": "Suppressed by an Aside: Transferring the Human Conditional-Suppression Effect into LLMs and Locating the Premise-Gating Mechanism", "Short Hypothesis": "LLMs exhibit the classic human conditional-reasoning suppression effect: adding a logically-irrelevant *additional-condition* premise to a valid modus ponens / modus tollens argument suppresses the model's endorsement of the deductively valid conclusion, whereas adding an *alternative-condition* premise selectively suppresses the invalid inferences (denial-of-antecedent / affirmation-of-consequent) - exactly the double dissociation observed in humans. We hypothesize this is not a competence failure but a premise-gating failure: the model still 'derives' the valid conclusion internally (it is linearly decodable), but a distinct signal triggered by the extra premise gates its expression, and suppressing that signal restores the valid inference without editing the rule.", "Related Work": "Human cognitive science established the suppression effect (Byrne 1989; Bonnefon; Quelhas & Johnson-Laird 2016; 'Suppression of Inferences From Counterfactual Conditionals' 2020): a valid modus ponens is suppressed by an *additional* premise while invalid inferences are suppressed by an *alternative* premise - a precise double dissociation. In LLMs, mechanistic work on fact-vs-counterfactual competition (Ortu et al. 2024; Fakepedia 2023) and RULEBREAKERS (2024) shows models over-rigidly apply logic and mis-integrate world knowledge, and semantic-vs-symbolic reasoning work (2023) shows reliance on surface semantics. Crucially, none of these transfer the *conditional suppression effect* - the selective, premise-type-dependent suppression of a specific inference form by a logically-non-defeating additional premise - into LLMs, none test whether it reproduces the human additional-vs-alternative double dissociation, and none localize a premise-gating mechanism or show that the valid conclusion remains internally derivable but expression-gated. This is orthogonal to all my prior proposals, which concern factual/entity retrieval; here the object is defeasible conditional inference and how an incidental premise gates a correctly-computed logical conclusion.", "Abstract": "Since Byrne (1989), cognitive science has documented the suppression effect: people who correctly infer 'she got wet' from 'if she watered the plants she got wet' and 'she watered the plants' will stop endorsing that valid modus ponens conclusion once an additional premise ('if the sprinkler was on she got wet') is added - even though it does not logically defeat the inference - while an alternative premise instead suppresses the invalid inferences. We ask whether LLMs exhibit this human signature and, if so, what mechanism produces it. We first document the behavior: on controlled conditional-reasoning items (modus ponens, modus tollens, denial-of-antecedent, affirmation-of-consequent) we measure endorsement of each inference in a baseline two-premise form versus forms augmented with an additional-condition or an alternative-condition premise, testing whether LLMs reproduce the human additional-vs-alternative double dissociation and how it scales with premise plausibility and model size. We then test a mechanistic hypothesis with a Location + Causal Intervention strategy: the valid modus-ponens conclusion is still internally derived (linearly decodable from intermediate activations) even when the model behaviorally withholds it, and a distinct 'premise-gating' signal - triggered by the added premise and separable from the conclusion content - causally suppresses its expression. Activation patching from a baseline (no-extra-premise) run and subtraction of a learned premise-gating direction should restore the valid conclusion with high specificity, while the same direction should, when injected, induce suppression on demand. Experiments span Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, and Gemma-2-9B, evaluating suppression prevalence, the additional-vs-alternative dissociation, hidden-conclusion recovery, causal recovery, and specificity.", "Experiments": ["1. Suppression dataset construction: Adapt the classic Byrne/Quelhas conditional-reasoning paradigm into a controlled LLM item set. For each base conditional 'if A then C' with a categorical premise, build four inference probes: modus ponens (A -> C, valid), modus tollens (not C -> not A, valid), denial-of-antecedent (not A -> not C, invalid), affirmation-of-consequent (C -> A, invalid). Create three versions per item: (i) baseline (single conditional + categorical premise), (ii) +additional-condition premise ('if B then C', where B is a further requirement that does not defeat the inference), (iii) +alternative-condition premise ('if D then C', an alternative sufficient cause). Use both everyday-semantics and abstract/letter versions to separate world-knowledge from pure form. Query endorsement via a fixed yes/no/uncertain template plus paraphrases. Report coverage.", "2. Suppression prevalence and human double-dissociation test: For each model, measure endorsement rate of each of the four inferences across the three versions. Test the human signature: additional-condition premises should suppress the *valid* inferences (modus ponens/tollens) while alternative-condition premises should suppress the *invalid* inferences (DA/AC), a premise-type x inference-type interaction. Fit mixed-effects logistic regression with premise-type x inference-type x item; test the interaction against a null in which any added premise merely adds uniform noise. Report per-model effect sizes and whether abstract-form items show the same pattern (isolating form from semantics).", "3. Plausibility and scale dose-response: Vary the plausibility/salience of the added premise (how strongly B/D is a believable further condition or alternative cause) and test whether suppression scales monotonically with plausibility, mirroring the human graded effect. Compare across Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B (and, where feasible, a size series within one family) to test whether larger models suppress more or less - distinguishing 'more human-like defeasible reasoning' from 'over-rigid logic'.", "4. Hidden-conclusion test (Location): On suppression cases (valid inference endorsed in baseline but withheld under the additional premise), train a linear probe on the last-token/relevant-position residual stream to decode whether the valid conclusion C is internally derived. Metric: probe recovery of the valid conclusion during behavioral suppression vs. verbalized endorsement, with held-out items and label-shuffle controls. Hypothesis: the valid conclusion remains decodable at rates far above verbalized endorsement (derived but expression-gated), comparable to the baseline run.", "5. Premise-gating signal localization: Contrast activations between the additional-premise (suppressing) and baseline runs to identify a candidate 'premise-gating' direction; via residualization confirm it is separable from the conclusion-content signal (it should track the presence/plausibility of the added premise, not the specific conclusion). Locate the layers/positions (relate to attention over the added-premise tokens), and test whether the same direction is engaged by additional premises for valid inferences but by alternative premises for invalid inferences (mechanistic counterpart of the behavioral dissociation).", "6. Causal intervention: (a) Patch the conclusion-position residual stream from a baseline (no-extra-premise) run into a suppression run and test whether endorsement of the valid conclusion is restored. (b) Subtract the learned premise-gating direction and re-measure. (c) Reverse control: inject the gating direction into a baseline run to induce suppression on demand. (d) Content control: confirm the intervention does not change unrelated inferences. Metrics: per-item recovery rate, sign/dose-response, and that restored answers match the baseline valid conclusion.", "7. Recovery intervention with specificity: Apply the best intervention on held-out suppression cases with dose control. Metrics: valid-inference recovery rate; specificity - endorsement on genuinely defeasible items (where the added premise *should* reduce confidence, e.g. a true blocking condition) must NOT be indiscriminately restored, and accuracy on 200 unrelated reasoning/factual items must be unchanged, pre/post; fluency (held-out perplexity). Baselines: a 'reason strictly from the premises' re-prompt, chain-of-thought, and a random-direction control. This tests whether we selectively reopen logically-valid inferences without breaking legitimate defeasibility.", "8. Transfer and generality: Test whether a single premise-gating suppression vector generalizes across inference forms and item domains (train on a subset, apply to held-out); repeat core analyses (2-7) on all three models; report whether the mechanism is architecture-general. Optional safety transfer: replicate Exp 2-3 on clinical/procedural conditional rules (e.g. 'if symptom X and no contraindication, give treatment T'), where inappropriate suppression of a valid inference by an incidental aside could produce higher-stakes errors, on a curated safe subset."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["The behavioral suppression could be a genuine, appropriate defeasibility response rather than an unwanted gating of a valid inference; the abstract-form items (isolating logical form from world knowledge) and the additional-vs-alternative double-dissociation test (Exp 2) are the decisive separators - only the human-signature selectivity distinguishes suppression-effect from rational defeasance.", "LLMs might not reproduce the human double dissociation at all (e.g. suppress uniformly or over-rigidly apply logic per RULEBREAKERS); a null or divergent pattern is itself an informative cross-species comparison, though it would weaken the 'human-like suppression' framing.", "The premise-gating and conclusion-content signals may be entangled; the causal patching/injection tests (Exp 6), not probe decoding alone, are decisive, and graded results remain informative.", "The recovery intervention could indiscriminately restore inferences that *should* be suppressed (breaking legitimate defeasibility) or degrade fluency; the genuine-defeasibility specificity control (Exp 7) is central and utility may be capped even if the analysis holds.", "Endorsement is prompt-sensitive (yes/no/uncertain framing, phrasing); we use multiple paraphrases and report robustness, since framing itself may modulate suppression.", "A single transferable gating vector may not exist if suppression is inference-form-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.", "Findings on 7-9B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget."]}}
