Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "retrieval_enhanced_suggestibility", "Title": "Recalling a Fact Makes It Easier to Overwrite: Retrieval-Enhanced Suggestibility and a Prediction-Error Destabilization Signal in LLMs", "Short Hypothesis": "Transferring the human retrieval-enhanced-suggestibility (RES) / reconsolidation effect: having an LLM first correctly retrieve a fact within a session makes that specific fact MORE vulnerable to being overwritten by a subsequently presented piece of misinformation than if it had not been pre-retrieved, and this excess vulnerability is gated by prediction error at retrieval (larger when the retrieval was effortful/low-confidence or the misinformation is surprising). We hypothesize the extra corruptibility is carried by a transient, prediction-error-scaled 'destabilization' signal in the residual stream imprinted at the retrieval act, distinct from ordinary context-following, and causally suppressible so that pre-retrieval no longer increases suggestibility.", "Related Work": "Human memory research shows retrieval destabilizes a memory and requires reconsolidation (Function and mechanisms of memory destabilization, 2020), that prediction error/surprise at retrieval drives this destabilization and later false memories (Surprise and destabilize, 2018), and that an initial test increases later misinformation uptake\u2014Retrieval-Enhanced Suggestibility, RES (New Theory of Disuse predicts RES, 2017; pre-retrieval warnings, 2025). In LLMs, prior work studies answer-flipping under re-prompting via non-retrieval 'uncertainty heads' (Interpreting and Mitigating Unwanted Uncertainty, 2025), intra-memory conflict and stubbornness of dynamic facts (DynamicQA, 2024), catastrophic interference from contradictory updates during training (In Praise of Stubbornness, 2025), and representational stability of truth under perturbation (P-StaT, 2025; LLM Knowledge is Brittle, 2025). Crucially, none test the RES signature at inference time: that the *act of correctly retrieving* a fact earlier in a session *causally increases* that fact's susceptibility to a later in-context contradiction relative to a not-pre-retrieved control, none test the prediction-error gating of that excess vulnerability, and none localize a retrieval-induced destabilization signal. This is distinct from my prior proposals on retrieval-induced suppression (recalling competitors inhibits a related fact) and self-commitment lock-in (the model defends its own prior answer): here pre-retrieving the target makes it *more* changeable, not more inhibited or more entrenched, and the driver is prediction error, matching the human reconsolidation account rather than in-context entrainment or interference.", "Abstract": "Human memory has a counterintuitive property: retrieving a memory temporarily destabilizes it, so being tested on an event before encountering misinformation makes people MORE likely to adopt the misinformation\u2014retrieval-enhanced suggestibility (RES), driven by prediction error at retrieval. We ask whether large language models exhibit an analogous inference-time effect and, if so, what mechanism produces it. We propose that when an LLM correctly retrieves a fact earlier in a conversation, the act of retrieval leaves a transient destabilization signal that makes that specific fact more susceptible to being overwritten by a subsequently presented in-context contradiction than the same fact would be with no prior retrieval. We test three claims. (1) Behavioral RES signature: for facts the model answers correctly, we compare acceptance of a later planted contradiction between a pre-retrieval condition (model first correctly states the fact, then is exposed to misinformation, then re-queried) and a matched no-pre-retrieval control (equal-length filler instead of the retrieval), predicting higher contradiction-acceptance in the pre-retrieval condition\u2014and we further predict this excess scales with prediction error (larger when retrieval was low-confidence/effortful and when the misinformation is surprising). (2) Destabilization locus: a linear 'destabilization' signal imprinted at the retrieval act is decodable, scales with prediction error, and is separable from ordinary context-following. (3) Causal control: activation patching from the no-pre-retrieval control and suppression of the destabilization direction eliminate the excess suggestibility (making pre-retrieved facts no more corruptible than un-retrieved ones) with high specificity, whereas a 'trust your own knowledge' re-prompt does not. Experiments span Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, and Gemma-2-9B on PopQA, T-REx, and a medical-fact subset, evaluating RES prevalence, prediction-error dose-response, destabilization decoding, causal reduction, and specificity.", "Experiments": ["1. RES dataset and paired-condition design: From PopQA/T-REx/EntityQuestions select facts (subject, relation, correct answer a) the model answers correctly in isolation. For each fact create two matched session conditions holding total token length equal: (i) PRE-RETRIEVAL\u2014the model is first asked and correctly states a (retrieval act), then a misinformation snippet asserting a false value a_false is presented, then the fact is re-queried; (ii) NO-PRE-RETRIEVAL control\u2014an equal-length neutral filler replaces the initial question, then the same misinformation, then the query. Also include a NO-MISINFORMATION baseline (retrieval then filler then query) to confirm pre-retrieval alone does not change the answer. Restrict to single-token-first answers; report coverage.", "2. RES prevalence (core behavioral test): Measure contradiction-acceptance rate (model now answers a_false) in the pre-retrieval vs no-pre-retrieval conditions, using paired McNemar / mixed-effects logistic regression with condition x fact. Hypothesis (RES): pre-retrieval yields HIGHER acceptance of misinformation than the no-pre-retrieval control, against a null that pre-retrieval is irrelevant or (per lock-in intuition) protective. Report per-relation breakdowns and the sign/size of the RES effect. A protective or null result is itself an informative cross-species comparison distinguishing LLM memory from human RES.", "3. Prediction-error dose-response: Operationalize prediction error two ways: (a) retrieval effort\u2014the model's confidence/margin when it initially retrieves a (low-confidence, effortful retrieval predicts stronger destabilization); (b) misinformation surprise\u2014the implausibility/log-prob of a_false given the subject. Test whether the excess suggestibility (pre-retrieval minus control acceptance) rises with both, mirroring the human surprise-drives-reconsolidation finding. Report dose-response curves and regression coefficients; include a low-surprise (plausible a_false) vs high-surprise condition.", "4. Storage/transience check: Confirm the corruption is a session-level destabilization, not permanent: re-query the same fact in a fresh session after a pre-retrieval-corruption trial and verify a is restored (transient, reconsolidation-like), distinguishing RES from genuine belief change. Also verify via a content probe that a remains internally decodable at the point of corrupted output. Report coverage.", "5. Destabilization-signal localization: Contrast activations at the re-query position between pre-retrieval and no-pre-retrieval conditions to identify a candidate 'destabilization' direction; via residualization confirm it is separable from a generic context-following/misinformation-attention signal (it should track prediction error and the presence of a prior retrieval act, not just the presence of a_false in context). Probe whether the signal magnitude at the retrieval act predicts later corruption (per-layer AUROC), and relate it to attention over the misinformation tokens. Label-shuffle controls.", "6. Causal test via patching/steering: (a) Patch the re-query residual stream from a no-pre-retrieval control run into a pre-retrieval run and test whether excess suggestibility is removed (acceptance drops to control level). (b) Suppress the learned destabilization direction (low-rank difference between high- and low-destabilization matched states) at the retrieval act / re-query positions and re-measure. (c) Reverse control: inject the destabilization direction into a no-pre-retrieval run to induce excess suggestibility on demand. (d) Content control: confirm the intervention does not change unrelated facts. Metrics: reduction in pre-retrieval-minus-control acceptance; sign and dose-response.", "7. Mitigation intervention with specificity: Apply the best intervention across held-out facts. Metrics: RES elimination (pre-retrieved facts no more corruptible than un-retrieved); specificity (the model must still appropriately update on legitimately-corrective context\u2014measure acceptance of TRUE corrections that should be adopted, which must NOT be indiscriminately suppressed; accuracy on 200 unrelated facts pre/post); fluency (held-out perplexity). Baselines: a 'trust your own prior knowledge / ignore the passage' re-prompt, a pre-retrieval warning analogue (the human RES mitigation), the uncertainty-head masking method (2025), and a random-direction control.", "8. Transfer, generality, and safety: Test whether a single destabilization-suppression vector generalizes across relations (train on a subset, apply to held-out); repeat core analyses (2-6) on Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, reporting whether larger models show weaker RES. Safety transfer: replicate Exp 2-3 on a curated medical-fact subset (e.g., first asking a correct drug indication, then exposing a plausible-but-wrong indication) to quantify whether pre-retrieval makes high-stakes facts more corruptible than in general-knowledge prompts."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["The pre-retrieval effect could be ordinary context-following/entrainment rather than a genuine RES/reconsolidation signature; the equal-length no-pre-retrieval control (same misinformation, no prior retrieval), the prediction-error dose-response (Exp 3), and the transience check (Exp 4) are the decisive separators\u2014only the retrieval-act-dependent, prediction-error-scaled excess suggestibility qualifies as RES.", "LLMs may not reproduce the human RES effect at all (pre-retrieval could be protective, matching a self-commitment/lock-in intuition, or null); a protective or null result refutes the transfer but is itself an informative cross-species finding about how LLM memory dynamics differ from humans.", "Corruption could reflect the model rationally updating on new context rather than destabilization; the transience/fresh-session recovery (Exp 4) and content-probe evidence separate transient destabilization from genuine belief change, and the TRUE-correction specificity control (Exp 7) ensures mitigation does not block legitimate updates.", "The destabilization and generic context-following signals may be entangled; the causal patching/injection tests (Exp 6), not probe decoding alone, are decisive, and graded results remain informative.", "Prediction-error operationalizations (retrieval confidence, misinformation surprise) are proxies; we report multiple operationalizations and their consistency.", "A single transferable suppression vector may not exist if destabilization is relation-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.", "Multi-token answers complicate readout; primary analysis restricts to single-token-first answers with reported coverage.", "Findings on 7-9B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget."]}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'retrieval_enhanced_suggestibility', 'Title': 'Recalling a Fact Makes It Easier to Overwrite: Retrieval-Enhanced Suggestibility and a Prediction-Error Destabilization Signal in LLMs', 'Short Hypothesis': "Transferring the human retrieval-enhanced-suggestibility (RES) / reconsolidation effect: having an LLM first correctly retrieve a fact within a session makes that specific fact MORE vulnerable to being overwritten by a subsequently presented piece of misinformation than if it had not been pre-retrieved, and this excess vulnerability is gated by prediction error at retrieval (larger when the retrieval was effortful/low-confidence or the misinformation is surprising). We hypothesize the extra corruptibility is carried by a transient, prediction-error-scaled 'destabilization' signal in the residual stream imprinted at the retrieval act, distinct from ordinary context-following, and causally suppressible so that pre-retrieval no longer increases suggestibility.", 'Related Work': "Human memory research shows retrieval destabilizes a memory and requires reconsolidation (Function and mechanisms of memory destabilization, 2020), that prediction error/surprise at retrieval drives this destabilization and later false memories (Surprise and destabilize, 2018), and that an initial test increases later misinformation uptake—Retrieval-Enhanced Suggestibility, RES (New Theory of Disuse predicts RES, 2017; pre-retrieval warnings, 2025). In LLMs, prior work studies answer-flipping under re-prompting via non-retrieval 'uncertainty heads' (Interpreting and Mitigating Unwanted Uncertainty, 2025), intra-memory conflict and stubbornness of dynamic facts (DynamicQA, 2024), catastrophic interference from contradictory updates during training (In Praise of Stubbornness, 2025), and representational stability of truth under perturbation (P-StaT, 2025; LLM Knowledge is Brittle, 2025). Crucially, none test the RES signature at inference time: that the *act of correctly retrieving* a fact earlier in a session *causally increases* that fact's susceptibility to a later in-context contradiction relative to a not-pre-retrieved control, none test the prediction-error gating of that excess vulnerability, and none localize a retrieval-induced destabilization signal. This is distinct from my prior proposals on retrieval-induced suppression (recalling competitors inhibits a related fact) and self-commitment lock-in (the model defends its own prior answer): here pre-retrieving the target makes it *more* changeable, not more inhibited or more entrenched, and the driver is prediction error, matching the human reconsolidation account rather than in-context entrainment or interference.", 'Abstract': "Human memory has a counterintuitive property: retrieving a memory temporarily destabilizes it, so being tested on an event before encountering misinformation makes people MORE likely to adopt the misinformation—retrieval-enhanced suggestibility (RES), driven by prediction error at retrieval. We ask whether large language models exhibit an analogous inference-time effect and, if so, what mechanism produces it. We propose that when an LLM correctly retrieves a fact earlier in a conversation, the act of retrieval leaves a transient destabilization signal that makes that specific fact more susceptible to being overwritten by a subsequently presented in-context contradiction than the same fact would be with no prior retrieval. We test three claims. (1) Behavioral RES signature: for facts the model answers correctly, we compare acceptance of a later planted contradiction between a pre-retrieval condition (model first correctly states the fact, then is exposed to misinformation, then re-queried) and a matched no-pre-retrieval control (equal-length filler instead of the retrieval), predicting higher contradiction-acceptance in the pre-retrieval condition—and we further predict this excess scales with prediction error (larger when retrieval was low-confidence/effortful and when the misinformation is surprising). (2) Destabilization locus: a linear 'destabilization' signal imprinted at the retrieval act is decodable, scales with prediction error, and is separable from ordinary context-following. (3) Causal control: activation patching from the no-pre-retrieval control and suppression of the destabilization direction eliminate the excess suggestibility (making pre-retrieved facts no more corruptible than un-retrieved ones) with high specificity, whereas a 'trust your own knowledge' re-prompt does not. Experiments span Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, and Gemma-2-9B on PopQA, T-REx, and a medical-fact subset, evaluating RES prevalence, prediction-error dose-response, destabilization decoding, causal reduction, and specificity.", 'Experiments': ['1. RES dataset and paired-condition design: From PopQA/T-REx/EntityQuestions select facts (subject, relation, correct answer a) the model answers correctly in isolation. For each fact create two matched session conditions holding total token length equal: (i) PRE-RETRIEVAL—the model is first asked and correctly states a (retrieval act), then a misinformation snippet asserting a false value a_false is presented, then the fact is re-queried; (ii) NO-PRE-RETRIEVAL control—an equal-length neutral filler replaces the initial question, then the same misinformation, then the query. Also include a NO-MISINFORMATION baseline (retrieval then filler then query) to confirm pre-retrieval alone does not change the answer. Restrict to single-token-first answers; report coverage.', '2. RES prevalence (core behavioral test): Measure contradiction-acceptance rate (model now answers a_false) in the pre-retrieval vs no-pre-retrieval conditions, using paired McNemar / mixed-effects logistic regression with condition x fact. Hypothesis (RES): pre-retrieval yields HIGHER acceptance of misinformation than the no-pre-retrieval control, against a null that pre-retrieval is irrelevant or (per lock-in intuition) protective. Report per-relation breakdowns and the sign/size of the RES effect. A protective or null result is itself an informative cross-species comparison distinguishing LLM memory from human RES.', "3. Prediction-error dose-response: Operationalize prediction error two ways: (a) retrieval effort—the model's confidence/margin when it initially retrieves a (low-confidence, effortful retrieval predicts stronger destabilization); (b) misinformation surprise—the implausibility/log-prob of a_false given the subject. Test whether the excess suggestibility (pre-retrieval minus control acceptance) rises with both, mirroring the human surprise-drives-reconsolidation finding. Report dose-response curves and regression coefficients; include a low-surprise (plausible a_false) vs high-surprise condition.", '4. Storage/transience check: Confirm the corruption is a session-level destabilization, not permanent: re-query the same fact in a fresh session after a pre-retrieval-corruption trial and verify a is restored (transient, reconsolidation-like), distinguishing RES from genuine belief change. Also verify via a content probe that a remains internally decodable at the point of corrupted output. Report coverage.', "5. Destabilization-signal localization: Contrast activations at the re-query position between pre-retrieval and no-pre-retrieval conditions to identify a candidate 'destabilization' direction; via residualization confirm it is separable from a generic context-following/misinformation-attention signal (it should track prediction error and the presence of a prior retrieval act, not just the presence of a_false in context). Probe whether the signal magnitude at the retrieval act predicts later corruption (per-layer AUROC), and relate it to attention over the misinformation tokens. Label-shuffle controls.", '6. Causal test via patching/steering: (a) Patch the re-query residual stream from a no-pre-retrieval control run into a pre-retrieval run and test whether excess suggestibility is removed (acceptance drops to control level). (b) Suppress the learned destabilization direction (low-rank difference between high- and low-destabilization matched states) at the retrieval act / re-query positions and re-measure. (c) Reverse control: inject the destabilization direction into a no-pre-retrieval run to induce excess suggestibility on demand. (d) Content control: confirm the intervention does not change unrelated facts. Metrics: reduction in pre-retrieval-minus-control acceptance; sign and dose-response.', "7. Mitigation intervention with specificity: Apply the best intervention across held-out facts. Metrics: RES elimination (pre-retrieved facts no more corruptible than un-retrieved); specificity (the model must still appropriately update on legitimately-corrective context—measure acceptance of TRUE corrections that should be adopted, which must NOT be indiscriminately suppressed; accuracy on 200 unrelated facts pre/post); fluency (held-out perplexity). Baselines: a 'trust your own prior knowledge / ignore the passage' re-prompt, a pre-retrieval warning analogue (the human RES mitigation), the uncertainty-head masking method (2025), and a random-direction control.", '8. Transfer, generality, and safety: Test whether a single destabilization-suppression vector generalizes across relations (train on a subset, apply to held-out); repeat core analyses (2-6) on Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, reporting whether larger models show weaker RES. Safety transfer: replicate Exp 2-3 on a curated medical-fact subset (e.g., first asking a correct drug indication, then exposing a plausible-but-wrong indication) to quantify whether pre-retrieval makes high-stakes facts more corruptible than in general-knowledge prompts.'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['The pre-retrieval effect could be ordinary context-following/entrainment rather than a genuine RES/reconsolidation signature; the equal-length no-pre-retrieval control (same misinformation, no prior retrieval), the prediction-error dose-response (Exp 3), and the transience check (Exp 4) are the decisive separators—only the retrieval-act-dependent, prediction-error-scaled excess suggestibility qualifies as RES.', 'LLMs may not reproduce the human RES effect at all (pre-retrieval could be protective, matching a self-commitment/lock-in intuition, or null); a protective or null result refutes the transfer but is itself an informative cross-species finding about how LLM memory dynamics differ from humans.', 'Corruption could reflect the model rationally updating on new context rather than destabilization; the transience/fresh-session recovery (Exp 4) and content-probe evidence separate transient destabilization from genuine belief change, and the TRUE-correction specificity control (Exp 7) ensures mitigation does not block legitimate updates.', 'The destabilization and generic context-following signals may be entangled; the causal patching/injection tests (Exp 6), not probe decoding alone, are decisive, and graded results remain informative.', 'Prediction-error operationalizations (retrieval confidence, misinformation surprise) are proxies; we report multiple operationalizations and their consistency.', 'A single transferable suppression vector may not exist if destabilization is relation-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.', 'Multi-token answers complicate readout; primary analysis restricts to single-token-first answers with reported coverage.', 'Findings on 7-9B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget.']}

