Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "difficulty_effort_gate", "Title": "Fooled by Hard-Looking Questions: A Perceived-Difficulty Signal That Gates Retrieval Effort and Hides Easy Knowledge", "Short Hypothesis": "LLMs compute an early, linearly-encoded 'perceived difficulty' signal about a factual query that is driven substantially by surface complexity (query length, syntactic nesting, rare/technical vocabulary, hedge words) rather than by the actual knowledge state, and this signal causally gates how much retrieval effort/processing depth is allocated. A distinct failure mode is difficulty-driven under-retrieval: a fact the model stores and answers correctly under a simple phrasing is missed when the same fact is asked in a surface-complex way that inflates perceived difficulty, even though the query is semantically equivalent. We predict (1) the difficulty signal is decodable before retrieval and tracks surface complexity more than fact difficulty, (2) inflating perceived difficulty via surface complexity alone (holding the fact fixed) causes under-retrieval of stored facts, and (3) suppressing the difficulty direction restores the answer while preserving the model's appropriate hedging on genuinely hard items.", "Related Work": "Recent work shows LLMs linearly encode problem difficulty with identifiable causal attention heads (Probing the Difficulty Perception Mechanism, 2025) and that difficulty perception diverges from entropy; Concept Depth (2024) and Demystifying LLM Layers (2025) show complex concepts/reasoning recruit deeper layers while retrieval is shallow; 'know but don't tell' (2024) documents a probe-vs-generation gap for long-context position; Information Anxiety (2024) shows popularity reduces attention to constraint tokens. All of these characterize that a difficulty/depth signal exists or that a retrieval-vs-utilization gap exists, but none test that perceived difficulty is *driven by surface complexity independent of the fact* and *causally gates retrieval effort so that stored knowledge is under-retrieved on hard-looking-but-easy queries*, nor that suppressing the difficulty signal recovers the hidden answer while sparing genuine hedging. This also differs from my prior familiarity_before_retrieval proposal, which concerns a scalar coverage/'feeling-of-knowing' estimate about whether the answer is known; here the gating variable is the perceived *hardness of the query/computation*, driven by surface-complexity cues rather than content coverage, and the failure is a semantically-equivalent surface-complexity manipulation suppressing an otherwise-retrievable fact.", "Abstract": "Large language models are known to internally represent how difficult a problem is, encoded linearly with identifiable attention heads. We ask whether this perceived-difficulty signal has a causal downstream role in factual recall: does judging a query to be 'hard' make the model allocate less retrieval effort, so that a fact it stores goes unretrieved simply because the question looks difficult? We propose the difficulty-effort gate: perceived difficulty is computed early, is driven substantially by surface complexity (length, syntactic nesting, technical vocabulary, hedging) rather than by the true knowledge state, and gates how much processing depth is spent retrieving the answer. The signature failure is difficulty-driven under-retrieval: a fact answered correctly under a plain phrasing is missed when the semantically-equivalent query is dressed up to look hard. We test three claims on factual queries. (1) Localization and drivers: a linear difficulty probe is decodable before the answer, tracks surface-complexity cues more than actual fact difficulty (measured by baseline plain-phrasing accuracy), and is separable (via residualization) from a fact-content signal. (2) Causal gating: inflating surface complexity while holding the fact fixed raises the difficulty signal and reduces correct retrieval, and activation-patching the difficulty representation from a plain-phrasing run into a complex-phrasing run restores the answer; on under-retrieval failures the correct answer remains linearly decodable (hidden, not absent). (3) Suppression recovery: subtracting the difficulty direction recovers stored facts with high specificity, crucially preserving appropriate low confidence/hedging on genuinely hard items, and outperforming a 'take your time' re-prompt. Experiments span Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, and Gemma-2-9B on PopQA, T-REx, and EntityQuestions, evaluating probe drivers, under-retrieval prevalence, causal recovery, and specificity.", "Experiments": ["1. Surface-complexity paraphrase dataset: From PopQA/T-REx/EntityQuestions select facts the model answers correctly under a plain phrasing. For each, generate semantically-equivalent variants that manipulate surface complexity while preserving the queried fact: (a) plain baseline, (b) length-inflated (added irrelevant clauses), (c) syntactically nested, (d) technical/rare-vocabulary rephrasing, (e) hedge-laden framing ('this is a tricky one, but...'). Validate semantic equivalence via GPT-4o judging + human spot-checks and confirm the fact target is unchanged. Restrict to single-token-first answers; report coverage.", "2. Difficulty-probe localization and driver analysis: Train a linear difficulty probe on the last-token/subject-token residual stream at each layer (target: an ordinal surface-complexity level, and separately the model's own difficulty rating elicited by prompt). Metrics: per-layer AUROC/accuracy; onset layer (decodable before the answer token). Regress the probe output on (i) surface-complexity features and (ii) true fact difficulty (baseline plain-phrasing accuracy / entity popularity). Hypothesis: the difficulty signal tracks surface complexity more than true fact difficulty. Residualize the difficulty probe on a fact-content probe to confirm non-redundant, separable signals; label-shuffle controls.", "3. Under-retrieval prevalence: Measure per-fact accuracy across complexity variants. Define difficulty-driven under-retrieval as: correct under plain phrasing but wrong under a semantically-equivalent surface-complex phrasing. Quantify prevalence per complexity type against a null in which paraphrasing only adds output noise (paired McNemar; mixed-effects logistic regression with complexity x fact), controlling for query length and answer frequency. On under-retrieval failures, verify the fact is still stored via a linear content probe decoding the correct answer (hidden, not absent); report coverage.", "4. Causal gating test via patching: (a) Patch the difficulty representation from a plain-phrasing run into a matched complex-phrasing under-retrieval run at the difficulty-onset layers and test whether the correct answer is restored while the surface content of the query is unchanged. (b) Reverse control: inject the high-difficulty direction into a plain-phrasing run to induce under-retrieval on demand. (c) Ablate the identified difficulty attention heads (from prior difficulty-head work) and re-measure. (d) Content control: patch the content signal alone and confirm it does not change the difficulty gating. Metrics: per-fact recovery rate; that recovery matches the plain-phrasing answer.", "5. Depth-of-processing analysis: Using logit-lens/tuned-lens, measure the layer at which the correct answer first becomes the top candidate under plain vs complex phrasings. Hypothesis: high perceived difficulty delays or prevents the answer from surfacing (deeper/never-reached commitment), linking the difficulty signal to processing depth allocated to retrieval; correlate answer-onset layer with difficulty-probe magnitude.", "6. Suppression recovery with specificity: Learn a low-rank difficulty steering vector (mean activation difference between high- and low-difficulty matched states) and subtract it on held-out under-retrieval failures. Metrics: recovery rate; specificity crucially includes GENUINELY-HARD items (facts the model does not store, and legitimately complex questions) where suppression must NOT induce confident wrong answers or destroy appropriate hedging/abstention (measure over-confidence and false-answer rate pre/post); accuracy on 200 unrelated facts; fluency (held-out perplexity). Baselines: a 'take your time / this is simple, just answer' re-prompt, the difficulty-head ablation, ROME editing (expected not to help since the fact is stored), and a random-direction control.", "7. Transfer and generality: Test whether a single difficulty-suppression vector generalizes across relations and complexity types (train on a subset, apply to held-out); repeat core analyses (2-6) on Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B; report whether larger models are more robust (less surface-complexity-driven under-retrieval).", "8. Source analysis: Regress under-retrieval rate on each surface-complexity feature (length, nesting depth, rare-vocabulary count, hedge presence) and on true fact difficulty, to quantify how much the gate is driven by superficial query complexity rather than genuine hardness (the core mechanistic claim)."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["Surface-complex paraphrases may not be perfectly meaning-preserving; added clauses could introduce genuine ambiguity or distractor content rather than pure difficulty inflation, confounding under-retrieval with interference. Strict semantic-equivalence validation, the fact-target check, and length controls are essential, and the technical-vocabulary/hedging conditions (which add minimal new content) are the cleanest tests.", "The difficulty signal and fact-content signal may be entangled; the causal patching/ablation tests (Exp 4), not probe onset alone, are decisive, and a graded result would weaken the strong gating claim while remaining informative.", "Suppression could over-correct on genuinely hard/unknown items, converting appropriate hedging into confident hallucination; the genuinely-hard specificity control (Exp 6) is central and practical utility may be capped even if the analysis holds.", "Perceived difficulty may be partly legitimate (some complex phrasings truly are harder to parse); the driver regression separates surface-complexity from true fact difficulty rather than claiming difficulty perception is entirely spurious.", "Verifying a fact is 'stored' during under-retrieval risks overestimation from probe overfitting; held-out probes, conservative thresholds, and plain-phrasing recall as an additional check with reported coverage mitigate this.", "A single transferable difficulty-suppression vector may not exist if the gate is relation- or complexity-type-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.", "Multi-token answers complicate readout; primary analysis restricts to single-token-first answers with reported coverage.", "Findings on 7-9B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget."]}}
