Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "homograph_entity_fusion", "Title": "Same Name, Merged Minds: How Shared-Name Entities Fuse Into One Representation and Bleed Attributes Across Each Other", "Short Hypothesis": "When two distinct real-world entities share a name (homograph entities, e.g. the basketball player and the statistician both named 'Michael Jordan'), an LLM builds a single, fused subject representation keyed on the shared surface form, and this fusion causes a distinct, localizable failure mode\u2014attribute bleed\u2014in which the model asserts for one entity an attribute that actually belongs to the name-sharing other entity, even when it stores both facts separately. We hypothesize (1) the degree of representational fusion (how much the two entities' subject-token representations collapse) predicts attribute-bleed errors, (2) a distinct 'competing-namesake' signal is decodable at the subject position and is causally responsible for the bleed, and (3) injecting a disambiguating context-signal that separates the two entity representations recovers the correct attribute without editing any fact.", "Related Work": "Behavioral work shows LLMs merge information across same-name entities (AmbigDocs 2024; AmbER 2021; To Know or Not To Know 2024) but is largely in-context/multi-document, offers no internal mechanism, and no targeted fix. Entity-representation work shows entities form low-dimensional linearly-separable subspaces in early layers (On Entity Identification 2025; Entity Lens 2025) and that last-token subject representations undergo 'token erasure' as entity meaning is built (Token Erasure 2024)\u2014but studies clean, unambiguous entities, not name-collisions. Co-occurrence/popularity work (Impact of Co-occurrence 2023; Information Anxiety 2024; AmbER popularity bias) shows recall is skewed toward frequent associations but does not isolate cross-entity attribute fusion under a shared name. My prior proposals use type-matched but differently-named distractors (retrieval_competition), incidental mentions of a different entity (context_priming_contamination), or a false aside about a different attribute of the same entity (presupposition_writeback); none concern two genuinely distinct entities forced to share one surface form, nor the parametric fusion of their subject representations. This transfers the human source-monitoring/name-collision confusion into parametric recall with a causal representational-fusion account and a non-destructive disambiguation fix.", "Abstract": "Many distinct real-world entities share a name: two people named Michael Jordan, several cities named Springfield, multiple films titled Crash. We ask whether large language models store such homograph entities as cleanly separate memories or fuse them into one representation keyed on the shared surface form, and whether that fusion causes a specific factual-recall failure. We propose homograph entity fusion: because the model builds a subject representation largely from the entity's name tokens, two entities sharing a name collapse toward a single fused representation, causing attribute bleed\u2014the model asserts for one entity an attribute that actually belongs to the name-sharing other, even when it can produce both attributes when the entities are disambiguated. We test three claims. (1) Behavioral signature: for facts the model recalls correctly when the entity is uniquely specified (e.g. with a disambiguating descriptor), it produces cross-entity attribute bleed when the same query uses only the bare shared name, above popularity- and frequency-matched baselines, and the bleed is disproportionately toward the more popular namesake. (2) Representational fusion: a linear probe over the subject-token residual stream shows the two entities' representations are less separable for high-collision names, this fusion degree predicts attribute bleed, and a distinct 'competing-namesake' signal is decodable and causally drives the bleed (activation patching / ablation). (3) Disambiguation recovery: injecting a low-rank disambiguation direction that separates the two entity representations, or patching in a context-disambiguated subject representation, recovers the correct attribute with higher specificity and less collateral damage than fact editing or a 'be specific' re-prompt. Experiments span Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, and Gemma-2-9B on Wikipedia-disambiguation-derived homograph sets, evaluating bleed prevalence, fusion-predicts-bleed geometry, causal recovery, and specificity.", "Experiments": ["1. Homograph dataset construction: Using Wikipedia disambiguation pages (following AmbigDocs/AmbER methodology), build sets of two or more distinct entities that share an exact name (people, places, works), each with distinct attributes under matched relations (e.g. person: occupation, birth year, nationality). For each namesake record popularity (Wikipedia pageviews/article length) and per-entity ground-truth attributes. Create paired queries: (a) BARE-NAME ('What is the occupation of Michael Jordan?') and (b) DISAMBIGUATED ('...the statistician Michael Jordan?' / '...Michael Jordan, the AI researcher?'). Restrict to single-token-first answers; report coverage.", "2. Attribute-bleed prevalence: Restrict to (entity, attribute) pairs the model recalls correctly under DISAMBIGUATED queries. Measure, under BARE-NAME queries targeting a specific (usually less-popular) namesake, the rate at which the model returns the OTHER namesake's attribute (cross-entity bleed) vs the correct one vs other. Test against a null in which recall is independent of name-collision (compare to unique-name entities matched on popularity and frequency; logistic regression). Test the prediction that bleed is directional\u2014toward the more popular namesake\u2014and scales with the popularity gap.", "3. Representational-fusion geometry: For each homograph pair, train linear probes on the subject-token residual stream (across layers, following entity-identification/Entity-Lens methods) to (a) decode which specific namesake is intended and (b) decode each namesake's distinct attribute. Compute a fusion score = 1 minus the separability (e.g. probe AUC / subspace angle) between the two namesakes' subject representations. Hypothesis: fusion is higher for high-collision / high-popularity-gap names, and fusion score predicts per-item attribute-bleed rate (regression/AUROC). Compare to unique-name entities (should be cleanly separable). Label-shuffle controls.", "4. Competing-namesake signal localization: Contrast BARE-NAME (fused) vs DISAMBIGUATED (separated) runs at the subject position to identify a 'competing-namesake' direction; via residualization confirm it is separable from the correct-attribute content signal (it should track the presence of a name collision and the popular namesake, not the specific queried attribute). Locate the layers/positions where fusion is strongest (relate to the early-layer entity-formation / token-erasure window).", "5. Causal test via patching/ablation: (a) Patch the subject-token representation from a DISAMBIGUATED run (where the intended namesake is correctly recalled) into a BARE-NAME bleed run and test whether the correct attribute is restored. (b) Suppress the competing-namesake direction (learned low-rank difference between fused and separated states) and re-measure bleed. (c) Reverse control: inject the popular-namesake's representation into a unique-name query to induce bleed on demand. (d) Content control: confirm the intervention does not change unrelated facts. Metrics: per-item recovery rate; recovery matches the DISAMBIGUATED answer.", "6. Disambiguation-recovery intervention with specificity: Learn a low-rank disambiguation steering vector (mean subject-token difference between the intended namesake's disambiguated state and the fused bare-name state) and apply it on held-out bleed cases. Metrics: correct-attribute recovery rate; specificity\u2014accuracy on the OTHER namesake's queries (must not be corrupted), on unique-name facts, and on already-correct bare-name facts, pre/post; that the model does not simply default to the popular namesake for genuinely ambiguous queries where it should hedge; fluency (held-out perplexity). Baselines: a 'which one do you mean / be specific' re-prompt, providing the disambiguating descriptor (upper bound), ROME editing (expected not to help since both facts are stored), and a random-direction control.", "7. Transfer and generality: Test whether a single disambiguation vector generalizes across homograph pairs and relations (train on a subset, apply to held-out); repeat core analyses (2\u20136) on Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B; report whether larger models fuse less (cleaner namesake separation).", "8. Source analysis: Regress attribute-bleed rate on the popularity gap between namesakes, the co-occurrence frequency of each namesake with its correct attribute, and the number of distinct namesakes sharing the name, to quantify how much bleed is driven by representational fusion toward the dominant namesake (the core mechanistic claim)."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["Bleed could reflect genuine ambiguity resolution (the bare name really is ambiguous, and defaulting to the popular namesake is reasonable) rather than a fusion failure; the key test is that the model recovers the correct attribute under DISAMBIGUATED queries (so the knowledge is separable-in-principle) and the specificity control that it should hedge when truly ambiguous\u2014distinguishing unwanted fusion from rational defaulting.", "The fusion and correct-attribute-content signals may be entangled; the causal patching/ablation tests (Exp 5), not probe separability alone, are decisive, and graded results remain informative.", "Disambiguation steering could over-correct\u2014forcing a namesake reading where the query is genuinely ambiguous, or corrupting the popular namesake's own recall; the other-namesake and hedging-appropriate specificity controls (Exp 6) are central and utility may be capped.", "Bleed may be rare for low-collision names or when the less-popular namesake is very obscure (genuine ignorance rather than fusion); the disambiguated-recall gating and popularity-matched baselines separate these, and coverage is reported.", "Shared names are often multi-token; subject-representation readout is cleanest for names with a stable last-token representation (per token-erasure work)\u2014we report coverage and restrict primary analysis accordingly.", "A single transferable disambiguation vector may not exist if fusion is name-pair-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.", "Findings on 7-9B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget."]}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'homograph_entity_fusion', 'Title': 'Same Name, Merged Minds: How Shared-Name Entities Fuse Into One Representation and Bleed Attributes Across Each Other', 'Short Hypothesis': "When two distinct real-world entities share a name (homograph entities, e.g. the basketball player and the statistician both named 'Michael Jordan'), an LLM builds a single, fused subject representation keyed on the shared surface form, and this fusion causes a distinct, localizable failure mode—attribute bleed—in which the model asserts for one entity an attribute that actually belongs to the name-sharing other entity, even when it stores both facts separately. We hypothesize (1) the degree of representational fusion (how much the two entities' subject-token representations collapse) predicts attribute-bleed errors, (2) a distinct 'competing-namesake' signal is decodable at the subject position and is causally responsible for the bleed, and (3) injecting a disambiguating context-signal that separates the two entity representations recovers the correct attribute without editing any fact.", 'Related Work': "Behavioral work shows LLMs merge information across same-name entities (AmbigDocs 2024; AmbER 2021; To Know or Not To Know 2024) but is largely in-context/multi-document, offers no internal mechanism, and no targeted fix. Entity-representation work shows entities form low-dimensional linearly-separable subspaces in early layers (On Entity Identification 2025; Entity Lens 2025) and that last-token subject representations undergo 'token erasure' as entity meaning is built (Token Erasure 2024)—but studies clean, unambiguous entities, not name-collisions. Co-occurrence/popularity work (Impact of Co-occurrence 2023; Information Anxiety 2024; AmbER popularity bias) shows recall is skewed toward frequent associations but does not isolate cross-entity attribute fusion under a shared name. My prior proposals use type-matched but differently-named distractors (retrieval_competition), incidental mentions of a different entity (context_priming_contamination), or a false aside about a different attribute of the same entity (presupposition_writeback); none concern two genuinely distinct entities forced to share one surface form, nor the parametric fusion of their subject representations. This transfers the human source-monitoring/name-collision confusion into parametric recall with a causal representational-fusion account and a non-destructive disambiguation fix.", 'Abstract': "Many distinct real-world entities share a name: two people named Michael Jordan, several cities named Springfield, multiple films titled Crash. We ask whether large language models store such homograph entities as cleanly separate memories or fuse them into one representation keyed on the shared surface form, and whether that fusion causes a specific factual-recall failure. We propose homograph entity fusion: because the model builds a subject representation largely from the entity's name tokens, two entities sharing a name collapse toward a single fused representation, causing attribute bleed—the model asserts for one entity an attribute that actually belongs to the name-sharing other, even when it can produce both attributes when the entities are disambiguated. We test three claims. (1) Behavioral signature: for facts the model recalls correctly when the entity is uniquely specified (e.g. with a disambiguating descriptor), it produces cross-entity attribute bleed when the same query uses only the bare shared name, above popularity- and frequency-matched baselines, and the bleed is disproportionately toward the more popular namesake. (2) Representational fusion: a linear probe over the subject-token residual stream shows the two entities' representations are less separable for high-collision names, this fusion degree predicts attribute bleed, and a distinct 'competing-namesake' signal is decodable and causally drives the bleed (activation patching / ablation). (3) Disambiguation recovery: injecting a low-rank disambiguation direction that separates the two entity representations, or patching in a context-disambiguated subject representation, recovers the correct attribute with higher specificity and less collateral damage than fact editing or a 'be specific' re-prompt. Experiments span Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, and Gemma-2-9B on Wikipedia-disambiguation-derived homograph sets, evaluating bleed prevalence, fusion-predicts-bleed geometry, causal recovery, and specificity.", 'Experiments': ["1. Homograph dataset construction: Using Wikipedia disambiguation pages (following AmbigDocs/AmbER methodology), build sets of two or more distinct entities that share an exact name (people, places, works), each with distinct attributes under matched relations (e.g. person: occupation, birth year, nationality). For each namesake record popularity (Wikipedia pageviews/article length) and per-entity ground-truth attributes. Create paired queries: (a) BARE-NAME ('What is the occupation of Michael Jordan?') and (b) DISAMBIGUATED ('...the statistician Michael Jordan?' / '...Michael Jordan, the AI researcher?'). Restrict to single-token-first answers; report coverage.", "2. Attribute-bleed prevalence: Restrict to (entity, attribute) pairs the model recalls correctly under DISAMBIGUATED queries. Measure, under BARE-NAME queries targeting a specific (usually less-popular) namesake, the rate at which the model returns the OTHER namesake's attribute (cross-entity bleed) vs the correct one vs other. Test against a null in which recall is independent of name-collision (compare to unique-name entities matched on popularity and frequency; logistic regression). Test the prediction that bleed is directional—toward the more popular namesake—and scales with the popularity gap.", "3. Representational-fusion geometry: For each homograph pair, train linear probes on the subject-token residual stream (across layers, following entity-identification/Entity-Lens methods) to (a) decode which specific namesake is intended and (b) decode each namesake's distinct attribute. Compute a fusion score = 1 minus the separability (e.g. probe AUC / subspace angle) between the two namesakes' subject representations. Hypothesis: fusion is higher for high-collision / high-popularity-gap names, and fusion score predicts per-item attribute-bleed rate (regression/AUROC). Compare to unique-name entities (should be cleanly separable). Label-shuffle controls.", "4. Competing-namesake signal localization: Contrast BARE-NAME (fused) vs DISAMBIGUATED (separated) runs at the subject position to identify a 'competing-namesake' direction; via residualization confirm it is separable from the correct-attribute content signal (it should track the presence of a name collision and the popular namesake, not the specific queried attribute). Locate the layers/positions where fusion is strongest (relate to the early-layer entity-formation / token-erasure window).", "5. Causal test via patching/ablation: (a) Patch the subject-token representation from a DISAMBIGUATED run (where the intended namesake is correctly recalled) into a BARE-NAME bleed run and test whether the correct attribute is restored. (b) Suppress the competing-namesake direction (learned low-rank difference between fused and separated states) and re-measure bleed. (c) Reverse control: inject the popular-namesake's representation into a unique-name query to induce bleed on demand. (d) Content control: confirm the intervention does not change unrelated facts. Metrics: per-item recovery rate; recovery matches the DISAMBIGUATED answer.", "6. Disambiguation-recovery intervention with specificity: Learn a low-rank disambiguation steering vector (mean subject-token difference between the intended namesake's disambiguated state and the fused bare-name state) and apply it on held-out bleed cases. Metrics: correct-attribute recovery rate; specificity—accuracy on the OTHER namesake's queries (must not be corrupted), on unique-name facts, and on already-correct bare-name facts, pre/post; that the model does not simply default to the popular namesake for genuinely ambiguous queries where it should hedge; fluency (held-out perplexity). Baselines: a 'which one do you mean / be specific' re-prompt, providing the disambiguating descriptor (upper bound), ROME editing (expected not to help since both facts are stored), and a random-direction control.", '7. Transfer and generality: Test whether a single disambiguation vector generalizes across homograph pairs and relations (train on a subset, apply to held-out); repeat core analyses (2–6) on Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B; report whether larger models fuse less (cleaner namesake separation).', '8. Source analysis: Regress attribute-bleed rate on the popularity gap between namesakes, the co-occurrence frequency of each namesake with its correct attribute, and the number of distinct namesakes sharing the name, to quantify how much bleed is driven by representational fusion toward the dominant namesake (the core mechanistic claim).'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['Bleed could reflect genuine ambiguity resolution (the bare name really is ambiguous, and defaulting to the popular namesake is reasonable) rather than a fusion failure; the key test is that the model recovers the correct attribute under DISAMBIGUATED queries (so the knowledge is separable-in-principle) and the specificity control that it should hedge when truly ambiguous—distinguishing unwanted fusion from rational defaulting.', 'The fusion and correct-attribute-content signals may be entangled; the causal patching/ablation tests (Exp 5), not probe separability alone, are decisive, and graded results remain informative.', "Disambiguation steering could over-correct—forcing a namesake reading where the query is genuinely ambiguous, or corrupting the popular namesake's own recall; the other-namesake and hedging-appropriate specificity controls (Exp 6) are central and utility may be capped.", 'Bleed may be rare for low-collision names or when the less-popular namesake is very obscure (genuine ignorance rather than fusion); the disambiguated-recall gating and popularity-matched baselines separate these, and coverage is reported.', 'Shared names are often multi-token; subject-representation readout is cleanest for names with a stable last-token representation (per token-erasure work)—we report coverage and restrict primary analysis accordingly.', 'A single transferable disambiguation vector may not exist if fusion is name-pair-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.', 'Findings on 7-9B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget.']}

