Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "parametric_binding_capacity", "Title": "Who Did What? A Working-Memory Binding-Error Signature in LLMs' Simultaneous Recall of Multiple Entities", "Short Hypothesis": "Transferring the human working-memory binding hypothesis (Oberauer 2019: capacity limits impair which-attribute-goes-with-which-item bindings, not item memory itself) into LLM parametric recall, we hypothesize that when an LLM must recall stored attributes of several entities at once, errors are dominated by BINDING errors\u2014correctly retrieving the pool of attributes but misassigning them across the co-queried entities (swapping A's attribute onto B)\u2014rather than by attribute loss, and that this binding-error rate rises with the number of co-queried entities like human set-size effects. We hypothesize a distinct attention-mediated entity-attribute binding pathway whose degradation causally produces the swaps, recoverable by strengthening that binding without editing any fact.", "Related Work": "Human WM research shows capacity limits specifically impair temporary bindings, not items: increasing set size raises binding errors (confusing which attribute belongs to which list item) while item memory is spared (Oberauer 2019 'WM Capacity Limits Memory for Bindings'; the Interference Model 2012/2023). In LLMs, PI-LLM / 'Unable to Forget' (2025) study proactive interference over IN-CONTEXT streamed key-value updates (the attributes are given in the prompt and later overwritten), and n-back capacity work (2023-2024) shows attention-entropy-driven capacity limits on in-context tasks. All existing LLM work provides the to-be-bound information in context; none study whether recalling PARAMETRICALLY-STORED attributes of multiple entities simultaneously produces the human binding-error signature (misassignment, not loss), whether it scales with the number of co-queried entities, or whether a specific attention binding pathway causally produces the swaps. This is also distinct from my prior proposals: retrieval_competition concerns candidate competition for a single entity; context_priming_contamination and homograph_entity_fusion concern bleed from a different or same-named entity; here the object is cross-entity attribute MISBINDING among several correctly-stored, distinctly-named entities held in working memory at once.", "Abstract": "A hallmark of human working memory is that its capacity limit does not erase items but corrupts their bindings: as more items are held, people increasingly confuse which attribute goes with which item, while rarely intruding attributes that were never in the set. We ask whether large language models exhibit this binding-error signature when recalling parametrically-stored facts about several entities simultaneously. We construct queries that ask for one attribute of each of k co-mentioned entities (e.g., 'Give the birth country of Marie Curie, Albert Einstein, and Alan Turing'), where the model reliably recalls each attribute in isolation. We predict three things. (1) Behavioral signature: as k grows, accuracy declines and the errors are dominated by cross-entity binding errors\u2014the model outputs an attribute that is correct for a DIFFERENT co-queried entity (a swap) far more than it outputs an attribute belonging to no queried entity (an extra-list intrusion) or omits it, mirroring the human within-set binding-error pattern, above frequency- and length-matched baselines. (2) Mechanism: a swap is a binding failure, not a retrieval failure\u2014the correct attribute pool is present (all correct attributes are decodable / appear somewhere in the output) but the entity-to-attribute assignment is scrambled; an attention pathway that binds each attribute to its subject token carries the assignment, and its degradation (measured by attention dispersion across the co-queried subjects, echoing n-back attention-entropy findings) predicts swaps. (3) Causal, non-destructive recovery: strengthening the entity-attribute binding attention (or patching the binding representation from a single-entity run) reduces swaps and recovers correct assignments with high specificity, outperforming a 'answer one at a time' re-prompt. Experiments span Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, and Gemma-2-9B on entity-attribute sets from PopQA/T-REx, evaluating set-size dose-response, error-type decomposition, attention-dispersion prediction, causal recovery, and specificity.", "Experiments": ["1. Multi-entity recall dataset: From PopQA/T-REx select entities with single-token-first attributes under a shared relation (e.g., person->birth country, city->country, element->group). For each set size k in {1,2,3,4,6,8}, build queries listing k distinct, differently-named entities and requesting the same attribute for each in a fixed output format ('For each, give their {relation}: E1, E2, ...'). Only include entities the model recalls correctly at k=1 (verified in isolation), so any error at k>1 is not simple ignorance. Match sets on entity popularity and total prompt length across k. Report coverage.", "2. Set-size dose-response (core signature): Measure per-entity accuracy as a function of k. Test whether accuracy declines with set size (mixed-effects logistic regression with k, controlling for prompt length and per-entity difficulty), replicating the human set-size effect and distinguishing it from a length-only null (compare to a matched control where k entities are queried but the prompt contains equal-length filler instead of additional entities).", "3. Error-type decomposition (the binding signature): Classify each error at k>1 as (a) SWAP\u2014the output attribute is correct for a different co-queried entity in the same set; (b) EXTRA-LIST INTRUSION\u2014the attribute belongs to no queried entity; (c) OMISSION/other. Test the human binding-hypothesis prediction that SWAPS dominate and rise with k while extra-list intrusions stay low (the diagnostic contrast: within-set confusion, not out-of-set intrusion), against a null in which errors are random attribute substitutions. Report the swap-to-intrusion ratio as a function of k.", "4. Attribute-pool-present test: For swap errors, verify the correct attribute pool is retrieved but misassigned: check whether all the set's correct attributes appear somewhere in the output (permutation of correct answers) more often than chance, and whether a linear probe at each subject's position still decodes that subject's correct attribute even when the emitted assignment is wrong. This establishes binding failure (assignment scrambled) vs retrieval failure (attribute absent). Report coverage.", "5. Attention-dispersion prediction: Identify attention heads at each attribute-output position that attend to the corresponding subject token (candidate entity-attribute binding heads). Measure binding-attention dispersion\u2014entropy of the answer position's attention over the co-queried subject tokens (following n-back attention-entropy methodology). Test whether higher dispersion predicts swap errors on held-out sets (AUROC), and whether dispersion rises with k, linking the capacity limit to attention spread.", "6. Causal test via patching/steering: (a) Patch the binding representation for a failing entity from a single-entity (k=1) run into the multi-entity run at that entity's answer position and test whether the correct attribute is assigned. (b) Sharpen binding attention\u2014upweight each answer position's attention to its own subject token and downweight others\u2014and measure swap reduction. (c) Reverse control: blur binding attention (increase dispersion) to induce swaps on demand. (d) Content control: confirm interventions do not change unrelated single-entity facts. Metrics: per-entity correct-assignment recovery; swap-rate change.", "7. Recovery intervention with specificity: Apply the best binding-strengthening intervention on held-out multi-entity queries with dose control. Metrics: swap reduction and accuracy gain at each k; specificity (accuracy on single-entity queries and on entities already correctly assigned must not drop; no new extra-list intrusions introduced); fluency (held-out perplexity). Baselines: an 'answer one entity at a time' re-prompt (upper bound), splitting into k separate queries, and a random-attention-edit control.", "8. Transfer, generality, and source: Test whether binding-strengthening generalizes across relations and set sizes (train on a subset, apply to held-out); repeat core analyses (2-6) on Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, reporting whether larger models have higher binding capacity (swaps rise more slowly with k). Regress swap rate on k, inter-entity semantic similarity (are similar entities confused more, per human similarity-based interference?), and attribute-frequency skew, to characterize the drivers of misbinding."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["Multi-entity queries add output-formatting demands that could cause errors unrelated to binding; the equal-length filler control (Exp 2), the swap-vs-intrusion diagnostic (Exp 3, which is specific to within-set confusion), and the attribute-pool-present test (Exp 4) are the decisive separators.", "Swaps could reflect output-ordering/copying artifacts rather than genuine misbinding; randomizing entity order, comparing free-order vs fixed-order outputs, and requiring the swap to be the correct answer for a specific co-queried entity mitigate this.", "The binding-attention signal and correct-attribute content may be entangled; the causal patching/attention tests (Exp 6), not attention-dispersion correlation alone, are decisive, and graded results remain informative.", "Binding-strengthening could over-focus attention and harm cases needing cross-entity context, or degrade fluency; the single-entity and already-correct specificity controls (Exp 7) are central and utility may be capped.", "LLMs may not reproduce the human dominance of swaps over intrusions (errors could be mostly out-of-set intrusions instead); a divergent pattern would refute the human-binding transfer but is itself an informative cross-species finding.", "A single transferable binding-strengthening recipe may not exist if binding is relation-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.", "Multi-token attributes complicate assignment scoring and readout; primary analysis restricts to single-token-first attributes with reported coverage.", "Findings on 7-9B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget."]}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'parametric_binding_capacity', 'Title': "Who Did What? A Working-Memory Binding-Error Signature in LLMs' Simultaneous Recall of Multiple Entities", 'Short Hypothesis': "Transferring the human working-memory binding hypothesis (Oberauer 2019: capacity limits impair which-attribute-goes-with-which-item bindings, not item memory itself) into LLM parametric recall, we hypothesize that when an LLM must recall stored attributes of several entities at once, errors are dominated by BINDING errors—correctly retrieving the pool of attributes but misassigning them across the co-queried entities (swapping A's attribute onto B)—rather than by attribute loss, and that this binding-error rate rises with the number of co-queried entities like human set-size effects. We hypothesize a distinct attention-mediated entity-attribute binding pathway whose degradation causally produces the swaps, recoverable by strengthening that binding without editing any fact.", 'Related Work': "Human WM research shows capacity limits specifically impair temporary bindings, not items: increasing set size raises binding errors (confusing which attribute belongs to which list item) while item memory is spared (Oberauer 2019 'WM Capacity Limits Memory for Bindings'; the Interference Model 2012/2023). In LLMs, PI-LLM / 'Unable to Forget' (2025) study proactive interference over IN-CONTEXT streamed key-value updates (the attributes are given in the prompt and later overwritten), and n-back capacity work (2023-2024) shows attention-entropy-driven capacity limits on in-context tasks. All existing LLM work provides the to-be-bound information in context; none study whether recalling PARAMETRICALLY-STORED attributes of multiple entities simultaneously produces the human binding-error signature (misassignment, not loss), whether it scales with the number of co-queried entities, or whether a specific attention binding pathway causally produces the swaps. This is also distinct from my prior proposals: retrieval_competition concerns candidate competition for a single entity; context_priming_contamination and homograph_entity_fusion concern bleed from a different or same-named entity; here the object is cross-entity attribute MISBINDING among several correctly-stored, distinctly-named entities held in working memory at once.", 'Abstract': "A hallmark of human working memory is that its capacity limit does not erase items but corrupts their bindings: as more items are held, people increasingly confuse which attribute goes with which item, while rarely intruding attributes that were never in the set. We ask whether large language models exhibit this binding-error signature when recalling parametrically-stored facts about several entities simultaneously. We construct queries that ask for one attribute of each of k co-mentioned entities (e.g., 'Give the birth country of Marie Curie, Albert Einstein, and Alan Turing'), where the model reliably recalls each attribute in isolation. We predict three things. (1) Behavioral signature: as k grows, accuracy declines and the errors are dominated by cross-entity binding errors—the model outputs an attribute that is correct for a DIFFERENT co-queried entity (a swap) far more than it outputs an attribute belonging to no queried entity (an extra-list intrusion) or omits it, mirroring the human within-set binding-error pattern, above frequency- and length-matched baselines. (2) Mechanism: a swap is a binding failure, not a retrieval failure—the correct attribute pool is present (all correct attributes are decodable / appear somewhere in the output) but the entity-to-attribute assignment is scrambled; an attention pathway that binds each attribute to its subject token carries the assignment, and its degradation (measured by attention dispersion across the co-queried subjects, echoing n-back attention-entropy findings) predicts swaps. (3) Causal, non-destructive recovery: strengthening the entity-attribute binding attention (or patching the binding representation from a single-entity run) reduces swaps and recovers correct assignments with high specificity, outperforming a 'answer one at a time' re-prompt. Experiments span Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, and Gemma-2-9B on entity-attribute sets from PopQA/T-REx, evaluating set-size dose-response, error-type decomposition, attention-dispersion prediction, causal recovery, and specificity.", 'Experiments': ["1. Multi-entity recall dataset: From PopQA/T-REx select entities with single-token-first attributes under a shared relation (e.g., person->birth country, city->country, element->group). For each set size k in {1,2,3,4,6,8}, build queries listing k distinct, differently-named entities and requesting the same attribute for each in a fixed output format ('For each, give their {relation}: E1, E2, ...'). Only include entities the model recalls correctly at k=1 (verified in isolation), so any error at k>1 is not simple ignorance. Match sets on entity popularity and total prompt length across k. Report coverage.", '2. Set-size dose-response (core signature): Measure per-entity accuracy as a function of k. Test whether accuracy declines with set size (mixed-effects logistic regression with k, controlling for prompt length and per-entity difficulty), replicating the human set-size effect and distinguishing it from a length-only null (compare to a matched control where k entities are queried but the prompt contains equal-length filler instead of additional entities).', '3. Error-type decomposition (the binding signature): Classify each error at k>1 as (a) SWAP—the output attribute is correct for a different co-queried entity in the same set; (b) EXTRA-LIST INTRUSION—the attribute belongs to no queried entity; (c) OMISSION/other. Test the human binding-hypothesis prediction that SWAPS dominate and rise with k while extra-list intrusions stay low (the diagnostic contrast: within-set confusion, not out-of-set intrusion), against a null in which errors are random attribute substitutions. Report the swap-to-intrusion ratio as a function of k.', "4. Attribute-pool-present test: For swap errors, verify the correct attribute pool is retrieved but misassigned: check whether all the set's correct attributes appear somewhere in the output (permutation of correct answers) more often than chance, and whether a linear probe at each subject's position still decodes that subject's correct attribute even when the emitted assignment is wrong. This establishes binding failure (assignment scrambled) vs retrieval failure (attribute absent). Report coverage.", "5. Attention-dispersion prediction: Identify attention heads at each attribute-output position that attend to the corresponding subject token (candidate entity-attribute binding heads). Measure binding-attention dispersion—entropy of the answer position's attention over the co-queried subject tokens (following n-back attention-entropy methodology). Test whether higher dispersion predicts swap errors on held-out sets (AUROC), and whether dispersion rises with k, linking the capacity limit to attention spread.", "6. Causal test via patching/steering: (a) Patch the binding representation for a failing entity from a single-entity (k=1) run into the multi-entity run at that entity's answer position and test whether the correct attribute is assigned. (b) Sharpen binding attention—upweight each answer position's attention to its own subject token and downweight others—and measure swap reduction. (c) Reverse control: blur binding attention (increase dispersion) to induce swaps on demand. (d) Content control: confirm interventions do not change unrelated single-entity facts. Metrics: per-entity correct-assignment recovery; swap-rate change.", "7. Recovery intervention with specificity: Apply the best binding-strengthening intervention on held-out multi-entity queries with dose control. Metrics: swap reduction and accuracy gain at each k; specificity (accuracy on single-entity queries and on entities already correctly assigned must not drop; no new extra-list intrusions introduced); fluency (held-out perplexity). Baselines: an 'answer one entity at a time' re-prompt (upper bound), splitting into k separate queries, and a random-attention-edit control.", '8. Transfer, generality, and source: Test whether binding-strengthening generalizes across relations and set sizes (train on a subset, apply to held-out); repeat core analyses (2-6) on Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, reporting whether larger models have higher binding capacity (swaps rise more slowly with k). Regress swap rate on k, inter-entity semantic similarity (are similar entities confused more, per human similarity-based interference?), and attribute-frequency skew, to characterize the drivers of misbinding.'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['Multi-entity queries add output-formatting demands that could cause errors unrelated to binding; the equal-length filler control (Exp 2), the swap-vs-intrusion diagnostic (Exp 3, which is specific to within-set confusion), and the attribute-pool-present test (Exp 4) are the decisive separators.', 'Swaps could reflect output-ordering/copying artifacts rather than genuine misbinding; randomizing entity order, comparing free-order vs fixed-order outputs, and requiring the swap to be the correct answer for a specific co-queried entity mitigate this.', 'The binding-attention signal and correct-attribute content may be entangled; the causal patching/attention tests (Exp 6), not attention-dispersion correlation alone, are decisive, and graded results remain informative.', 'Binding-strengthening could over-focus attention and harm cases needing cross-entity context, or degrade fluency; the single-entity and already-correct specificity controls (Exp 7) are central and utility may be capped.', 'LLMs may not reproduce the human dominance of swaps over intrusions (errors could be mostly out-of-set intrusions instead); a divergent pattern would refute the human-binding transfer but is itself an informative cross-species finding.', 'A single transferable binding-strengthening recipe may not exist if binding is relation-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.', 'Multi-token attributes complicate assignment scoring and readout; primary analysis restricts to single-token-first attributes with reported coverage.', 'Findings on 7-9B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget.']}

