Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "fluency_as_truth_heuristic",
  "Title": "Easy to Read, Easy to Believe: An Internal Retrieval-Fluency Signal LLMs Misuse as a Truth Cue",
  "Short Hypothesis": "Transferring the human fluency-as-truth heuristic into LLM factual verification: when judging whether a statement is true, LLMs partly rely on an internal retrieval-fluency signal (how quickly/easily the statement's content is processed) as evidence for truth, rather than solely on stored content. We hypothesize (1) verification errors are systematically biased so that fluently-processed false statements are over-accepted and disfluently-processed true statements are over-rejected, above matched baselines; (2) a linear 'retrieval-fluency' signal, decodable before the verdict and driven by surface/processing ease more than by the fact's actual truth, causally drives the verdict; and (3) suppressing this fluency direction (not editing any fact) selectively fixes fluency-induced verification errors while preserving correct verdicts.",
  "Related Work": "Human cognitive science robustly establishes that processing fluency and retrieval fluency are used as cues for truth and confidence, independent of actual validity (Epistemic Status of Processing Fluency 2010; Illusory Certainty / repetition-fluency 2024; Processing Fluency and Judgment 2016; Retrieval Fluency contributes to JoLs 2019). In LLMs, prior interpretability work encodes only truth/correctness-correlated signals: attention-to-constraint predicts factual accuracy (SAT Probe 2023), attention-head entropy predicts correctness (HEADENTROPY 2026), local intrinsic dimension separates truthful from untruthful generations (2024), and entropy/token-frequency neurons regulate confidence (Confidence Regulation Neurons 2024); Information Anxiety (2024) shows popular queries converge early internally. Idiom-recall work (2023) shows memorized content is promoted early (a fluency proxy). Crucially, all of these treat internal ease/confidence signals as (imperfect) indicators of truth; none test the reverse causal misuse — that an LLM treats its own retrieval fluency as EVIDENCE for truth, causing it to accept fluently-processed falsehoods and reject disfluently-processed truths in a verification task. This transfers the human fluency-as-truth heuristic into LLM verification with a causal fluency-signal locus and a non-destructive fix, and is orthogonal to my prior proposals, which all concern retrieval/expression of a target answer rather than verification of a presented statement.",
  "Abstract": "Humans judge statements they process fluently as more likely true — a fluency-as-truth heuristic that operates independently of the statement's actual validity. We ask whether large language models exhibit an analogous, mechanistically-localizable bias in factual verification. Existing interpretability work reads internal ease/confidence signals as indicators of truth; we test the reverse causal misuse: that an LLM treats how fluently it can process a statement as evidence for that statement's truth, causing it to over-accept fluently-processed falsehoods and over-reject disfluently-processed truths. We test three claims. (1) Behavioral signature: on true/false verification of factual statements, holding truth value fixed, manipulating processing fluency (via surface cues — rhyme/phonetic ease, high-frequency phrasing, prior in-context repetition, and lexical simplicity) shifts verdicts toward 'true' for fluent framings and 'false' for disfluent ones, producing directional errors above content-matched baselines. (2) Localization and drivers: a linear retrieval-fluency signal is decodable before the verdict, tracks surface/processing-ease cues more than the statement's actual truth (measured by plain-phrasing verification accuracy), and is separable (via residualization) from a content/truth signal. (3) Causal control: activation-patching the fluency representation from a disfluent-but-true (or fluent-but-false) matched run, and suppressing the fluency direction, remove the fluency-induced verdict bias with high specificity — preserving correct verdicts and appropriate uncertainty — whereas a 'ignore how it sounds, judge only the facts' re-prompt does not. Experiments span Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, and Gemma-2-9B on FEVER-style and curated true/false factual sets, evaluating fluency dose-response, verdict-bias geometry, causal removal, and specificity.",
  "Experiments": [
    "1. Fluency-manipulated verification dataset: Take factual statements with known truth labels (from FEVER, curated true/false sets over PopQA/T-REx facts). For each statement, generate semantically-equivalent framings that vary processing fluency while holding truth value and propositional content fixed: (a) plain baseline, (b) rhyming/phonetically-easy phrasing (transferring the classic 'rhyme-as-reason' fluency effect), (c) high-frequency lexical phrasing vs (d) rare/technical phrasing, and (e) prior in-context repetition (the statement appears once earlier as neutral text) vs first-exposure. Validate truth-value preservation and semantic equivalence via GPT-4o judging + human spot-checks. Elicit verdicts via a fixed true/false/uncertain template plus paraphrases. Report coverage.",
    "2. Fluency dose-response and directional-bias test (core signature): Measure verdict rates as a function of fluency manipulation, holding truth fixed. Hypothesis: fluent framings shift verdicts toward 'true' and disfluent framings toward 'false', regardless of actual truth, yielding two diagnostic error types — fluent-false over-acceptance and disfluent-true over-rejection — above a content-matched null in which framing only adds output noise (mixed-effects logistic regression with fluency x truth-value x item; paired McNemar on matched framings). Report signed verdict shift per fluency cue and per model.",
    "3. Repetition/truth-effect transfer: Test the human repetition-fluency truth effect: statements (both true and false) that appeared once earlier in context as neutral mentions should be judged 'true' more often than first-exposure matched statements, independent of truth value. Quantify the repetition-induced acceptance lift and test whether it interacts with the other fluency cues (as in the human illusory-certainty literature).",
    "4. Retrieval-fluency signal localization and drivers: Train a linear fluency probe on the last-token/statement-position residual stream at each layer, target = an ordinal processing-fluency level (and separately the model's self-rated ease, and a proxy: the layer at which the statement's key content is promoted, following idiom-recall methodology). Metrics: per-layer AUROC and onset layer (decodable before the verdict token). Regress the probe output on (i) surface fluency cues and (ii) actual truth value / plain-phrasing verification accuracy, predicting the fluency signal tracks processing ease more than truth. Residualize the fluency probe on a content/truth probe to confirm separable, non-redundant signals; label-shuffle controls.",
    "5. Causal test via patching/steering: (a) Patch the fluency representation from a disfluent-but-true matched run into a fluent-but-false run (and vice versa) at the fluency-onset layers and test whether the verdict flips toward the fluency-driven bias while content is unchanged. (b) Suppress the fluency direction (learned low-rank difference between high- and low-fluency matched states) and re-measure verdict bias. (c) Reverse control: inject the high-fluency direction to induce over-acceptance on demand (dose-response). (d) Content control: patch the content/truth signal alone and confirm it changes the verdict without changing the fluency-bias pattern. Metrics: per-item verdict-flip rates; sign and dose-response consistent with the fluency-as-truth account.",
    "6. Debiasing intervention with specificity: Learn a low-rank fluency steering vector and subtract it on held-out fluency-biased verification items. Metrics: reduction in fluent-false over-acceptance and disfluent-true over-rejection (the two directional errors), i.e. verdict accuracy conditioned on fluency cue moving toward the plain-baseline; specificity — verdicts on items where fluency and truth already agree must NOT degrade, appropriate 'uncertain' responses on genuinely ambiguous statements must be preserved, and accuracy on 200 unrelated verification items must be unchanged, pre/post; fluency-of-output/perplexity control. Baselines: an 'ignore how it sounds / judge only on facts' re-prompt, chain-of-thought verification, and a random-direction control.",
    "7. Transfer and generality: Test whether a single fluency-suppression vector generalizes across fluency-cue types and relations (train on a subset, apply to held-out); repeat core analyses (2-6) on Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B; report whether larger models are more robust (weaker fluency-as-truth bias).",
    "8. Safety-domain transfer: Replicate Exp 2-3 on a curated medical/health-claim verification subset (true and false claims about drug indications, symptoms, treatments) where fluent-but-false claims (e.g., catchy or frequently-repeated misinformation) could be dangerously over-accepted, quantifying whether the fluency-as-truth bias produces higher-stakes verification errors than in general-knowledge prompts."
  ],
  "Risk Factors and Limitations": [
    "Fluency manipulations (rhyme, rare vocabulary, repetition) risk altering meaning or introducing genuine ambiguity rather than pure processing ease; strict truth-value and semantic-equivalence validation, content-matched baselines, and using multiple independent fluency cues (so any shared effect isolates fluency, not one confound) are essential.",
    "LLMs might not reproduce the human directional bias (e.g., verdicts could be insensitive to surface fluency, or the effect could be pure repetition-copying); a null or divergent pattern would refute the transfer but is itself an informative cross-species finding, and the repetition cue is distinguished from verbatim copying by including both true and false repeated items.",
    "The fluency and content/truth signals may be entangled; the causal patching/steering tests (Exp 5), not probe onset alone, are decisive, and a graded/partial dissociation would weaken the strong claim while remaining informative.",
    "Debiasing could over-correct — making the model reject fluent-but-true statements or lose appropriate confidence; the fluency-truth-agree and genuine-uncertainty specificity controls (Exp 6) are central and utility may be capped even if the analysis holds.",
    "The fluency probe target (ordinal cue level / promotion layer / self-rated ease) is a proxy for internal retrieval fluency; we report multiple operationalizations and their consistency.",
    "A single transferable fluency-suppression vector may not exist if the bias is cue- or relation-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.",
    "Verification verdicts are prompt-sensitive (true/false/uncertain framing); we use multiple paraphrases and report robustness, since framing itself may modulate the bias.",
    "Findings on 7-9B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget."
  ]
}}
