Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "abstention_momentum", "Title": "Abstention Is Contagious: How One 'I Don't Know' Suppresses Knowledge on the Next Question", "Short Hypothesis": "Abstention in LLMs carries session-level momentum: once a model abstains ('I don't know') on one query, it becomes more likely to abstain on a subsequent, unrelated, and easily-answerable query in the same conversation, hiding knowledge it demonstrably holds. We hypothesize this is a distinct behavioral phenomenon\u2014abstention hysteresis\u2014carried by a transient, query-independent 'abstention-mode' signal imprinted by the prior abstention act, and causally suppressible so a prior abstention no longer contaminates later recall.", "Related Work": "Prior work treats each query's abstention/refusal decision as independent: entity-recognition directions gate knowledge refusal on the current entity (Do I Know This Entity, 2024), sparse ambiguity-encoding neurons control answer-vs-abstain for the current question (AENs, 2025), over-refusal benchmarks measure per-prompt refusal rates (OR-Bench, 2024; Do RALMs Know When They Don't Know, 2025), and abstention frameworks estimate per-query knowledge boundaries (ABCA, 2025; Know Your Limits survey, 2024). None test whether the *act of abstaining on one query causally biases the abstention decision on a later, independent query* within a session, whether this hides knowledge the model would otherwise express, or whether a transient abstention-mode signal carries this contamination. This transfers the human response-set perseveration / decision-hysteresis phenomenon into LLM knowledge expression, and is orthogonal to my prior single-turn gating proposals (familiarity, difficulty, stress, persona) which all manipulate the *current* query rather than a *prior abstention act*.", "Abstract": "Large language models increasingly operate over multi-turn conversations, deciding on each turn whether to answer or abstain. Existing work treats these abstention decisions as independent per-query events. We ask whether abstention is instead contagious: does the act of saying 'I don't know' on one query causally increase the model's propensity to abstain on a subsequent, unrelated, easily-answerable query in the same session\u2014thereby hiding knowledge the model demonstrably holds? We call this abstention hysteresis. We first document the behavior: for facts a model answers correctly in a fresh context, we compare later-query abstention rates between a condition where the model first genuinely abstained (on an unanswerable prior query) and matched controls where it first answered a question or saw equal-length filler. We predict elevated abstention (and reduced accuracy) after a prior abstention, above baselines, scaling with how many prior abstentions occurred and their recency. We then test the mechanism with a Location plus Causal Intervention strategy: a transient 'abstention-mode' signal imprinted by the prior abstention should be linearly decodable, separable (via residualization) from the current-query content signal, and\u2014during a contaminated failure\u2014the correct answer should remain internally decodable (hidden, not lost); activation patching from an answer-first run and suppression of the abstention-mode direction should restore correct answers with high specificity, while preserving genuinely appropriate abstention on truly unanswerable follow-ups. Experiments span Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, and Gemma-2-9B on PopQA/T-REx plus a medical subset, evaluating hysteresis prevalence, dose-response, hidden-knowledge recovery, causal recovery, and specificity.", "Experiments": ["1. Hysteresis dataset and paired-condition design: From PopQA/T-REx/EntityQuestions select target facts the model answers correctly in isolation. For each, build two-stage sessions holding total token length equal: (i) ABSTENTION-FIRST\u2014the model is first asked a genuinely unanswerable question (nonexistent entity / undefined attribute) on which it abstains, then asked the answerable target; (ii) ANSWER-FIRST control\u2014first asked an answerable unrelated question it answers, then the target; (iii) FILLER control\u2014equal-length neutral text, then the target. Verify the prior abstention actually occurred. Restrict targets to single-token-first answers; report coverage.", "2. Hysteresis prevalence (core behavioral test): Measure target-query abstention rate and accuracy across the three conditions. Test whether ABSTENTION-FIRST yields higher abstention / lower accuracy on the answerable target than the answer-first and filler controls (paired McNemar; mixed-effects logistic regression with condition x fact), against a null in which prior turns only add output noise. Report per-relation breakdowns and effect sizes. A null or protective result is itself an informative finding.", "3. Dose-response: Vary (a) the number of consecutive prior abstentions (1, 2, 3) and (b) recency (turns between the prior abstention and the target). Hypothesis: contamination rises with the number and recency of prior abstentions, mirroring response-set perseveration. Report dose-response curves controlling for cumulative length and per-target difficulty.", "4. Hidden-knowledge test: On contaminated failures (target abstained/wrong in ABSTENTION-FIRST but correct in isolation), train a linear content probe on intermediate residual-stream activations to decode the correct answer; compare to verbalized accuracy, with held-out facts and label-shuffle controls. Hypothesis: the correct answer is decodable at rates far above verbalized accuracy (hidden, not lost), comparable to the answer-first run.", "5. Abstention-mode signal localization: Contrast activations at the target query between ABSTENTION-FIRST and answer-first conditions to identify a transient 'abstention-mode' direction; via residualization confirm it is separable from the current-query content signal (it should track the presence/recency of prior abstention, not the specific fact). Locate the layers/positions (relate to attention over the prior abstention tokens and to known early abstention/ambiguity signals). Per-layer AUROC of the signal predicting target abstention; label-shuffle controls.", "6. Causal test via patching/steering: (a) Patch the target-query residual stream from an answer-first run into an abstention-first contaminated run and test whether the correct answer is restored. (b) Suppress the learned abstention-mode direction and re-measure. (c) Reverse control: inject the abstention-mode direction into an answer-first run to induce contamination on demand (dose-response). (d) Content control: confirm suppression does not change unrelated facts. Metrics: per-target recovery rate; recovery matches the isolation answer.", "7. Recovery intervention with specificity: Apply the best intervention on held-out contaminated cases with dose control. Metrics: recovery rate; specificity crucially preserves GENUINELY-APPROPRIATE abstention\u2014on truly unanswerable follow-ups, suppression must NOT force confident hallucination (measure false-answer rate on unanswerable items pre/post); accuracy on 200 unrelated answerable facts pre/post; fluency (held-out perplexity). Baselines: a 'treat each question independently' re-prompt, re-querying the target in a fresh context (upper bound), refusal-tuning, and a random-direction control.", "8. Transfer, generality, and safety: Test whether a single abstention-mode suppression vector generalizes across relations (train on a subset, apply to held-out); repeat core analyses (2\u20137) on Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, reporting whether larger models show weaker hysteresis. Safety transfer: replicate Exp 2\u20133 on a curated medical factual-recall subset, where an early appropriate abstention on an unknowable question could contaminate later correct clinical answers, quantifying higher-stakes hidden-knowledge suppression."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["The effect could be generic conversational drift or output-format entrainment rather than an abstention-specific momentum; the answer-first control (same prior-turn structure, no abstention) and equal-length filler control (Exp 2), plus the induce-on-demand test (Exp 6c), are the decisive separators.", "LLMs might not reproduce the hysteresis effect (per-query independence, or prior abstention could even prime helpfulness); a null or opposite result refutes the transfer but is an informative cross-species / behavioral finding.", "Contaminated abstention could reflect rational recalibration (the model updates downward on its own competence) rather than hidden-knowledge suppression; the content probe (Exp 4) and fresh-context recovery separate hidden from lost knowledge.", "The abstention-mode and content signals may be entangled; the causal patching/steering tests (Exp 6), not probe AUROC alone, are decisive, and graded results remain informative.", "Suppression could break genuinely-appropriate abstention on unanswerable follow-ups (inducing hallucination); the unanswerable-item false-answer specificity control (Exp 7) is central and utility may be capped.", "A single transferable suppression vector may not exist if hysteresis is relation-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.", "Multi-token answers complicate readout; primary analysis restricts to single-token-first answers with reported coverage.", "Findings on 7-9B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget."]}}
