Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "ordinal_access_chain", "Title": "Stored as a Chain, Queried by Position: Why LLMs Know the Sequence but Fail to Retrieve Its Nth Element", "Short Hypothesis": "LLMs store ordered real-world sequences (rankings, chronologies, alphabetical/positional lists, taxonomic orders) as a linked chain accessible primarily by neighbor-traversal, not as position-indexed slots. A distinct failure mode is ordinal-access failure: a model that reliably knows 'what comes after Y' and can recite the whole sequence nonetheless fails to answer 'what is the Nth element' by direct positional query, even though the element is internally recoverable. We hypothesize (1) whether a query is answered via a decodable neighbor-chain signal vs a position-index signal is separable, (2) ordinal-access errors concentrate on mid-sequence, high-N elements where chain traversal must span many steps, and (3) injecting a decoded 'running-position' signal (or patching from a recitation run) recovers the correct element without editing any stored fact.", "Related Work": "Beyond Memorization (2024) shows LLMs access parametric memory sequentially but struggle with RANDOM access, mitigating via recitation and training-time sentence permutation on SYNTHETIC data; it offers no inference-time mechanistic locus, no test on real-world ordered knowledge, and no steering fix. Your-Context-Is-Not-an-Array (2024) locates in-CONTEXT random-access failure in attention on algorithmic tasks, not parametric ordered knowledge. Serial-position / temporal-bias / SORT / listwise-ranking work (2022-2025) all concern IN-CONTEXT ordering, positional bias, or episodic order recall, not positional access to sequences stored in weights. Structure-Development-in-List-Sorting (2025) studies training-time head specialization on synthetic sorting. Crucially, none isolate ordinal-access-by-position as a distinct parametric-recall failure on real ordered knowledge, none contrast a neighbor-chain vs position-index representation causally, and none show a non-destructive intervention that recovers the Nth element. This is orthogonal to all my prior single-fact retrieval/gating proposals; here the object is the internal representational format of ordered knowledge (chain vs index) and how query type gates access.", "Abstract": "Large language models memorize ordered sequences from pretraining: the order of US presidents, the alphabet, chart rankings, the periodic-table order, a book's chapter order. Prior work shows LLMs can recite memorized content sequentially but struggle to access it randomly, mitigating this only at training time on synthetic data. We propose an inference-time mechanistic account for real ordered knowledge: LLMs store sequences as a neighbor-linked chain accessible by traversal, not as position-indexed slots, so positional queries fail while neighbor queries succeed. We call this ordinal-access failure, and predict it is not a storage failure: the Nth element is internally recoverable even when the model cannot answer 'what is the Nth element'. We test three claims on curated ordered-knowledge sets. (1) Behavioral signature: for sequences the model can fully recite and whose neighbor relations it answers correctly, direct positional queries ('what is the Nth X?') fail at rates far above neighbor queries, and failure grows with position N and with mid-sequence location, above frequency-matched baselines. (2) Chain-vs-index dissociation: a linear probe recovers a neighbor-chain signal (predicting adjacent elements) and, separately, a running-position/index signal; whether a query is answered by chain traversal vs index lookup is decodable, and on positional failures the correct element remains decodable while the position-index signal is weak/absent. (3) Causal recovery: patching a 'running-position' representation from a recitation run into a direct-positional-query run, or injecting a decoded position-index direction, recovers the Nth element with high specificity, whereas fact editing does not, and outperforms a 'recite then answer' re-prompt. Experiments span Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, and Gemma-2-9B, evaluating positional-vs-neighbor accuracy, position dose-response, hidden-element recovery, causal recovery, and specificity.", "Experiments": ["1. Ordered-knowledge dataset: Curate sequences with authoritative ground-truth order from Wikidata/reference sources across types: ordinal succession (US presidents, monarchs, Olympic host cities by year), canonical fixed orders (alphabet, months, periodic-table element order, planets by distance), and ranked lists (chart-topping songs, ranked sports champions by year). For each sequence build three query forms holding the fact fixed: (a) NEIGHBOR ('what comes immediately after Y?' / 'who preceded Z?'), (b) POSITIONAL ('what is the Nth element?' / 'who was the Nth president?'), (c) FULL RECITATION (list the whole sequence). Restrict to sequences the model can fully recite correctly and to single-token-first answers; report coverage.", "2. Positional-vs-neighbor prevalence (core signature): For each sequence, restrict to elements the model recalls correctly under NEIGHBOR queries, then measure POSITIONAL-query accuracy on the same elements. Test whether positional accuracy is significantly lower than neighbor accuracy (paired McNemar; mixed-effects logistic regression with query-form x element), against a null in which query form only adds output noise. Report per-sequence-type breakdowns and effect sizes.", "3. Position dose-response: Test whether positional-query failure grows with N (distance from the sequence start/anchor) and is worse for mid-sequence elements (far from both endpoints, where chain traversal is longest), controlling for element popularity and sequence length. Report accuracy-vs-position curves; predict a U-shaped accuracy (endpoints easy, middle hard), mirroring the traversal-cost account.", "4. Hidden-element test: On positional-query failures, train a linear probe on intermediate residual-stream activations to decode the correct Nth element, using held-out sequences and label-shuffle controls. Metric: probe recovery accuracy vs verbalized accuracy under the positional query; hypothesis: the correct element is decodable at rates far above verbalized accuracy (hidden, not absent), comparable to the neighbor-query run.", "5. Chain-vs-index probe dissociation: Train two probes on the residual stream: a neighbor-chain probe (predict the adjacent element / whether two elements are adjacent) and a running-position/index probe (predict the ordinal position of the current element, e.g. during recitation). Metrics: per-layer AUROC/accuracy for each; via residualization confirm they carry non-redundant information. Test whether the position-index signal is strong during recitation/neighbor success but weak/absent during positional-query failures (the diagnostic mechanistic contrast).", "6. Causal recovery via patching/steering: (a) Patch the running-position representation from a recitation run (where the model implicitly tracks position while listing) into a direct-positional-query failure run at the identified layers/positions and test whether the correct Nth element is produced. (b) Inject a decoded position-index direction (from Exp 5) scaled to N and test recovery. (c) Reverse control: ablate the position-index signal during recitation and test whether the model loses positional access. (d) Content control: confirm the intervention does not change unrelated facts. Metrics: per-element recovery rate; recovery matches the ground-truth Nth element.", "7. Recovery intervention with specificity: Apply the best intervention on held-out positional-query failures with dose control. Metrics: recovery rate; specificity (accuracy on neighbor queries and on already-correct positional queries must not drop; accuracy on 200 unrelated non-sequence facts pre/post); fluency (held-out perplexity). Baselines: a 'recite the sequence, then answer' re-prompt (upper bound / the recitation mitigation from prior work), chain-of-thought counting, ROME editing (expected not to help since the element is stored), and a random-direction control.", "8. Transfer, generality, and source: Test whether a single position-injection recipe generalizes across sequence types (train on presidents, apply to periodic-table order); repeat core analyses (2-6) on Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B; report whether larger models have position-indexed (not just chain) access (flatter position dose-response). Source analysis: regress positional-failure rate on position N, mid-sequence location, element popularity, and sequence recitation-frequency proxy, to test whether failure is driven by traversal cost rather than element obscurity (the core mechanistic claim)."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["Positional-query failure could reflect arithmetic/counting incompetence (the model cannot map N to a step count) rather than a chain-vs-index storage format; the hidden-element probe (Exp 4) and the recitation-patching recovery (Exp 6a) separate storage-format failure from counting failure, and the neighbor-vs-positional contrast on the same elements controls for element knowledge.", "The neighbor-chain and position-index signals may be entangled or the position signal may be genuinely absent (no index representation at all); the causal patching/ablation tests (Exp 6), not probe AUROC alone, are decisive, and a graded result remains informative. A genuine absence of any position-index signal is itself an informative finding about the chain-only format.", "Recitation itself may be imperfect or the model may recite a wrong order, confounding storage verification; we restrict to sequences with verified-correct full recitation and report coverage.", "Position injection could over-correct (produce a plausible but wrong element at the target position) or degrade fluency; the neighbor-query and already-correct specificity controls (Exp 7) are central and utility may be capped.", "Endpoint elements (first/last) may be trivially accessible for reasons unrelated to indexing (salience/frequency), inflating the U-shape; popularity controls and mid-sequence focus mitigate this.", "A single transferable position-injection recipe may not exist if position encoding is sequence-type-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.", "Multi-token elements complicate readout; primary analysis restricts to single-token-first answers with reported coverage.", "Findings on 7-9B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget."]}}
