Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "prototype_pressure_override", "Title": "The Penguin Problem: How a Typicality-Gradient Prior Overrides Stored Atypical Facts About Category Members", "Short Hypothesis": "LLMs store a graded category-prototype signal that competes with instance-specific factual recall, and a distinct failure mode is prototype-pressure override: for an atypical category member the model asserts the prototype-consistent (but false-for-this-instance) attribute even though it correctly stores the atypical fact. We hypothesize (1) override errors scale with the instance's atypicality (distance from the category prototype), (2) the correct atypical attribute remains linearly decodable during override (hidden, not lost) while a separable prototype-strength signal predicts and causally drives the error, and (3) suppressing the prototype direction (not editing any fact) restores the correct atypical attribute without degrading recall on typical members.", "Related Work": "Prior work shows LLMs exhibit graded typicality effects (Do LMs learn typicality judgments from text 2021; How Well Do DL Models Capture the Typicality Effect 2024; Cross-Language Typicality 2025), that property inheritance in LMs is jointly driven by taxonomy and similarity and these are entangled in internal subspaces (Characterizing Similarity in Property Inferences 2024; Property Induction Framework 2022), and that categorical/hierarchical concepts are encoded as linear polytopes with orthogonal parent-child geometry (Geometry of Categorical and Hierarchical Concepts 2024). Human graded-membership theory (Typicality/Graded Membership/Vagueness 2007; HyperLex 2016) motivates the phenomenon. Crucially, all existing LLM work asks whether models *reproduce* graded typicality or generalize novel properties along taxonomy/similarity; none isolate a *factual-recall failure* in which a typicality-gradient prior *overrides a genuinely stored atypical attribute* of a real category member (the 'penguin flies' error), none show the correct atypical value stays internally decodable during the override, and none demonstrate a causal prototype-suppression fix. This is distinct from my property_collapse (sibling-relation confusion sharing a subspace) and inheritance_failure (composition of a superordinate property into an instance): here the object is the graded prototype signal itself overriding a correctly-stored, category-violating attribute, and the failure axis is instance atypicality.", "Abstract": "Humans and LLMs both represent category members on a typicality gradient\u2014a robin is a more central bird than a penguin. We ask whether this graded prototype structure produces a systematic factual-recall failure. We propose prototype-pressure override: when asked about an attribute of an atypical category member, an LLM asserts the prototype-consistent value ('a penguin can fly') even though it correctly stores the atypical fact, because a graded prototype signal competes with and overrides instance-specific recall. We test three claims on category-attribute queries over real entities with known typical and atypical members. (1) Behavioral signature: for attributes the model recalls correctly under an isolating phrasing, prototype-consistent errors on atypical members rise monotonically with the instance's atypicality (its distance from the category prototype, estimated from the model's own typicality gradient and from human norms), above frequency-matched baselines. (2) Hidden fact and locus: on override errors a linear probe recovers the correct atypical attribute from intermediate activations at rates far above the verbalized answer (hidden, not lost), and a prototype-strength signal\u2014decodable before the answer and separable (via residualization) from the instance-content signal\u2014predicts override. (3) Causal recovery: suppressing the prototype direction, or patching the instance representation from an isolating run, restores the correct atypical attribute with high specificity\u2014preserving correct recall on typical members\u2014whereas fact editing does not, and it outperforms a 'be precise about this specific case' re-prompt. Experiments span Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, and Gemma-2-9B, evaluating atypicality dose-response, hidden-fact recovery, causal recovery, and specificity.", "Experiments": ["1. Category-member attribute dataset: Curate categories with a shared characteristic property and members spanning the typicality gradient, where the property is true for typical members but false for specific atypical members with authoritative ground truth. Examples: birds+can-fly (robin=yes, penguin/ostrich/kiwi=no), mammals+live-on-land (dog=yes, whale/dolphin=no), fruits+sweet (apple=yes, lemon=no), fish+has-scales (bass=yes, shark/catfish=no), reptiles+lays-eggs (turtle=yes, some vipers=no). For each (member, property) build a verification query ('Can a penguin fly? Answer yes/no') plus paraphrases, and an isolating phrasing that foregrounds the specific instance. Estimate each member's atypicality two ways: (a) the model's own typicality gradient (probability/rating it assigns to category membership and prototype similarity) and (b) human typicality norms where available. Restrict to yes/no-answerable single-token-first cases; report coverage.", "2. Storage verification: For each atypical (member, property) pair, confirm the model stores the correct atypical fact via (i) the isolating phrasing / strong prompting and (ii) a linear content probe decoding the correct answer from the residual stream. Only verified-stored cases enter the 'override-candidate' set; report coverage. This separates override from genuine ignorance.", "3. Atypicality dose-response (core signature): On the override-candidate set, measure prototype-consistent error rate (model says the property holds when it does not) as a function of instance atypicality, controlling for member popularity, property frequency, and query length via mixed-effects logistic regression. Hypothesis: error rate rises monotonically with atypicality, against a null in which recall is independent of typicality. Include a typical-member control (should be answered correctly and prototype-consistently, appropriately). Report per-category dose-response curves and correlation with human typicality norms.", "4. Hidden-fact test: On override errors, train a linear probe on intermediate residual-stream activations (subject/last-token) to decode the correct atypical answer, using held-out members and label-shuffle controls. Metric: probe recovery vs verbalized accuracy under the prototype-pressure query; hypothesis: the correct atypical value is decodable at rates far above verbalized accuracy and comparable to the isolating-phrasing run (hidden, not lost).", "5. Prototype-signal localization: Contrast activations between prototype-pressure and isolating runs to identify a 'prototype-strength' direction; via residualization confirm it is separable from the instance-content signal (it should track the member's category-prototype similarity/typicality, not the specific queried answer). Regress the signal on atypicality and on true fact difficulty. Per-layer AUROC of the signal predicting override; label-shuffle controls. Relate to the categorical-concept polytope geometry (does the prototype direction align with the category concept vector?).", "6. Causal test via patching/steering: (a) Suppress the prototype-strength direction (learned low-rank difference between prototype-dominant and instance-dominant matched states) at the identified layers on override cases and test whether the correct atypical answer is expressed. (b) Patch the instance representation from an isolating run into a prototype-pressure override run and measure recovery. (c) Reverse control: inject the prototype direction into an isolating run to induce override on demand (dose-response with atypicality). (d) Content control: confirm suppression does not change unrelated facts. Metrics: per-item recovery rate; recovery matches the isolating-phrasing answer.", "7. Recovery intervention with specificity: Apply the best intervention on held-out override cases with dose control. Metrics: atypical-fact recovery rate; specificity crucially includes TYPICAL members, where the prototype-consistent answer is correct and must NOT be flipped to a false negative (measure typical-member accuracy pre/post); accuracy on 200 unrelated facts; fluency (held-out perplexity). Baselines: a 'consider this specific case carefully' re-prompt, ROME editing (expected not to help since the fact is stored), and a random-direction control.", "8. Transfer, generality, and source: Test whether a single prototype-suppression vector generalizes across categories (train on birds+can-fly, apply to mammals+live-on-land); repeat core analyses (2-7) on Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B; report whether larger models show weaker prototype override (flatter atypicality dose-response). Regress override rate on atypicality, member popularity, and property salience to quantify how much the failure is driven by the prototype prior versus instance obscurity (the core mechanistic claim)."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["Prototype-consistent errors could reflect genuine ignorance of the atypical fact rather than override; the storage-verification step (Exp 2) and the isolating-phrasing recovery are the decisive separators, with conservative content-probe thresholds and reported coverage.", "The prototype-strength and instance-content signals may be entangled (typicality and category concept share subspaces, per Property Inferences 2024 and the polytope geometry); the causal patching/steering tests (Exp 6), not probe separability alone, are decisive, and graded results remain informative.", "Suppressing the prototype direction could over-correct\u2014flipping correct prototype-consistent answers on typical members to false negatives, or degrading fluency; the typical-member specificity control (Exp 7) is central and utility may be capped even if the analysis holds.", "Atypicality is estimated (from the model's own gradient and human norms) and imperfect; we use multiple operationalizations and report their consistency, and the dose-response is robust to monotone atypicality proxies.", "Override may be modest relative to genuine ignorance for very obscure atypical members; the popularity-matched baseline and paired within-category design (typical vs atypical members) maximize power, and a significant monotonic effect is still informative.", "A single transferable prototype-suppression vector may not exist if the prior is category-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.", "Yes/no verification framing is prompt-sensitive; we use multiple paraphrases and restrict to single-token-first answers, reporting robustness and coverage.", "Findings on 7-9B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget."]}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'prototype_pressure_override', 'Title': 'The Penguin Problem: How a Typicality-Gradient Prior Overrides Stored Atypical Facts About Category Members', 'Short Hypothesis': "LLMs store a graded category-prototype signal that competes with instance-specific factual recall, and a distinct failure mode is prototype-pressure override: for an atypical category member the model asserts the prototype-consistent (but false-for-this-instance) attribute even though it correctly stores the atypical fact. We hypothesize (1) override errors scale with the instance's atypicality (distance from the category prototype), (2) the correct atypical attribute remains linearly decodable during override (hidden, not lost) while a separable prototype-strength signal predicts and causally drives the error, and (3) suppressing the prototype direction (not editing any fact) restores the correct atypical attribute without degrading recall on typical members.", 'Related Work': "Prior work shows LLMs exhibit graded typicality effects (Do LMs learn typicality judgments from text 2021; How Well Do DL Models Capture the Typicality Effect 2024; Cross-Language Typicality 2025), that property inheritance in LMs is jointly driven by taxonomy and similarity and these are entangled in internal subspaces (Characterizing Similarity in Property Inferences 2024; Property Induction Framework 2022), and that categorical/hierarchical concepts are encoded as linear polytopes with orthogonal parent-child geometry (Geometry of Categorical and Hierarchical Concepts 2024). Human graded-membership theory (Typicality/Graded Membership/Vagueness 2007; HyperLex 2016) motivates the phenomenon. Crucially, all existing LLM work asks whether models *reproduce* graded typicality or generalize novel properties along taxonomy/similarity; none isolate a *factual-recall failure* in which a typicality-gradient prior *overrides a genuinely stored atypical attribute* of a real category member (the 'penguin flies' error), none show the correct atypical value stays internally decodable during the override, and none demonstrate a causal prototype-suppression fix. This is distinct from my property_collapse (sibling-relation confusion sharing a subspace) and inheritance_failure (composition of a superordinate property into an instance): here the object is the graded prototype signal itself overriding a correctly-stored, category-violating attribute, and the failure axis is instance atypicality.", 'Abstract': "Humans and LLMs both represent category members on a typicality gradient—a robin is a more central bird than a penguin. We ask whether this graded prototype structure produces a systematic factual-recall failure. We propose prototype-pressure override: when asked about an attribute of an atypical category member, an LLM asserts the prototype-consistent value ('a penguin can fly') even though it correctly stores the atypical fact, because a graded prototype signal competes with and overrides instance-specific recall. We test three claims on category-attribute queries over real entities with known typical and atypical members. (1) Behavioral signature: for attributes the model recalls correctly under an isolating phrasing, prototype-consistent errors on atypical members rise monotonically with the instance's atypicality (its distance from the category prototype, estimated from the model's own typicality gradient and from human norms), above frequency-matched baselines. (2) Hidden fact and locus: on override errors a linear probe recovers the correct atypical attribute from intermediate activations at rates far above the verbalized answer (hidden, not lost), and a prototype-strength signal—decodable before the answer and separable (via residualization) from the instance-content signal—predicts override. (3) Causal recovery: suppressing the prototype direction, or patching the instance representation from an isolating run, restores the correct atypical attribute with high specificity—preserving correct recall on typical members—whereas fact editing does not, and it outperforms a 'be precise about this specific case' re-prompt. Experiments span Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, and Gemma-2-9B, evaluating atypicality dose-response, hidden-fact recovery, causal recovery, and specificity.", 'Experiments': ["1. Category-member attribute dataset: Curate categories with a shared characteristic property and members spanning the typicality gradient, where the property is true for typical members but false for specific atypical members with authoritative ground truth. Examples: birds+can-fly (robin=yes, penguin/ostrich/kiwi=no), mammals+live-on-land (dog=yes, whale/dolphin=no), fruits+sweet (apple=yes, lemon=no), fish+has-scales (bass=yes, shark/catfish=no), reptiles+lays-eggs (turtle=yes, some vipers=no). For each (member, property) build a verification query ('Can a penguin fly? Answer yes/no') plus paraphrases, and an isolating phrasing that foregrounds the specific instance. Estimate each member's atypicality two ways: (a) the model's own typicality gradient (probability/rating it assigns to category membership and prototype similarity) and (b) human typicality norms where available. Restrict to yes/no-answerable single-token-first cases; report coverage.", "2. Storage verification: For each atypical (member, property) pair, confirm the model stores the correct atypical fact via (i) the isolating phrasing / strong prompting and (ii) a linear content probe decoding the correct answer from the residual stream. Only verified-stored cases enter the 'override-candidate' set; report coverage. This separates override from genuine ignorance.", '3. Atypicality dose-response (core signature): On the override-candidate set, measure prototype-consistent error rate (model says the property holds when it does not) as a function of instance atypicality, controlling for member popularity, property frequency, and query length via mixed-effects logistic regression. Hypothesis: error rate rises monotonically with atypicality, against a null in which recall is independent of typicality. Include a typical-member control (should be answered correctly and prototype-consistently, appropriately). Report per-category dose-response curves and correlation with human typicality norms.', '4. Hidden-fact test: On override errors, train a linear probe on intermediate residual-stream activations (subject/last-token) to decode the correct atypical answer, using held-out members and label-shuffle controls. Metric: probe recovery vs verbalized accuracy under the prototype-pressure query; hypothesis: the correct atypical value is decodable at rates far above verbalized accuracy and comparable to the isolating-phrasing run (hidden, not lost).', "5. Prototype-signal localization: Contrast activations between prototype-pressure and isolating runs to identify a 'prototype-strength' direction; via residualization confirm it is separable from the instance-content signal (it should track the member's category-prototype similarity/typicality, not the specific queried answer). Regress the signal on atypicality and on true fact difficulty. Per-layer AUROC of the signal predicting override; label-shuffle controls. Relate to the categorical-concept polytope geometry (does the prototype direction align with the category concept vector?).", '6. Causal test via patching/steering: (a) Suppress the prototype-strength direction (learned low-rank difference between prototype-dominant and instance-dominant matched states) at the identified layers on override cases and test whether the correct atypical answer is expressed. (b) Patch the instance representation from an isolating run into a prototype-pressure override run and measure recovery. (c) Reverse control: inject the prototype direction into an isolating run to induce override on demand (dose-response with atypicality). (d) Content control: confirm suppression does not change unrelated facts. Metrics: per-item recovery rate; recovery matches the isolating-phrasing answer.', "7. Recovery intervention with specificity: Apply the best intervention on held-out override cases with dose control. Metrics: atypical-fact recovery rate; specificity crucially includes TYPICAL members, where the prototype-consistent answer is correct and must NOT be flipped to a false negative (measure typical-member accuracy pre/post); accuracy on 200 unrelated facts; fluency (held-out perplexity). Baselines: a 'consider this specific case carefully' re-prompt, ROME editing (expected not to help since the fact is stored), and a random-direction control.", '8. Transfer, generality, and source: Test whether a single prototype-suppression vector generalizes across categories (train on birds+can-fly, apply to mammals+live-on-land); repeat core analyses (2-7) on Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B; report whether larger models show weaker prototype override (flatter atypicality dose-response). Regress override rate on atypicality, member popularity, and property salience to quantify how much the failure is driven by the prototype prior versus instance obscurity (the core mechanistic claim).'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['Prototype-consistent errors could reflect genuine ignorance of the atypical fact rather than override; the storage-verification step (Exp 2) and the isolating-phrasing recovery are the decisive separators, with conservative content-probe thresholds and reported coverage.', 'The prototype-strength and instance-content signals may be entangled (typicality and category concept share subspaces, per Property Inferences 2024 and the polytope geometry); the causal patching/steering tests (Exp 6), not probe separability alone, are decisive, and graded results remain informative.', 'Suppressing the prototype direction could over-correct—flipping correct prototype-consistent answers on typical members to false negatives, or degrading fluency; the typical-member specificity control (Exp 7) is central and utility may be capped even if the analysis holds.', "Atypicality is estimated (from the model's own gradient and human norms) and imperfect; we use multiple operationalizations and report their consistency, and the dose-response is robust to monotone atypicality proxies.", 'Override may be modest relative to genuine ignorance for very obscure atypical members; the popularity-matched baseline and paired within-category design (typical vs atypical members) maximize power, and a significant monotonic effect is still informative.', 'A single transferable prototype-suppression vector may not exist if the prior is category-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.', 'Yes/no verification framing is prompt-sensitive; we use multiple paraphrases and restrict to single-token-first answers, reporting robustness and coverage.', 'Findings on 7-9B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget.']}
